elegantly
leisure
appreciating# Concept: Preferred size and the Coasean ceiling
Source: Paper 001, §§2.2, 5.1 (Proposition 1).

Preferred size m*(z*) = the input that gives the largest value over m of average product S(m, z*)/m for the mean of a firm coordination z*. Competence does not enter it: at fixed
composition, better task skills raise output but leave preferred size
unchanged. With human coordination < 1, preferred size is finite — firms
that outgrow it split. This finite preferred size IS the Coasean ceiling in
the model. Proposition 1 bounds almost every active size of the firm between
outer reaches M and M-bar that depend on AI coordination and the human supply
constraint (a firm also cannot be larger than the talent stock allows).
# Concept: Supercooperation threshold and the concentration phase transition
Source: Paper 001, §§2.3, 5.2 (Proposition 3).

Scale returns are governed by gamma, the coordination-driven returns
parameter (gamma = y* in the worked CES cases). Three patterns:
- gamma < 1 (below the line): average product of a large firm vanishes; as the
  market grows (N -> the smallest value) the industry expands by REPLICATING bounded-size
  firms and the market share of any one firm -> 0.
- gamma > 1 (above the line in working together): one large AI firm produces Q at cost of
  order Q^(1/gamma), with 1/gamma < 1 — splitting output across firms loses
  the scale advantage. Equilibrium piles up: a relaxed HHI -> 1, almost
  all output in firm types as large as the market.
- gamma = 1 is the excluded knife-edge.
Crossing the threshold is a PHASE TRANSITION in market structure, driven
by a coordination improvement that may be tiny and benchmark-invisible.
The paper flags the styling: regulation, competing AI providers,
capacity constraints and rigid contracts would likely prevent a literal
one-firm singularity in practice.
# Concept: The size amplifier
Source: Paper 001, §5.2 (Proposition 2).

When AI coordination y*_M rises (still < 1), firm size responds through two
channels: a direct effect (higher mean coordination raises preferred size
at fixed composition, always >= 0) and a composition effect (the firm
re-mixes toward/away from machines, changing mean coordination again).
- If AI coordinates BETTER than humans (y*_H < y*_M < 1): both effects are
  positive — composition AMPLIFIES the direct gain (illustrated machine
  share 0.54 -> 0.83 as firms grow).
- If AI coordinates WORSE (y*_M < y*_H): the composition effect is
  ambiguous and can Soften growth (illustrated machine share 0.38 -> 0.46
  with size gains partly offset).
So the sign of the coordination gap decides whether AI progress compounds
or self-brakes inside the firm.
# Concept: Firm composition patterns — mixed vs separate, human vs AI-only
Source: Paper 001, §5.3 (Proposition 4).

The cost-adjusted best average-product envelope a(lambda, w) — best output
per unit cost at human share lambda — decides who works with whom:
- Machine-only: if AI dominates EVERY point of the human capability hull
  (y_M > all of co(Y_H)) and compute is no costing more than wages (r <= w), all
  firms are machine-only.
- Humans in the loop: if the envelope rises above its machine-only endpoint
  (mosaic intelligence pays) and w <= r, some firms must employ humans.
- Curvature then decides the form: envelope strictly CONVEX in lambda ->
  separate pure-human and pure-AI firms; strictly CONCAVE -> mixed firms,
  all sharing the unique optimal human share.
Same curvature variable returns in displacement dynamics (scroll 1.2.10).
# Concept: Step by step vs sudden displacement
Source: Paper 001, §6.2 (Proposition 5).

Human displacement has two dynamically different forms, selected by the
curvature of the composition envelope a(lambda):
- CONCAVE (mixed-firm regime): displacement is WITHIN-firm. As AI improves,
  firms continuously lower their human share; employment, output and firm
  size move continuously. Full unemployment only when adding a first human
  to an all-machine industry does not pay even at the reservation wage.
- CONVEX (separate-firm regime): displacement is ACROSS-firm. Human-only
  and AI-only firms coexist until a cost inequality flips, then human firms
  are wiped out in jumps — full employment to full unemployment at
  a threshold, wages max{w, r*a(1)/a(0)}.
As AI coordination improves the envelope can itself flip concave -> convex,
so an economy can see step by step erosion first, then a sudden spike.
Task-model form (Proposition 7): for one human type, humans are absent
from every equilibrium exactly when
  max_d (y_d^H / y_d^M) + (y*_H - y*_M) * log(1 + m*(y*_M)) <= w / r
— best relative task productivity PLUS the human coordination advantage
valued at machine-only firm size. A coordination advantage can therefore
cover a task-productivity gap; under constant returns the second
term vanishes and only the task race remains.
# Concept: Wages as bids — shadow value and sorting
Source: Paper 001, §6.1 (Theorem 2), §4.2.

In equilibrium the wage of a worker equals the highest BID for them: firms bid
the marginal of the worker contribution to firm surplus (a bid function of their
full capability profile, evaluated at the size of the firm, mix and scale).
Consequences: (a) pay can rise without the worker changing at all, when
complementary teammates or scale raise their marginal contribution
(coders' pay grew to near three times the size when writers arrived); (b) standalone-weak
coordinator profiles can be among the highest paid, because value of the coordination
scales with the whole team; (c) workers SORT — each type is won by the firm
type whose bid for their particular jagged profile is highest, so sorting
patterns reveal relative strengths vs AI.
Formally (under Assumption 2) the bid is AFFINE in the profile:
b_f(y) = r + beta^f . (y - y_M) — a per-dimension price beta_d for each
task skill and beta_* for coordination. the bid of Every firm plane passes
through the common AI anchor (y_M, r): a worker identical to the AI is
worth exactly the rental of one copy cost. A profile lying in the winning of no firm region falls back to the outside option.
# Concept: Technology effect vs re-organization effect (wage decomposition)
Source: Paper 001, §6.3 (Proposition 6).

Along a smooth path of AI improvement, a wage change breaks into parts as:
  the change in the wage as AI improves = technology effect + re-organization effect.
Technology effect: AI capability changes, holding of the firm that uses it, the
size, machine share and human mix FIXED — the value of the human inside the
existing firm. Re-organization effect: the own of the firm adjustments — change
in size (dm), machine share (ds), and human skill mix — each
times the sensitivity of the bid of the firm to that margin.
Key point: equilibrium maximizes total surplus, not wages, so the envelope
theorem does NOT cancel the re-organization terms — they are first-order and
can REVERSE the technology effect. Worked illustration: as firms grow
(m = 2 -> 118) a human wage of the coordinator first RISES with re-organization,
then falls; holding firm composition fixed it would have fallen
in one direction only. Any wage forecast that holds organizations fixed is, in
this model, simply wrong-signed in some patterns.
# Concept: AI capability patterns
Source: Paper 001, §§2.3 (for showing map), 4.3 (formal definitions).

For showing map (§2.3), in (task competence, coordination) space:
- Minimal AGI: matches/exceeds AVERAGE standalone human competence and
  coordination, beats no best-human outright, below the coordination
  threshold.
- Beyond the best single profile: exceeds every human on both tasks, coordination at or
  below threshold.
- Above the line in working together: crosses the coordination threshold, does not exceed
  the best human on every task.
- ASI: exceeds every human on all tasks AND crosses the threshold.

Formal patterns (§4.3) are stated against the human capability HULL:
- Weak: AI is dominated by the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Minimal AGI: AI lies WITHIN the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Superintelligent: AI dominates the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Beyond the best single profile (formal): y_d^M > max_i y_d^i in every productive domain.
- Above the line in working together (formal): gamma^M = gamma(delta_{y_M}) > 1 — an
  ABSOLUTE property of the scale of the AI tail, NOT a comparison against the
  best human coordinator; the average product of an AI-only firm then grows
  without bound in firm size.
the empirical of the paper read of the present (§7.2): current AI is jagged-
high-competence, below the line-coordination — consistent with leaner
startups, less tall org charts, rising demand for experienced human
managers, and little aggregate employment/market-structure movement.
# Concept: Measurement agenda — what to track instead of benchmark scores
Source: Paper 001, §8 (Conclusion).

If transformation is a coordination event, isolated-task benchmarks are a
misleading gauge of it — in both directions (mosaic human teams can beat a
benchmark-winning model today; a benchmark-invisible coordination gain can
tip an economy tomorrow). the proposed of the paper measurements:
1. Scale curves: how output scales with the number of identical AI copies
   at FIXED compute per copy, alongside isolated competence L — estimate
   competence and coordination separately across model generations.
2. Firm-level scale counted in BOTH workers and rented agentic compute
   (m_f = human rolls + AI instances), and payroll-vs-AI-expenditure shares
   over time.
3. The cross-of a section distribution of firms — AI-only / AI-native vs mixed
   — because within-firm vs across-firm displacement have vastly different
   unemployment dynamics.
4. Employer-employee panels tracking how different skill profiles sort
   across firms — the direct test that separates technology from
   re-organization effects on wages.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 002): a second, independent reason benchmark
scores give the wrong idea — they measure a model+HARNESS bundle (scroll 1.2.15).
Paper 002 shows the harness term alone is worth 35-61% of held-out cost
at fixed weights, and that the bundle can keep improving DURING the
measurement task itself. Any competence estimate that does not fix and
report the harness is mixed up.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 003): a worked example of measuring at the
right level. StoryScope measures DISCOURSE-level choices (scroll 1.2.24)
rather than output scores or surface style — and the level matters:
surface style signals are transient (a model revision or a light edit
wipes out them; fine-tuning to mimic human style drops style-based
detection from 97% to 3%, as cited therein), while structural choices
survive style editing (93.9% macro-F1 after span-level rewriting)
because changing them requires rewriting the construction of the artifact.
Measurement lesson for this agenda: prefer variables that are expensive
for the measured system to fake — the same property Paper 001 wants
from scale curves and sorting panels, and Paper 002 gets from
cost-annotated histories (scroll 1.2.20).

Revision 2026-11-07 (Paper 006): a third way a score leads its reader wrong,
from the specification side. A pass rate is won under a
structure the agent settled on and no one measuring can see:
Paper 006 proves the same test set can leave nearly every
candidate standing under one structure and settle the question
under another (1.2.58), and that quality axes no test can see
(1.2.60) move with the type of specification alone — code
erodes faster under behavioral specification than under a
standing skeleton (1.2.64), at equal pass rates. A score that
does not fix or report the structure measures a bundle — task,
tests, and a hidden structure — just as the Paper 002 revision
showed it measures a bundle of model and harness. Add to the
agenda: report the structure the score was won under, and at
least one measure on an axis tests are blind to.
# Concept: Harness and harness policy
Source: Paper 002, §§1-2. NEW in Paper 002; no counterpart in Paper 001.

Harness: the scaffolding around a language model that (1) assembles the input of the model and (2) executes and observes its actions in an environment
(usually a computing sandbox). Harness POLICY: every programmable rule
governing how the agent uses model, state and environment — tool
implementations and selection, prompting, context assembly and
pressing together, memory and skill indexing and retrieval, service adapters,
task scheduling — AND the strategy for deciding when and how to attempt
further improvements. Policy is encoded in harness code, prompts,
configuration and reusable skills. Paper 001's agents had capability
profiles but no harness layer; this scroll adds the missing layer
between weights and outcomes.
# Concept: Runtime self-improvement — the collapsed optimization loop
Source: Paper 002, §§1, 3.1. NEW in Paper 002.

Classical setup (adaptive control): an external OPTIMIZER observes
execution and updates the controller (harness policy) between runs —
Meta-Harness, GEPA, AlphaEvolve-style loops. It needs representative
benchmark tasks (75% of production teams in one cited study evaluate
without any), pays full task runs per candidate (untenable as
horizons stretch to days/weeks), benchmarks go stale, and the resulting
policy stays FIXED during the work where its gaps appear.
Collapsed loop: the running agent itself chooses both task actions and
policy revisions through the same action interface, during the task,
grounded in the environment it actually works in. Weights never change.
Each successor policy retains the ability to revise itself further,
including revising its improvement strategy — improvement of the
part that improves is in scope, not a meta-level add-on.
# Concept: Optimizer symmetry and being written in the same stuff it works on (enabling properties)
Source: Paper 002, §3.1. NEW in Paper 002.

Two properties of harnesses make the collapsed loop (scroll 1.2.16)
possible where traditional adaptive control keeps the optimizer outside:
- Optimizer symmetry: a policy update can be REALIZED as a sequence of
  ordinary task actions — inspect source, edit files, run tests, request
  activation — using tools the agent already has. Revision is not a
  privileged operation; it is work.
- Being written in the same stuff it works on: the policy being optimized is code, represented as files
  inside the own of the optimizer environment. The object of optimization is
  available as data to the optimizer.
Together: the proposal of the optimizer logic can move inside the harness
because nothing about proposing a revision requires leaving the action
domain or the representation the agent already works on.
# Concept: Policy/mechanism partition (protected mechanism layer)
Source: Paper 002, §§1, 4.1, after Exokernel (Engler et al. 1995). NEW.

The architectural thing needed first for safe self-editing: partition the
harness into (a) a minimal PROTECTED MECHANISM layer — only what the
safety properties need, exposed at the lowest level of abstraction —
and (b) an editable POLICY layer (the 'the worker part') holding everything
else. Protected: canonical execution history (append-only, queryable,
cost-annotated), versioned artifact store (memory, skills, snapshots),
credential storage, sandbox API, and the guardian (scroll 1.2.19).
Editable: all tools, context assembly/pressing together, prompting, memory
indexing/retrieval, adapters, scheduling — and the improvement policy.
Design pressure runs one way: shrink the protected set to the minimum,
maximize what the agent may rewrite. Two failure modes motivate it:
fate-sharing (a faulty edit turns off the agent AND its repair logic) and
state erasure (an edit destroys the experience needed to learn/recover).
# Concept: State and replacement contracts; the guardian; independent rollback
Source: Paper 002, §§3.2, 4.2, App. A. NEW in Paper 002.

State s_t = (d_t durable data, e_t sandbox, pi_t active policy), advanced
only through protected mechanism K. Two contracts:
- Storage contract: execution history is APPEND-ONLY — H(d_t) is a prefix
  of H(d_t+1); records cannot be written over or deleted. Memory and
  artifacts may change current value but must retain prior versions.
- Replacement contract: editing produces an arbitrary candidate pi'
  (possibly crashing); activation requires a GUARDIAN G(s_t, pi') in
  {0,1} that lies OUTSIDE the policy it judges. Default guardian checks
  only build success; richer guardians (LLM-judge inspection, forked-
  environment trials, human approval) are future work in the paper.
Rollback is INDEPENDENT across the three state components (policy,
durable state, sandbox), and history survives rollback — evidence of
failed attempts stays available. Scope limit (App. A): the preservation
proof is conditional on all protected transitions being mediated by K,
and external effects in the world are not that can be run backward by any rollback.
Protection guarantees preservation and recovery authority — explicitly
NOT improved behaviour or continued progress.
# Concept: Internal legibility (cost-annotated history as reward signal)
Source: Paper 002, §§4.3, 7. NEW in Paper 002.

A self-improving agent needs to see itself. Exo exposes execution history
for retrieval and analysis with model-message events annotated by cost,
token counts and cached-token share, plus a reporting tool (spending,
cached input share, repeated tool calls, context composition). Policy
code decides what enters the prompt, but the full annotated record
remains open to looking inside even when compacted out of context. The paper credits
this as a key reason the system works: cost/latency/cache annotations
give DENSE reward signals about efficiency DURING the task, long before
any completion signal arrives. Legibility is also the audit surface:
adaptations are explicit code diffs that operators can inspect, test,
and give the right to — contra continual learning hidden in weights.
# Concept: Specialization cost and spreading of the cost by reuse
Source: Paper 002, §§5.2-5.4. NEW in Paper 002.

Runtime learning has an investment profile: an early SPECIALIZATION COST
(building tools, revising context policy — in the combat run Exo had
spent ~$0.31 at 10 min vs Codex $0.12 / Pi $0.16) buys a lower spend rate
later; cumulative cost crossing points came at 25 min (vs Pi) and 86 min (vs
Codex). Whether the investment pays is governed by REUSE:
- Within-task reuse (long repetitive tasks): pays before completion.
- Across-task transfer (scroll 1.2.23): pays again on repeated objectives.
- Low-reuse work: does NOT pay. On one-shot quests (each completed once,
  distinct steps/items/locations) Exo averaged $0.28 per quest point vs
  Codex $0.24 and Pi $0.21 — the self-improving harness was the EXPENSIVE
  option. Negative result, recorded at full weight: self-editing is a
  bet on recurrence.
# Concept: Intelligence floor for self-improvement
Source: Paper 002, §5.4 ('Limits of adaptation'). NEW in Paper 002.

Access to self-editing helps only if the underlying model can discover
and reuse effective changes. Control experiment: Exo on AppWorld with
gpt-5-mini — three seeds made limited harness edits and did not reuse
their created tools on held-out tasks; total cost and pass rates were
statistically a wash ($6.63 / 59% vs control $6.73 / 55%). The authors'
statement: 'there is an intelligence floor necessary to reap the
benefits of Of Exo architecture.' The architecture is an amplifier with a
threshold, not a substitute for capability — compare Paper 001's
coordination THRESHOLD (scroll 1.2.7): in both papers the interesting
action is at a critical value of the variable the paper is about.
# Concept: Transfer of learned policy (specialization as portable artifact)
Source: Paper 002, §§5.3-5.4. NEW in Paper 002.

Because adaptations are code, they PERSIST and TRAVEL: a harness
specialized on one run can be carried to a fresh instance of the task
(fresh game account, retained agent state) and pays immediately — the
transfer results are the largest in the paper (45-82% below Codex/Pi;
combat $1.92 vs $6.87/$10.89). Two added conditions from the paper itself:
(1) learned procedures DIFFER across seeds — seeds 1-2 built broadly
reusable tools, seed 3 built many scenario-specific tools and spent more
in learning, yet all three won on held-out cost; specialization does not
converge to a single implementation. (2) Transfer shown is to REPEATED
objectives under changed conditions, not to novel task families.
Relation to Paper 001 scroll 1.2.5: a learned harness is reproducible
like AI expertise generally — but unlike a weight profile, it is also
open to looking inside, that can be forked and that can be owned as an artifact.

Delta-link 2026-11-06 (Paper 004): a SECOND transfer mechanism, unlike
the artifact how well a thing carries above. Paper 004 transfers capability to
unseen tasks with no artifact carried over: a shared pool of expert
networks persists, and a gating network routes each new input to the
experts trained on similar SUBSTRUCTURES (scroll 1.2.32). What travels
is not a learned policy but a routing decision. The two mechanisms make
different bets: artifact transfer assumes the whole specialization is
reusable; substructure routing assumes only parts are, and pays a
gating network to find out which.
# Concept: Discourse-level narrative features vs surface style
Source: Paper 003, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 003.

A generated artifact can be measured at (at least) two levels. SURFACE
STYLE: lexical and syntactic choices — word choice, sentence rhythm,
signature phrases (em-dashes, 'delve'). DISCOURSE / NARRATIVE level:
structural decisions about how the artifact is constructed — plot
architecture, character agency, temporal ordering, how and when
information is revealed, how explicitly themes are stated.

Paper 003's finding: the discourse level carries nearly all the
who wrote a thing signal of the surface level (narrative-only features reach
93.2% macro-F1 on human-vs-AI detection, >=97% of the combined model)
while being far more DURABLE: style cues are erased by model revisions
or light edits, but narrative choices survive span-level style rewriting
(93.9% after editing) because changing them means restructuring the
work, not putting in new words it. The two levels also fail differently: style
identifies THAT a model wrote something; discourse features say HOW
the source constructs — that can be read with sense, per-decision.
# Concept: Narrative space, AI convergence, human dispersion
Source: Paper 003, §§4-5. NEW in Paper 003.

Embed each story as a vector in an induced narrative feature space
(scroll 1.2.28) and the population geometry becomes measurable:
- The five AI sources (Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, GPT, Kimi) cluster in
  one shared region; the six most-confused attribution pairs are all
  AI<->AI.
- Human stories occupy a DISTINCT region (closest human-AI centroid
  pair farther than the most distant AI-AI pair) and are more DISPERSED
  within it: mean distance to own centroid 33.2 vs 27.4 for AI (+22%),
  median 10-NN radius 1.13x larger.
Convergence here is a population property, not a per-model defect: five
independently trained systems from five labs make systematically
SIMILAR narrative choices (thematic over-being stated out loud, causal being tidy,
temporal straight line behavior, bodily/sensory emotion rendering, vague a pointing at other works without naming them),
while human choices spread wider on the same axes. Relation to scroll
1.2.4 recorded in the 2026 of that scroll-11-06 revision.
# Concept: Core features vs fingerprint features
Source: Paper 003, §§2.2, 4-5. NEW in Paper 003.

A discriminating feature space splits by SCOPE of its signal:
- CORE features (30 of 304): stable, important markers of the
  human-vs-AI boundary that hold across ALL five AI models — signed
  human-leaning or AI-leaning (e.g. thematic being stated out loud and
  of the narrator stating the moral, AI-leaning; chronological discontinuity and
  explicit named between texts reference, human-leaning). 30 core
  features alone reach 84.8% macro-F1 (~91% of the full narrative model).
- FINGERPRINT features (75): signal concentrated in ONE source — the
  odd habits that make six-way attribution possible (68.4% macro-F1
  narrative-only). As reported: Claude = flat event escalation, uniform
  voice, ending chapters, full of respect toward convention; GPT = gossip/rumor as
  plot mechanism, retrospective framing, subverted expectations;
  DeepSeek = front-loaded context; Gemini = most tidy endings, extended
  final endings, most bleak settings; Kimi = fewest fingerprints, sits at
  the generic center of the AI distribution.
General shape worth keeping: a class boundary usually lives in a compact
core; identity within the class lives in a longer tail of fingerprints.
# Concept: Rarity as an originality proxy
Source: Paper 003, §§1, 4-5, App. H. NEW in Paper 003.

Originality is turned into a working measure as STATISTICAL RARITY in a feature space
of decisions: a work is 'rare' by its mean distance to its 25 nearest
neighbors among comparable works (here, stories written to the same
prompts). The legal/theoretical anchor the paper cites: minimal of the copyright-originality standard and Torrance's notion of originality as
being uncommon of response.

Measurements (as reported): human stories' mean rarity percentile 0.71
vs AI 0.49 (Cohen's d = 0.83); humans hold 24.7% of the top-10% rarest
stories vs 7.1% AI (top 1%: 3.0% vs 0.6%); at the prompt level the human
version is the rarest of the six 57.8% of the time (chance: 16.7%).

Watch the limits of the proxy: rarity is relative to the corpus and feature
space chosen; it measures being uncommon of choices, not their quality or
intent; and any rarity metric that becomes a target invites gaming
(Goodhart exposure — see open question Q11).
# Concept: Induced that can be read with sense feature space (comparative induction on a parallel corpus)
Source: Paper 003, §§2, 2.1-2.2. NEW in Paper 003. A METHOD concept —
the first in this model that is about how to build a measuring instrument.

Recipe, as executed:
1. PARALLEL CORPUS: hold the task constant and vary the source —
   10,272 prompts reverse-engineered from human stories, each written
   by the human and five LLMs: 61,608 stories, ~5,000 words each.
   Same-prompt comparison is what makes 'choice' separable from 'topic'.
2. STRUCTURED TEMPLATES: convert each artifact into a structured
   representation along a theory-grounded taxonomy (10 of NarraBench's
   12 narrative dimensions), forcing downstream reasoning over content,
   not surface.
3. CROSS-SOURCE COMPARISON: an LLM judge compares the six versions of
   each prompt in a 600-story discovery pool and records where sources
   systematically diverge (~2.7M tokens compressed to ~686K).
4. FEATURE DISCOVERY + Merging of copies: per-dimension expert prompts propose
   closed-form questions (categorical / ordered / scale / binary /
   multi-select); 408 candidates joined together over 3 runs, embedding-clustered
   (cosine >= 0.85) down to 304 features.
5. ASSIGNMENT AT SCALE: a cheaper model scores every story on every
   feature (Gemini 3 Flash; being the same on a second run Krippendorff alpha = 0.90;
   human validation Cohen's kappa = 0.84 on 240 items).
6. ROLE ASSIGNMENT: bootstrap SHAP stability analysis separates core
   from fingerprint features (scroll 1.2.26).
Total corpus turning into features cost as reported: $4.4k. The transferable
idea: when the interesting variable is a latent CHOICE, don't annotate
it directly — build a mirrored corpus, compress comparisons, and let
the feature space be INDUCED from observed divergence, then validate.
# Concept: Mixture of experts — gating as learned routing over specialists
Source: Paper 004, §§1, 3. NEW in Paper 004 (the architecture is from
Jacobs et al. 1991 and Shazeer et al. 2017; the concept as used here is
Paper 004's deployment of it).

Replace a monolithic network block with several parallel EXPERT
networks plus a GATING network. For each input, the gate computes
weights over the experts (softmax; the weights read as attention), and
the output is the weighted sum of expert outputs — DENSE routing in
Paper 004 (all experts contribute; sparse top-k routing trades
interpretability and stability for compute). Because the gate keys on
the representation of the input, similar inputs are routed to similar
experts, and experts specialize on the regions of representation space
they are routed: specialization is an EMERGENT outcome of routing plus
training, not an assignment. Requirements noted by the paper: experts
sharing a mixture must share input/output shapes (in their graph network, MoE is
applied at the prediction Prediction network, not inside message passing, so experts
share structure); a LINEAR gate worked best in their pre-exploration.

Revision (Paper 007, 2026-11-07): routing has a second continent.
In attention, what is routed over is not specialists but the keys
of the cache, and the routed set grows with the context — so the
choosing itself becomes the cost, and ZHA answers by giving the
choosing an index of its own (1.2.67): selection stacked on
selection. The difference is recorded, not smoothed over: an
expert is a piece of the model, picked per token; a key is a
piece of the past, picked per query; the gate here is trained
with the model, while the reference queries of the tree are
distilled afterward into a frozen one (1.2.69). What carries
across is the shape — a cheap choosing step in front of expensive
capacity — and the failure mode that comes when the cost of the
choosing itself is ignored.
# Concept: Hierarchical (multi-granularity) mixture of experts
Source: Paper 004, §§1, 3-4. NEW in Paper 004.

One mixture is not enough when the object has natural structure at
several scales. Paper 004's object is the control of a program/data-flow
graph, with three natural granularities: NODE (instructions/variables),
BASIC BLOCK (pseudo nodes for code blocks — the level pragma instructions modify),
and GRAPH (the whole kernel). A LOW-LEVEL MoE is built per granularity
(node MoE on post-Prediction network node representations, block MoE on pseudo nodes,
graph MoE on the gathered graph representation). A HIGH-LEVEL MoE then
gates over the three granularity-models' outputs — a mixture of
mixtures, whose gate reads the hidden representation (design 2, better
than gating on raw input features). Two findings carry the concept:
(a) which granularity matters is INPUT-DEPENDENT — different kernels
favor different granularities, and the high-level gate learns to assign
the highest weight to the empirically best granularity per kernel;
(b) stacking the three granularities' MoE models inside ONE flat model is
WORSE than even a single-granularity model (Table 4) — the hierarchy
itself, keeping the mixture of each scale separate and adding up late, is
what trains in a stable way and generalizes. Late aggregation over separately
specialized scales beats early fusion.
# Concept: Expert polarization (mode collapse of a mixture) and its steps taken against it
Source: Paper 004, §§3-4. NEW in Paper 004.

The intrinsic failure mode of a trained mixture: random initialization
gives some experts a head start; the gate learns to weight better
experts higher; better-weighted experts get more training; the gap
compounds until the gate gives nearly everything to ONE expert and
the rest starve — POLARIZATION. The mixture collapses to a single
network, carrying dead capacity, and the generalization the mixture was
built for disappears. Paper 004 shows the collapse concretely at the
high level: naive joint training gives nearly all weight to the
graph-MoE model.
Steps taken against it, both needed (taking one part away to test it Table 6: removing either costs
loss):
1. LOAD-BALANCING PRESSURE: a balance penalty on the coefficient of
   variation of total weights assigned per expert (weight 5e-3 here),
   punishing one sided use.
2. TWO-STAGE TRAINING: stage 1 (warm up) trains each expert model
   SEPARATELY, so every expert is competent before any gating exists;
   stage 2 alternates joint and separate training; the high-level gate
   is initialized to uniform weights. Polarization is a rich-get-richer
   dynamic — the fix is to make experts viable BEFORE competition and
   keep rehearsing them separately during it.
General shape: any routed-specialist system has a collapse gradient;
diversity of the specialist pool is a maintained state, not a default
(cf. Paper 003's convergence at population level, scroll 1.2.25).
# Concept: Domain generalization via shared substructures (routing, not invariance)
Source: Paper 004, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 004.

Problem: a model trained on some domains (here: each program kernel is
a domain) must perform on UNSEEN domains whose difference from the
training domains is large — and INFORMATIVE (the differences carry
signal for the prediction, so erasing them is harmful). The standard
domain-generalization playbook (adversarial alignment, invariant
representations) is therefore REJECTED by the paper: do not force the
domains into one distribution.
The alternative: assume domains share SUBSTRUCTURES even when they
differ wholesale. Learn specialists on substructures (MoE experts over
nodes/blocks/graph patterns) and a router (scroll 1.2.29); a new domain
is handled by composing the specialists its substructures resemble.
Generalization becomes a retrieval-and-composition problem rather than
an invariance problem. In Paper 004 this is what lets a predictor
trained on 42 benchmark kernels transfer to unseen and more complex
kernels with only 50 fine-tuning points per target kernel.
Evaluation protocol worth keeping: leave-one-domain-out — train on
source kernels, test on held-out target kernels; report both offline
prediction error (total mean square error over objectives) and online utility (the
quality of designs the search of the predictor actually finds).
# Concept: Surrogate prediction for expensive design spaces
Source: Paper 004, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 004. The task pattern the machinery of the paper serves.

When evaluating one candidate design costs minutes-to-hours (an high level synthesis
run), exhaustive or even generous search is impossible — so train a
SURROGATE that predicts the expensive quantity (latency, resource use,
validity) from the representation of the design, search cheaply in
prediction space (here: DFS over up to 75,000 pragma designs), and
spend the real evaluation budget only on the predicted top-M (here:
top 10) for verification. the errors of the surrogate are therefore not
uniform in cost: what matters is ranking quality near the optimum and
validity classification at the constraint boundary, which is why the
paper reports ONLINE speedup (best verified design vs best in dataset)
alongside offline mean square error. The binding constraint that shapes everything
else in the paper: labels are scarce BECAUSE they are expensive, so
the surrogate must generalize from few, costly examples (scroll 1.2.32).
# Concept: Fake quadrics — same rational invariants, different manifold
Source: Paper 005, §1. NEW in Paper 005; first concept of the topology
annex (cluster E). No relation to Papers 001-004 beyond meta-level.

A QUADRIC here is S^2 x S^2 (the product of two spheres, a closed
symplectic 4-manifold). A FAKE QUADRIC is a closed symplectic
4-manifold with the SAME rational cohomology ring as S^2 x S^2 —
rationally indistinguishable from it — that is nevertheless not
diffeomorphic (not even homeomorphic) to it: the difference lives in
more fine invariants, here finite nontrivial first homology where the
genuine quadric has H_1 = 0. Akhmedov's families (built in his earlier
work with Baldridge [ref 6 in the paper] by symplectic sums of ruled
surfaces along cyclic multisections, scroll 1.2.35) give INFINITE
families of fake quadrics, distinguished from each other by the order
of the finite group H_1. No complex structure is assumed or claimed:
'fake' is a statement about invariants, not about almost-complex
geometry. The general lesson the concept carries: the identity of an object
is relative to the invariant granularity you measure at — rational
equality is not equality (see also scroll 1.2.38).

REVISION, Paper 008 (2026-11-09): the genuine quadric of this
scroll — S^2 x S^2 — is Q^2 in the flag family of Paper 008
(1.2.102), and it is the site of the first fall of the strong
Arnold conjecture: the map of Jiao on S^2 x S^2 has two fixed points
where the conjecture asked for at least Crit = 3 (Paper 008,
its ref 29). The manifold this scroll holds up as the genuine
article turns out to carry, for a different count, the case
that ended a sixty-year claim. Its own Arnold number is 2,
known whole (1.2.103). Nothing in the claim of this scroll
changes: the fake quadrics still differ from it in whole
numbers, exactly as stated.
# Concept: Cyclic (braided) multisection of a ruled surface
Source: Paper 005, §§1-2, Fig. 1. NEW in Paper 005.

Setting: Sigma_g x S^2, the product of a genus-g surface (g >= 2) with
a sphere — a RULED surface. Fix a map u : Sigma_g -> S^1 (the middle of the sphere circle) whose induced map on pi_1 is onto every target. The CYCLIC
MULTISECTION of degree p >= 2 is the surface
  F_{g,p} = { (x, z) in Sigma_g x S^1 : z^p = u(x) }.
Properties (as stated in the paper): the projection F_{g,p} -> Sigma_g
is a connected, With no merging of sheets p-fold cover (being connected uses
being onto of u_* mod p); F_{g,p} is symplectic for the product
form, has homology class p[S] (S = Sigma_g x {pt}), self-intersection
zero, and genus G = 1 + p(g-1). 'BRAIDED': travelling around a base
loop in a cycle cycles the p sheets (the Figure-1 model, g=2 p=3,
shows the permutation 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1 around a loop of the base) —
the sheets are braided together rather than p separate copies, which
is exactly what makes the cover connected. The name of the construction of the paper: sums of such braided pieces across a sphere seam, the
ring shaped joins carrying no extra braid at the seam itself.
# Concept: Multisection complement and its handlebody decomposition
Source: Paper 005, §§1-2, App. A, Theorem 1.1/2.2. NEW in Paper 005.

The COMPLEMENT M_{g,p} = (Sigma_g x S^2) minus an open tubular
neighborhood of F_{g,p} is the working piece from which fake quadrics
are glued (scroll 1.2.37). Structurally it is a bundle over Sigma_g
with fiber P_p, a sphere with p open disks removed. Paper 005's first
contribution is EXPLICIT HANDLEBODY Recipes in parts of the three
complements the sums need, with 2-handle framings induced by the
product coordinates — handle counts (h_0, h_1, h_2, h_3):
  M_{2,3}: (2, 11, 14, 3)    M_{3,2}: (2, 14, 14, 2)
  M_{2,4}: (2, 12, 18, 4)
A handlebody decomposition turns the complement from an existence
statement into a computable object: its handle chain complex yields
integral homology directly (scroll 1.2.38), its attaching maps are the
data the gluing must match, and (in the announced follow-up with
Akbulut and Yildiz) the closed sums' full diagrams are to be assembled
from these pieces. Proof detail certified in App. D: the pole
projections used to read off framings.
# Concept: Symplectic sum along a surface, and gluing data as real data
Source: Paper 005, §§1, 3-4, Theorem 1.1, App. B. NEW in Paper 005.

The SYMPLECTIC SUM: take two symplectic 4-manifolds containing the
same surface with opposite normal bundles, remove tubular
neighborhoods of the surface, and glue the resulting boundaries by an
orientation-reversing diffeomorphism that flips the normal circle:
phi_m(w, e^{i theta}) = (f_m(w), e^{-i theta}) for w in the genus-G
gluing surface. Gompf's operation; here the pieces are multisection
complements (1.2.36) and the sums are Akhmedov's fake quadrics
(1.2.34). Degree pairs with Euler characteristic 4 and signature 0
from the prior classification: (2,3), (2,4), (3,3); Paper 005 works
the (3,3) and (2,4) families in product ruled surfaces.
The conceptual payload is about DATA vs SUMMARY: Akhmedov-Yeung had
computed the gluing MATRICES on H_1 that the sums require. Paper 005
insists — and this is stated verbatim in its introduction — that 'the
matrices alone do not determine the smooth sums': the actual gluing
requires MARKED SURFACE DIFFEOMORPHISMS f_m realizing those matrices,
with specified boundary choices of plain coordinates (here: no surface-dependent
normal-circle twist). Same matrix, different marking, potentially
different manifold. The invariant summary is a shadow of the gluing
data, not a substitute for it.
# Concept: Integral homology and torsion as the distinguishing invariants
Source: Paper 005, §§2.3, 3.3, 4, Cors 2.4/5.3, App. C. NEW in
Paper 005.

Where Paper 005's computations land. For the complement (Theorem 2.3):
  H_2(M_{g,p}; Z) ~= Z^{1 + 2(g-1)(p-1)} ⊕ Z/p,
and the TORSION subgroup Z/p is generated geometrically (Cor 2.4) by
the difference of the two pole sections, [S_S] - [S_N] — a class with
a picture, not just a chain-complex artifact. For the closed sums
(Theorem 1.1, the integral gluing calculation of §3.3 carried out in §4):
  H_1(Z_m^{3,3}) ~= (Z/3)^2 ⊕ Z/(3m),   H_1(Z_m^{2,4}) ~= (Z/2)^2 ⊕ Z/(4m)
for every twist parameter m >= 1 — recovering, by explicit geometric
realization, the formulas Akhmedov-Yeung had obtained from
Mayer-Vietoris presentations. Full integral homology of the sums
(Cor 5.3): H_0 = H_4 = Z, H_1 = T (the finite group above), H_2 ~=
Z^2 ⊕ T, H_3 = 0; Euler characteristic 4, signature 0 throughout.
The distinguishing logic: rationally all these manifolds are S^2 x S^2
(H_1 vanishes over Q); In whole numbers they are two by two separated by
|H_1| = 27m (in that order 16m) — an infinite family read off one torsion
order. Torsion — the part of homology that the move to fractions deletes —
is precisely where the identity of these manifolds lives.
# Concept: Aspherical sums and automatic symplectic being minimal
Source: Paper 005, §5, Prop 5.1, Cor 5.2. NEW in Paper 005.

Being aspherical: a space is aspherical if its universal cover is
contractible (all higher homotopy groups vanish). Prop 5.1: gluing
two multisection complements by ANY orientation-reversing boundary
diffeomorphism yields an aspherical closed manifold with infinite
fundamental group. Proof shape: each complement has an aspherical
cyclic cover (Sigma_G x P_p, both factors aspherical) with one to one
boundary pi_1, so pi_1 of the sum is an AMALGAMATED PRODUCT over
pi_1(Sigma_G) x Z; the universal cover is then a tree of contractible
pieces over the Bass-Serre tree, and a sphere-mapping argument over
finite subtrees kills all higher homotopy.
Being minimal, for free (Cor 5.2): a symplectic manifold is minimal if
it contains no embedded sphere of self-intersection -1 (no
exceptional sphere to blow down). In an aspherical manifold every
sphere map is shrunk to nothing, so every embedded sphere has square 0 —
a (-1)-sphere cannot exist. Aspherical therefore implies
symplectically minimal in one line. The fake quadrics of Theorem 1.1 are
thus aspherical, minimal, rationally S^2 x S^2, and in whole numbers an
infinite family — all four properties from one construction.
# Concept: Propositions as types — proofs are programs
Source: Study S001, Ch.1 and Ch.4. NEW in Study S001.

A proposition is a type; a proof of it is a term of that type. 't has
type A' and 't proves A' are one claim read two ways (the Curry-Howard
correspondence). Saying 'for all' is a function type whose result may
depend on the input (the Pi type); saying 'if, then' is a plain
function type. A logic is then a reading of a type system: the rules
of the logic and the typing rules pair off, rule for rule (the book
proves the pairing in full, in its Appendix A). What this buys:
checking a proof is the same job as type checking a program, so one
small checker serves both.
# Concept: The proof kernel — one small trusted checker
Source: Study S001, Ch.1, Ch.5, Ch.10, Appendix E. NEW in Study S001.

The kernel is the part of a proof system that checks finished proof
terms against the typing rules. Everything else — tactics, notation,
automation, even a language model proposing steps — only PRODUCES
terms; if the kernel is right, a bug anywhere else can fail to prove
a true thing but can never make the system accept a false one. So
the whole trust of the system is priced into one small part, and the
part is kept small enough to read: in MiniLean4 the kernel is 1,398
lines of Rust with no outside code at all (measured from the open
code, 2026-11-06). Kinship: the guardian of Paper 002 (1.2.19) plays
this role for a running system — a small checker at the gate, all
else untrusted. The difference: the guardian guards state changes;
the kernel guards claims.
# Concept: Typing rules and the judgment
Source: Study S001, Ch.3 and Ch.5. NEW in Study S001.

A judgment is a claim of the form: under this context (an ordered
list of named givens, each with its type), this term has this type.
Eight rules define every judgment: two for contexts (the empty
context; add a name whose type checks), and six for terms (a universe
lives in the next universe; a name has its given type; a Pi type
lands where the imax function puts it (1.2.46); a lambda has the Pi
type of its body; an application puts the input into the result
type in place of the bound name; and conversion — a term may be
read at any type equal to its own by computation (1.2.43)). Seven
of the rules are fixed by the shape of the term; conversion is not,
so the checking algorithm splits in two — Infer computes a type by
shape, Check compares — and conversion is used in exactly one
place. That split is what makes the rules a program.
# Concept: The two equalities — by computation, and as a claim
Source: Study S001, Ch.9 and Ch.12. NEW in Study S001.

Two things are equal in two unlike ways. The first way is by
computation: their terms compute to the same still form (1.3.75) —
the checker decides it by running the computation steps (beta: a
function applied steps into its body with the input put in; delta:
a defined name opens up; iota: a recursor (1.2.44) runs on a built
value). The second way is as a claim: 'a equals b' is itself a
type, Eq, and a proof of it is a term that may need real work
(induction, 1.2.44). Eq is not built in: it is an inductive type
like any other, with one way to build it (refl, when the two sides
are already the same by computation) — and because it has that
shape, its recursor carries a fact about a to a fact about b:
transport, which is how rewriting by an equation works. The example
of the book: n + 0 = n holds by computation (the definition of +
runs on its second input); 0 + n = n does not, and needs induction.
# Concept: Inductive types and recursors
Source: Study S001, Ch.7 and Ch.8. NEW in Study S001.

An inductive type is declared by its ways of being built (its
constructors): Nat is built by zero, or by succ of a Nat. For each
such type the system generates one recursor: a function that takes
a motive (what is to be shown or built for each value), one small
case per constructor, and the value itself — and serves both
recursion (computing a value) and induction (proving a claim) with
the same shape; the case for a built value carries the answers for
its parts (the induction steps for the parts). MiniLean4 gives the
general recipe for any single inductive type (parameters, indices,
many constructors) and the checks a declaration must pass: the
result of each constructor is the type itself, applied to its
parameters in order and to index terms; fields pass strict
positivity (1.2.45); the floors of the fields fit the floor of the
type (1.2.46); and a type living in Sort 0 gives its recursor to
all floors only when it has at most one constructor and every field
is a proof or an index — the rule that lets the recursor of Eq
work everywhere and the recursor of Or only inside propositions.
# Concept: Strict positivity — a type may not eat itself
Source: Study S001, Ch.8. NEW in Study S001.

In a declaration, a field of a constructor may use the type being
declared only in a safe place: either not at all, or as the result
of a function type whose inputs do not use the type. A field that
is a function FROM the type — the type in a negative place — is
refused. The refusal is what keeps out a declaration like 'Bad,
built from a function Bad -> False': that field would let a value
be fed to itself as an input, and the system would prove false
things (the self-eating shape of 1.3.76, one level down). The
check is small (80 lines, measured) because the rule is stated on
the shape of the field types alone.
# Concept: Universes and imax — every type lives one floor up
Source: Study S001, Ch.13. NEW in Study S001.

Sort 0 (propositions) is itself a type, so it must live somewhere:
Sort 0 : Sort 1 : Sort 2, one floor up each time, no floor holding
itself — a single self-holding universe ('Type : Type') proves
every claim (the paradox of Girard, in the form of Hurkens;
1.3.76). A function type takes its floor from the imax function:
if its result is a proposition (floor 0), the whole is a
proposition, no matter what it ranges over — floor 0 is
impredicative, the one floor allowed to range over itself, safe
because proofs there can not be looked inside for data (kin of the
rule of 1.2.44 for when the recursor of a proposition may work at
every floor). Any other result puts the function type at the higher
of the two floors. Real Lean shares this design; MiniLean4 differs
in two stated ways: floors are plain numbers here (one statement
is not made for all floors at once), and proofs are not made equal
by computation.
# Concept: Consistency — the empty claim stays empty
Source: Study S001, Ch.14 and Appendix D. NEW in Study S001.

A logic is consistent when the claim 'for every proposition P, P'
has no closed proof term: a term of that type would prove anything
at all. The book proves it for the core system (the eight rules
with universes, without inductive types) in three moves:
computation keeps types (subject reduction); every well typed term
stops computing (the hard step, taken from the work of others and
named as such); so a closed proof of the empty claim would have to
sit still as a still term, and the shapes a still term can take
leave it nowhere to sit (under the single given P, nothing still
has type P). The honesty of the study is part of the record: the
proof does NOT cover the shipped system with inductive types; for
that the book points to the known results for the calculus of
inductive constructions (Werner 1994). The guarantee of a model is
worth only what its stated scope is — stated here in the canonical
scroll, not in the fine print.
# Concept: Verification by translation — prove the model, and the translator is trusted
Source: Study S002, README and documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022. NEW in Study S002.

To prove a Rust program, Aeneas first carries it into a proof
assistant as a pure functional program (1.2.49, 1.2.50), and the
proofs are proved about THAT. So the claim 'the Rust is right'
stands on a chain, and the study records the links plainly: the
DESIGN of the carrying is proved in papers (the functional
semantics of LLBC, ICFP 2022; LLBC itself proved a sound view of
a heap-and-addresses model of running, and its symbolic semantics
proved a sound borrow checker, ICFP 2024). The CODE that does the
carrying — Charon at the front, Aeneas itself (77,012 lines of
OCaml, measured) — is not verified. The hand-written models of
outside code (the standard library and the rest) are trusted as
written. And the floor is the kernel of the proof assistant the
proofs land in (1.2.41). Verification by translation therefore
moves trust; it does not delete it. Its ledger is this scroll.
# Concept: Backward functions — a borrow handed back is a value plus a put-back
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022. NEW in Study S002.

A shared borrow in Rust becomes, in the translation, the plain
value itself; a mutable borrow becomes the value too — the rule
of Rust itself (1.3.81) is what makes this sound: while a mutable
borrow lives, it is the only road to the value, so the value can
travel in its place. The hard case is a function that RETURNS a
mutable borrow: the one who borrowed goes on editing, and at the
end of the borrow the final value must flow back to the owner.
Aeneas gives such a function two outputs: the borrowed value, and
a BACKWARD function — one more pure function that takes the final
value and hands back the updated owners. (The likeness the
documentation itself gives: a lens — the value is its get, the
backward function its put.) Each function is carried on its own,
from its signature alone; a call is met by the pair, never by
opening the body.
# Concept: LLBC — a borrow calculus with no memory in it
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022 and 2024. NEW in Study S002.

LLBC (Low-Level Borrow Calculus) is the in-between language of
the toolchain: Charon reads the Rust in the form the Rust
compiler holds it (MIR) and writes LLBC; Aeneas reads LLBC. LLBC
is built to be given a PURE meaning: its semantics is value
based — there is no memory in it, no addresses, no arithmetic on
pointers — and ownership based: a borrow is sound when the LOANS
it stands on are honored, checked as a fact about values in the
semantics, not as a rule about lifetimes in the text. The 2024
paper proves the two things that make this more than a hope:
LLBC is a correct view of a low-level model that does have a heap
and addresses, and running LLBC in the symbolic way (1.2.52)
checks borrowing — a program that passes it does not get stuck
when run on the heap model.
# Concept: Failure as a value — ok, fail, div
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo. NEW in Study S002.

Every function the translation makes returns a Result with three
shapes: ok (a value), fail (the run failed: a panic, a sum that
flows over its whole numbers, a read outside a list, a division
by zero), and div (the run never ends). Nothing is hidden under
the floor: to prove a Rust function right you must prove its
translation never answers fail on the inputs you claim, and
either never answers div or is marked as allowed to. A claim
about a function is written as: the function answers ok with x,
and the fact P holds of x — and the checker of the proof
assistant is walked through such claims call by call by a tactic
the toolchain ships for the shape (step).
# Concept: Symbolic execution as a borrow checker
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2024. NEW in Study S002.

The translation is made by RUNNING the LLBC, but on stand-ins:
values are symbols, a borrow is a loan written in a symbolic
state, and where calls or the two arms of a choice meet, the
state is gathered into ABSTRACTIONS — bundles of loans from
which the backward functions (1.2.49) are computed. Loops are the
hard meeting place; the 2024 paper adds the JOIN, a way of
merging two symbolic states that keeps the abstraction and the
borrow facts, proved to keep both — and it is the join that let
loops into the toolchain. In the code that comes out, a loop is
either a small helper function that calls itself, or a loop
combinator fed a body that answers 'go on' or 'done'; that it
stops is proved in the backend, or the function is kept partial
and honest about it.
# Concept: The verified kernel and its untrusted shell, at framework scale
Source: Study S003, the manual, roadmap and design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

h5i-app is a web framework with the split of 1.2.41 built in: the
holder of the application logic is a small KERNEL written in
Rust, and everything around it — the web server, the database,
the decoding of what comes in — is the SHELL, trusted with
nothing. The kernel is carried into Lean 4 (by Charon and Aeneas,
1.2.48, driven as tools under the command of h5i itself) and
properties are proved of it there: that one tenant can not read
the rows of another, that the permission rules hold, that the
state keeps its invariants. For the shapes that come back again
and again — schemas, permission rules, storage — the
specifications and the proofs are made by the tool, not written
by hand. The split is stated in the crate at the center: 'the
core contract between a verified h5i-app kernel and its
untrusted shell'.
# Concept: Receipt before bytes — the record is written before the act
Source: Study S003, the roadmap and browser design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

The red-team half of h5i drives a browser through a proxy that
sits on the road every byte takes, and its rule is: for each
request, a receipt is written FIRST — what was asked, under which
policy, allowed or not — and only then do the bytes move. If the
receipt can not be written, the request does not go. The record
is therefore not a log made after the fact, to be trusted or not;
it is a gate the act itself must pass. The claim of the design,
in its own words, is that tools which drive a browser from
outside 'can not tell you what it reached'; owning the road is
what makes the evidence whole. The pattern is the guardian of
Paper 002 (1.2.19) turned outward: check and record BEFORE the
state changes — here, before the world outside changes.
# Concept: Mutation-tested proofs and the assumption ledger
Source: Study S003, the design files on trust and the app road map. NEW in Study S003.

A proof can be true and still prove nothing you care about, if
its statement was written too weak. h5i answers in two parts.
First, the reach of the verification is governed by written
documents that have the standing of scope authority: what is
proved, what is assumed, what is deliberately not claimed — an
ASSUMPTION LEDGER kept beside the proofs. Second, the proofs are
MUTATION-TESTED: the tool changes the kernel in small wrong ways
(the command is 'h5i app mutate') and checks that the proofs now
FAIL. A proof that still passes a wrong kernel was not holding
the kernel at all. Together: the ledger says what the proofs
mean, and the mutations say whether they bind.
# Concept: Fail-closed boxes for agents
Source: Study S003, the sandbox design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

h5i runs agents — its own browser work, and coding agents such as
Claude and Codex — inside BOXES: layers of holding-in (Landlock,
seccomp, separate name spaces for the system tables, and
containers that need no root) with a policy that is FAIL-CLOSED:
a box that can not be set up as specified does not run at all, in
place of running weaker than specified. The box images ship for
the purpose of holding agents, which makes the box the mirror of
the kernel pattern: the agent is an untrusted producer (1.2.41),
the box is the checked boundary it can not talk its way out of.
# Concept: Skeleton — program structure given as a specification
Source: Paper 006, §§1-2 and App. A. NEW in Paper 006.

A skeleton S is a partial abstraction of a program: its data types,
module boundaries, permitted calls and data flow, and function
declarations with signatures — the implementation bodies left out.
A skeleton may carry more: the notes inside the signatures, local
contracts at each function (1.3.90), and global invariants across
functions. To give a skeleton is to give part of the program — the
structural part — and leave the rest to the agent. In the paper's
model a skeleton fixes the class H_S of programs that share it
(1.2.58), and every other thing the developer gives then works
inside that class. A skeleton is code-like on purpose, and can be
kept, compared and revised like code; the paper's experiments find
that code-like structural specifications beat prose design
documents carrying the same content (results at 10.1.4).
# Concept: How many tests are needed is a property of the structure, not the task
Source: Paper 006, §§2-2.1, Theorem 2.1, App. E.1. NEW in Paper 006.

Let H be the programs an agent can produce, G the ones the
developer would accept, D a set of tests, and the agent's spread
over programs conditioned exactly on what it is given. N (with the
paper's delta for the allowed failure share) is the smallest
number of tests after which the agent lands in G with the asked
probability. Fix a skeleton S and the count inside its class is
N(S). Theorem 2.1: the count is not fixed by the target. There are
two skeletons for one target, each admitting 2^d programs with
distinct behaviors, where the weak skeleton needs 2^d - 1 tests and
the strong one needs d: under the weak skeleton each test rules
out at most one wrong program, under the strong one each test
rules out half of what is wrong. A test set that is exactly right
under the strong skeleton leaves success probability 1/(2^d - d)
under the weak one. Worse, left to choose, the agent's spread over
skeletons is itself shifted by the tests toward the skeleton under
which the tests say least (its eq. 3: success is an average over
the agent's own choice of skeleton, weighted by surviving mass).
So no developer can know whether a test set is enough without
knowing the structure — and structure is the one thing end-to-end
tests cannot say. That is the paper's central claim, and its
reason for giving the structure itself (1.2.57).
# Concept: Interfaces make the middle of a program visible to tests
Source: Paper 006, §2.2, Theorem 2.2, App. E.2. NEW in Paper 006.

An end-to-end test sees only a program's final output. A fault
inside is caught only if the faulty part runs, the fault damages
the state, and the damage survives to the output; later steps can
hide all three. Naming the interfaces of a skeleton names the
middle values (1.3.91), so each part can be tested where it
stands. Theorem 2.2 prices the hiding: a chain of n stages, each
carrying one bit of state, with the zero function as the goal —
there is a prior over the stages' tables under which end-to-end
tests need 2^n tests to pin the program down exactly, while 4n
local tests (the full table of each stage, four cases a stage)
settle every stage. The unknown content is only 4n bits; it is
the hiding, not the size, that costs. The theorem is stated for
exact pinning under a named prior — the paper's appendix says it
does not give a bound for every prior, or for prediction at a
fixed error share — so it prices visibility, not all testing.
# Concept: Some requirements no output test can see
Source: Paper 006, §2.3, Proposition 2.3, App. E.4. NEW in Paper 006.

Programs that agree on every input and every output form a
behavior class. Proposition 2.3: conditioning on any set of
output tests leaves the odds among the members of a behavior
class exactly as they were — every member passes every test the
reference passes, so tests cannot favor the acceptable members
over the rest. If the goal set takes only part of a behavior
class — the rest compute the same function in ways the developer
rejects, too costly to run or too tangled to keep up — then the
number of tests needed is infinite whenever the asked certainty
passes the prior share of the acceptable part. For such
requirements the one lever the developer holds is the skeleton
itself: say the structure, since no test can. The paper's
measured case is code erosion (1.2.64).
# Concept: The cost of pinning a program down splits three ways
Source: Paper 006, §2.4, Theorem 2.4, App. E.3. NEW in Paper 006.

For a skeleton with parts h_1 to h_k, the tests needed for
certainty split into: the cost of describing the skeleton itself;
the local tests that pin the own behavior of each part; and the
end-to-end tests — J_S in the paper — that rule out combinations
of locally good parts whose composition is not acceptable.
Theorem 2.4: the total is the minimum, over choices of the local
goal sets, of (sum of local costs + J_S); and J_S is zero exactly
when every combination of locally acceptable parts is acceptable.
Complete contracts — the asked and promised conditions of each part
strong enough that keeping every promise is enough — drive J_S
to zero, and then the parts are independent: independent agents
can build them side by side with no risk of clash. What makes a
good skeleton, in the paper's terms: short; parts with narrow
interfaces that a few tests or a short contract can pin; and
parts whose separate correctness adds up to whole correctness.
The trade is length: stronger contracts cost more words up front
and buy fewer tests and fewer clashes after.
# Concept: Prompting is not conditioning
Source: Paper 006, §3. NEW in Paper 006.

The counting of 1.2.58 assumes the agent's spread over programs
is cut by exact conditioning on the specification: keep only the
programs that fit it, re-share the rest. A prompted model does
not do that. The spread the prompt in fact produces is not the
conditioned spread, and a program drawn from it can break the
very specification it was given. Under true conditioning, added
specifications can only hold or lower the entropy of the spread in
expectation; under prompting there is no such promise. The
paper's experiments catch the failure in the wild: correct but
overly detailed contracts lower the pass rate, worst on the
small models (10.1.4). This gap is why the paper moves from
giving specifications to checking them: a specification that
can be checked can close the loop by drawing again what fails
(1.2.63); one that cannot be checked is only a hope about the
spread. (The paper grounds the gap in work arguing that
in-context learning is not the conditioning it resembles; that
work is cited here, not taken in.)
# Concept: Checking part by part turns a product into a sum
Source: Paper 006, §3, Propositions 3.1-3.2, App. E.5-E.6. NEW in Paper 006.

Let each part of a skeleton carry a contract, and let alpha_i be
the share of draws of part i that pass its own contract.
Proposition 3.1 (formal checks): drawing whole programs again
until every check passes takes 1/(alpha_1 x ... x alpha_k)
programs in expectation; drawing each part again until its own
check passes takes (1/alpha_1 + ... + 1/alpha_k) part-draws.
Both roads return programs spread as the conditioned spread
over the acceptable set — where the check sits turns a cost
that multiplies into a cost that adds. Proposition 3.2
(informal checks): when the contract is words in the signature
notes and a judge — the agent itself, or a judge model — does
the accepting, a part the judge accepts is truly good with
probability alpha_i / (alpha_i + (1 - alpha_i) x epsilon_i),
where epsilon_i, the false-accept rate, splits into beta_i, the
share of broken contracts the words read literally still
allow, plus the error of the judge on the rest. Beta is a floor
no judge removes; only tighter contract words lower it. A check
earns its keep only when it goes wrong far less often than the agent
fails: epsilon_i much smaller than alpha_i. The paper's
experiments run the informal kind; the formal kind is its
stated future work (10.1.5).
# Concept: Code erosion — complexity mass piling into the hardest functions
Source: Paper 006, §4 (measures) and Fig. 7; the measure itself is
taken from the SCBench work the paper builds on. NEW in Paper 006.

Erosion measures how much of the complexity of a code base sits in
its hardest functions: each function carries weight equal to its
cyclomatic complexity times the square root of its length in
lines, and erosion is the share of the total weight held by
functions whose complexity passes 10. It is a quality axis no
output test can see (1.2.60): two programs pass the same set of
tests while one piles its work into a few tangled functions and
the other spreads it along stated boundaries. Paper 006 measures
erosion as a thing the type of specification moves: signature
specifications lower it against the task-only starting point
(0.66 to 0.56 on SCBench; 0.41 to 0.24 on SDDBench), behavioral
specifications raise it (to as much as 0.73 and 0.43), and the
structural forms cut it by 24.7% against task-only. The paper's
stated mechanism: a standing skeleton records which decisions
are requirements, so an agent adding a feature can rewrite
cleanly in place of piling patches onto accidental behavior.
# Concept: The keys a query needs grow slower than the context
Source: Paper 007, Section II-A, Figure 1, Appendix A. NEW in Paper 007.

Measure, on a trained model reading long text, how many keys hold
almost all of the attention of a query (the top-p set, 1.3.94). The paper
finds the count grows as a power law in the length of the context,
with an exponent under 1, for every share p under the whole: the set
grows, but slower than the context grows, so the longer the text,
the smaller the share of it any one query needs. Longer sequences
are more compressible because the millionth token adds less that is
new than the first tokens did. Two borders are written into the
measurement itself: it is a fact about this data and this model, not
about attention as such — a stream of random tokens would need the
whole cache — and it weakened on the one model tried whose keys
carry no position rotation (Appendix A-C): the way position is
written into the keys is part of the phenomenon.
# Concept: Sparse for one query, whole over all queries — so nothing can be thrown away
Source: Paper 007, Section II-B, Figure 2. NEW in Paper 007.

The same measurement, turned toward the future: take the keys one
query needs (1.2.65), then the keys any of the next W queries will
need, and count the union. As W grows, the union covers the cache:
on narrative text, even a hundred future queries nearly cover it,
and the cover tends to the whole cache as the horizon grows.
Any single key is nearly useless to the query at hand and is the
answer to some query coming. So keys cannot be removed for good —
a cache that throws keys away is a cache that has decided which
future questions it will fail. The saving has to come from finding,
per query, the few keys that query needs, over a cache that stays
whole. This is the fork the paper builds on: removal methods on
one side, per-query retrieval on the other.
# Concept: A tree of summaries over the cache (the hierarchical indexer)
Source: Paper 007, Section III, Figures 3-4. NEW in Paper 007.

Hold the cache (1.3.92) under a tree (1.3.95): keys in a row are cut
into chunks of C; each chunk carries compressed summaries of its
keys; chunks are grouped again, level by level, up to L levels, so
the top summaries stand, through C-to-the-L raw keys, for the whole
cache. One tree is kept per key-value head. A query enters at the
top, is scored against summaries only, and opens just the chunks
that look worth opening (1.2.70), so the expensive scoring of single
keys is spent on a small part of the cache. The shape is fixed by
three numbers: the chunk size C, the depth L, and R, the number of
reference queries each summary is built from (1.2.68). The depth is
allowed to grow as the cache grows — that growth is where the cost
claim comes from (1.2.71).
# Concept: A summary built from tangents at reference queries
Source: Paper 007, Section III-A. NEW in Paper 007.

The total pull of a chunk on a query — the log of the sum of the key scores of the chunk, before the shares are made to add to one — is a smooth,
everywhere-rounded curve as the query moves (1.3.97). ZHA stands in
for that curve with R straight lines: the tangents of the curve at R
chosen queries, the REFERENCE QUERIES. Under each reference query, a
the summary of a chunk is one compressed key — the pull-weighted
average of the keys of the chunk under that query — plus one number, a bias, that
puts the line at the right height. A new query scores a chunk by
taking the highest of the R lines. Summaries of summaries merge by
the same construction: a parent is computed from its children's
summaries under the same reference queries; where position rotations
are in play the merged score is a lower bound on the true pull, not
the pull itself, and the paper says so. Approximate the curve, and
the tree of 1.2.67 can be scored without touching single keys.
# Concept: Reference queries — the only trained part, distilled into a frozen model
Source: Paper 007, Sections III-B and V-A, Appendix B. NEW in Paper 007.

Nothing in the tree (1.2.67) is trained except the reference queries
(1.2.68): one shared set per tree, the same queries at every node
and every level, shared again across the heads of a group. They are
taught by distillation (1.3.98): at each level, the estimated
shares of the tree over chunks are matched to the attention of the dense model itself,
gathered into chunks, and the gap between the two spreads is driven
down — while the model itself is frozen, untouched. One billion
tokens at 32K context was enough on the two large models converted;
the added parameters are a few millionths of the model. So the
method is a conversion, not a retraining: a small trained module is
bolted onto an already-trained model, and if the module is removed the
model is what it was. Early in training, when the tree's estimates
are still poor, all children are selected and the choice is cut down
to the top-p set as the estimates improve.
# Concept: Top-p at every level — the budget follows the mass
Source: Paper 007, Sections III-C and VI-A. NEW in Paper 007.

Route by mass, not by count: at every level of the tree, keep the
smallest set of nodes whose estimated pull reaches the share p,
then score only their children at the next level down. A fixed
count (top-K) spends the same budget on a query whose mass sits in
three chunks as on one whose mass is spread over three hundred;
the top-p rule spends where the mass is, so the budget follows the
query. The paper measures how much this matters: at about 64K
tokens, the chunks holding 90 percent of a head's attention mass
are a median 2.3 percent of chunks on a retrieval suite, 11.7
percent on a coding-agent suite, 15.0 percent on fiction — no one
budget fits all three. The router kept less than the 90-percent
set and still held a median 78 to 80
percent of the mass. Two keys ride
outside the rule: the first key of the cache and the chunk now
being written are always attended; chunks not yet full are always
passed down.
# Concept: Growing the depth is what makes the cost grow slower than the square
Source: Paper 007, Section V, Figures 7-8, Appendices D-E. NEW in
Paper 007.

Count what the router touches. At each level the selected nodes
grow as a power law in the context length, exponent under 1, the
exponent largest at the top and falling as the walk goes down.
Summed over a tree whose depth grows slowly with the length, the
work per query — routing plus the final attention over the chosen
keys — grows slower than the context, so the whole attention cost
grows slower than the square while still growing faster than a
straight line. The numbers past the measured range are a roofline
reckoning (1.3.99), not measured times — the paper releases no kernels and
says the far end has not been run: in the reckoning, a one-level tree
passes dense attention near 48K tokens, a two-level tree passes the
strongest fixed-sparsity rival near half a million, and at a
billion tokens the reckoning gives 373 to 417 times the speed of
dense attention, at depth five. Held as the model's output, marked
as such wherever this phext cites it.
# Concept: The effective-p trap — depth taxes the threshold
Source: Paper 007, Section VII. NEW in Paper 007.

The limit the paper states for its own method, kept as a concept because it is a
shape, not a detail. Hold the share p the same at every level, and
the mass that survives the walk falls like p raised to the depth:
grow the tree and the kept set shrinks toward a fixed size — the
cache has quietly become a fixed-state memory, the very thing
1.2.66 rules out. Push p toward 1 as the depth grows instead, and
the top levels keep nearly everything: the tree dissolves, and the
cost returns to the square. Between the two failures lies a
schedule — p set by depth, at a rate that tracks the data's own
fall in new information (1.2.65) — and the paper names that schedule
as work not yet done. The subquadratic claim (1.2.71) is therefore
conditional in a way the paper states plainly: it holds as far as
the data keeps its measured shape, which the paper has measured
only to 256K tokens.
# Concept: The process in memory — text, data, and stack
Source: Study S004, the section of the article on process memory organization. NEW in Study S004.

A running program (a process) holds its life in three regions
of memory. The TEXT region holds the code and the read-only
data; it is fixed by the program file, and a write to it is
stopped by the system with a segmentation violation. The DATA
region holds the static variables — set at load time, first
set or left empty — and can grow by the brk call. The STACK
region holds the run itself: the live calls, their local
variables, and the data that lets each call return. The
regions differ in what may be written to them: text not at
all, data and stack freely. Everything the article does
happens on the stack; and everything the proving work of
clusters F-H does, in the end, is about who may write what,
where.
# Concept: The stack frame — one record for each live call
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on the stack.
NEW in Study S004.

A call lays down a FRAME on the stack: the parameters passed
in, the local variables of the called function, the saved
frame pointer (SFP) of the caller, and the return address
(RET) — the place in the caller where running resumes when
the called function ends. Frames stack in call order: the
last of the call frame sits on top, and a return takes the top
frame away. All four parts lie side by side in one run of
memory, in a fixed order the compiler chooses: local variables at
negative offsets from the frame pointer, parameters at
positive ones. The frame is the unit of the call machine
(12.2.1): one frame for each live call, pushed at the call,
popped at the return.
# Concept: The frame ceremony — prolog and closing code
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on the stack.
NEW in Study S004.

The first code of a called function is its PROLOG: save the
of the caller frame pointer on the stack, copy the stack pointer
into the frame pointer, and move the stack pointer to make
room for the local variables, rounded up to whole words. The
last code is its closing code: undo all of it — give the space
back, restore the of the caller frame pointer from the saved one,
and take the return address as the next instruction to run.
The machines carry instructions for the pair: ENTER and LEAVE
on Intel, LINK and UNLINK on Motorola. The ceremony is small,
fixed, and run at every call — which is why its shape can be
learned once and counted on: the saved frame pointer and the
return address always sit in known places against the frame
pointer.
# Concept: The overflow — a write past the end of a buffer
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on buffer overflows and on finding them. NEW in Study S004.

A buffer is a run of memory set aside to hold data of one
kind. An OVERFLOW is what happens when more data is put into
a buffer than the buffer can hold: the write does not stop
at the of the buffer end, because in C nothing makes it stop —
the language carries no built-in check of bounds, and its
common copy functions (strcpy, strcat, sprintf, gets, and
scanf without a width) copy until the source ends, not until
the target is full. The extra data lands in whatever lies
next to the buffer in memory. When the buffer is a local
variable, what lies next is the rest of its own frame — the
saved frame pointer and the return address (1.2.74). The
error is common and its shape is always the same: a length
nobody checked, against a bound nobody set a guard on.
# Concept: The return address as data that becomes control
Source: Study S004, the second and third examples of the article.
NEW in Study S004.

The return address is written by the call as data — a number
saved in the frame — and read by the return as control: the
next place to run. It is the one word in the frame where the
two meet. An overflow that reaches it (1.2.76) therefore
does not only spoil data: when the function returns, the
machine jumps to the address the overflow put there. Filled
with one repeated character, the address is junk and the run
dies in a segmentation violation — the crash of the of the article
second example. Chosen with care, the address is the start
of code the writer of the overflow placed there himself —
the turn of the third example, where a small change to the
saved address steps over an instruction and changes what the
program does. It is the core claim of the article itself: a buffer
overflow allows us to change the return address of a
function, and in this way we can change the flow of
execution of the program.
# Concept: Shellcode — code delivered as data
Source: Study S004, the sections of the article on shellcode.
NEW in Study S004.

The code an overflow runs is not in the program: it arrives
as data, in the same string that does the overflowing, and
sits in the buffer as bytes until the turned return address
lands on it. The article builds the smallest useful piece:
a byte string that asks the system to run a shell — execve
of /bin/sh — 46 bytes in all. Two crafts make it work. It
must hold no zero byte of its own, because the copy that
carries it stops at a zero — so every zero it needs, it
makes at run time, out of instructions that hold none. And
it must find its own string wherever it lands, for it does
not know its address ahead of time: a jump-and-call pair
plants the of the string address where the code can take it, the
call itself pushing that address onto the stack as a return
address. A last piece: code that changes itself must sit
where writing is allowed — the stack or the data region,
not the read-only text (1.2.73).
# Concept: Guessing the address — the stack starts at the same place
Source: Study S004, the section of the article on writing an exploit. NEW in Study S004.

An overflow of another program must aim its return address
at a buffer it can not see. The of the article answer rests on how
regular the world is: for every program the stack starts at
the same address, and most programs push only a few hundred
to a few thousand bytes before the point that matters. So
the address of the target buffer is one's own stack pointer,
less an offset — and the offset can be searched: run the
pair again and again, moving the offset, until the run lands
in the buffer instead of dying. The search is brutal in the telling of the article itself: at best a hundred tries, at worst a
couple of thousand, and a guess off by one byte gives a
segmentation violation or an illegal instruction. What makes
the search possible at all is that the layout of the machine
is fixed and knowable — the same fixed shape the frame ceremony (1.2.75) creates.
# Concept: The wide landing — sled, egg, and the price of a guess
Source: Study S004, the third and fourth exploits of the article and its Appendix B. NEW in Study S004.

Two crafts turn the brutal search of 1.2.79 into a likely
hit. The SLED: fill the front half of the overflow string
with no-operation instructions (one byte each on Intel), put
the shellcode at the center, and the guessed addresses after:
any landing inside the sled slides down the no-operations
into the code, so the guess need only be right to within the
length of the sled, not to the byte. In the run the article prints, a
612-byte string against a 512-byte buffer lands the shell on
the first try. The EGG: when the target buffer is too small
to hold the code at all, the code rides in an environment
variable — a far larger string the system sets in the
process — and the small buffer carries only guessed addresses
pointing into the egg; Appendix B of the article puts both crafts into one tool, eggshell, with the buffer size, the egg
size, the offset, and the alignment as its settings. The
lesson of the pair: an aim that can not be made exact can be
made wide.
# Concept: A design system — one shared set of parts and rules
Source: Study S005, the root README and the layout of the repo. NEW in Study S005.

A design system is a kit a whole company builds with: a
set of ready parts for screens (buttons, dialogs, menus,
fields), a layer of named values that decide how the
parts look, ready pages to start from, and the rules
that keep the parts alike. The point of the kit is that
no team starts from nothing, and no two teams' screens
drift apart without knowing it. Astryx is the kit of
Meta, carried out of the company and opened: the parts
in one package, the looks in another, the tool that
answers questions about both in a third. What the kit
holds is less a library than an agreement — the parts,
the values, and the rules are one artifact, and a change
to any of them is a change to the agreement. See also
the skeleton of Paper 006 (1.2.57): a design system is a
skeleton a company agrees to share.
# Concept: The document beside the part — machine-readable docs as part of the component
Source: Study S005, AGENTS.md and the core tree. NEW in Study S005.

In Astryx a part carries a file of its own: a document
file in a plain script form, holding a typed object —
the name of the part, what it is for, its props, its kinds,
its examples. 110 of the 122 folders of the core carry
one, and the command line tool reads these files to
answer, so the document is not a page about the part —
it is a part of the part, shipped in the same package
and held to the same checks. A document in this shape
can be read by a person, by the tool, and by an agent
with no step between: one text, three readers. The rule
that makes it hold is a record of the house (1.2.87):
every part that can be found must own such a file, so a
part without a document is a part the tool cannot see.
# Concept: One surface for people and machines — the command line as the shared tool
Source: Study S005, the CLI README and the manifest it is made from. NEW in Study S005.

The Astryx command line tool is built twice-facing:
every answer it gives a person it can also give as
typed data — a wrapped answer naming its kind, its
version, and which copy of the tool answered — and
every failure comes with a code meant to be branched
on, a code whose meaning, once shipped, never changes
and is never taken back; the words of the failure may
change, the code may not. The tool describes itself:
one command prints every command, every option, and
every answer kind, as data — and the table of commands
in its own README is made from that description by a
script, so the book and the tool cannot drift apart.
It reads at three depths — names only, names with one
line, the full document — and in a dense form that
spends fewer words for a reader who pays by the word.
A person and an agent do not get two tools; they get
one tool with two faces, and the face for machines is
held to the full rule.
# Concept: A theme is data — the look as a set of named values
Source: Study S005, the core README and the theme packages. NEW in Study S005.

A theme in Astryx is not a fork of the parts and not a
wrapper around them: it is a set of named values —
colors, spaces, corner rounds, shadows, letter faces —
written as the custom properties of the page (1.3.108),
laid over the parts by the order of the sheets: first
the reset, then the parts, then the theme, each in its
own layer, so the word of the theme wins by place, not by
force. To make the system yours you change values, not
code; light and dark are two readings of the same
values, not two builds. Seven themes ship, each a small
package whose color sets are made by a script and kept
with the receipt of their making; an eighth folder, a
probe, is a made-up theme the checks use to test the
layer itself. Guard scripts watch the layer: that every
named value can be reached from the parts, and that the
order of the sheets stands as written.
# Concept: Swizzle — owning the source as the way out
Source: Study S005, the swizzle code of the CLI and the record of its spec. NEW in Study S005.

When a part almost fits, Astryx does not ask you to wrap
it or to fork the whole kit: the tool copies the source
of the part into your own project — the part and the inner
modules it needs, traveling whole along the module
graph (1.3.109) — while its calls on the public parts
of the kit stay calls on the kit. Names that clash are
renamed, and the fixing of the copies' readings of each
other runs until nothing moves. A marker file is set in
the folder, whose one job is to tell the build tool:
what stands here is Astryx source — build it as source.
The tool answers with a receipt: what was copied,
where, how many files, what the copy needs to stand.
The word is defined at 1.3.111. The shape of the thing:
the kit holds its parts loosely — the way out of a part
is to own it, and owning is a first-class move the tool
makes for you, not a break with the system.
# Concept: Conventions a tool can check — rules with tests, and a ratchet
Source: Study S005, the lint plugin of internal/. NEW in Study S005.

Astryx holds its conventions — how props are named, what
a part must take, what no part may do — as rules of a
lint tool (1.3.112): 38 rules in the repo's own plugin,
each with its own tests, run over every change. A rule a
tool can check does not need to be remembered or argued
in review; it is run. Seven of the rules carry a
baseline: the recorded list of the breaks that already
stand, so the rule fails only on new breaks — a ratchet
that lets a convention gain ground over a large old code
base without a day of reckoning. The claim of the
README — we test conventions rather than assert them —
has its machinery here: a convention that matters
becomes a rule, a rule carries tests, and the breaks it
finds are either fixed or written into the baseline
list where all can see them.
# Concept: Records that govern — standing by approval: draft, current, set aside
Source: Study S005, AGENTS.md and the records of the specs folder. NEW in Study S005.

The repo keeps its shared decisions as records, each
marked with one of three marks: draft — not yet a rule
of the house; current — approved, and the only mark
that governs building and review; set aside — kept for
its reasons, with a word for why (a newer record stands
in its place, or it was withdrawn, or it is history).
82 such records stand in the specs folder of the docs.
The discipline is in the moves that do NOT count: code
merged, quiet in review, an old page on the wiki — none
of these makes a record current; approval is a step
someone takes, or the record does not govern. A tool
answers which records govern any path, and a script
checks the records for their shape. It is a registry of
claims about how the system shall be — the same shape
as the standing record of this tracker (1.4.x), with
the ground of standing set the other way: theirs is
approval, ours is receipts (Q34).
# Concept: The vibe tests — a system measured by what a fresh agent can build with it
Source: Study S005, the README of internal/vibe-tests. NEW in Study S005.

The vibe tests measure the kit itself, through agents:
a battery of prompts — build this page, under this
limit — is given to fresh agents with no memory of the
system, who must find their way from the shipped files
alone: the README in the installed package, the
documents, the tool. The same prompts run against other
kits and against bare HTML; the answers they were
expected to reach are held back from the agents and
used only in the scoring, which runs over the code the
agents wrote. Five rules of fairness guard the measure:
the judging is the same for all; only the kit changes;
the answer is never leaked; the setting is the one a
real user gets; every agent starts empty. A second form
runs long talks — ten turns of filling and stray
turns — and probes the work at set turns, to measure
how fast the good building fades as the talk grows.
What is measured is not the agent: it is whether the
system's own words about itself are enough to build
with.
# Concept: Build-to-learn — the artifact made by hand is the lesson
Source: Study S006, the design of the course as its pages and its code state it. NEW in Study S006.

Some systems are learned by reading about them, and
some only by making them. TinyTorch bets the whole
course on the second: the student does not read about
a tensor, an autograd engine, a training loop — the
student makes each one, in order, and the making is
the lesson. The text that goes with each part (the
problem, the idea, the code, a worked trace) is built
around the making, not in place of it. The bet has a
price: the course can only teach what a student can
make in the time a module takes, so each part is cut
to the size of one sitting — which is what the split
of 1.2.91 is for. See also the level-zero rule of
this tracker (charter rule 9): the same bet, laid on
words — nothing is held that was not built up from
the ground.
# Concept: The built stack — each layer of the framework made on the one before
Source: Study S006, the module map and the sources of modules 01-13. NEW in Study S006.

The framework is not made all at once; it is stacked.
Module 01 makes the tensor; 02-05 make the parts that
stand on it (activations, layers, losses, the loading
of data); 06 makes the autograd engine that the parts
of 01 already point at — module 01 carries the fields
and the class the engine will fill, marked in the
source for module 06; 07-08 close the training loop;
09-13 build the architectures (vision, then language)
on the trained stack. Each layer is a working thing
before the next begins, and the later layers import
the earlier ones through the student's own exported
package — the stack the student stands on is the
stack the student made. The order is the teaching:
no layer is used before it has been built.
# Concept: Core and scaffold — the written part and the given part, split by marks in the source
Source: Study S006, the solution policy of the tito tool and the counts of the study. NEW in Study S006.

Every implementation in the course sources is cut
into regions, and each region carries a role, marked
in the source itself. The core regions are the ones
the student writes: for the student, their bodies
are cleared and an error stands in their place until
the student fills them. The scaffold regions ship
solved — helpers, shape checks, the patterns that
come back module after module. The split is the
answer the course gives to a hard question: how much of a
real system can a student write and still finish?
Its measured answer: 54 core regions, 976 lines of
the 45,533 in the module sources — about one line
in fifty. The rest is given, in the open: the same
source serves the student tier and the instructor
tier, and one shared policy decides for each region
and each tier whether it is cleared, kept, or taken
out. The policy stands in one place because it once
stood in two, and the two disagreed — in September
2026 the student path handed out the full solutions
by mistake (the own record of the tool, bug 1684), and
the fix was to make the split one machine with one
home.
# Concept: Assessment by reconstruction — the landmarks of the field, rebuilt with one's own code
Source: Study S006, the milestone system of the site and the code. NEW in Study S006.

The course does not end a stage with a test alone.
At seven points it asks the student to rebuild a
landmark from the history of the field itself with the code
they have made so far: the perceptron of 1958, the
XOR crisis of 1969, the multi-layer perceptron revival of 1986, the CNN
revolution of 1998, the transformer era of 2017,
serving as MLPerf tells it (2018), and custom
kernels (2024). Each landmark names the modules it
needs, and it stands complete only when every part
it names has been run and has passed on the student's
own exported code — the history is not read, it is
re-made, and the re-making is the measure. The
requirements were themselves checked against the
own traces of the tool of what the landmark code runs
(the audit recorded in the milestone code, 2026).
Run with its checks skipped, a landmark is a demo
and records nothing: the course keeps the difference
between seeing a thing run and having made it run.
# Concept: The progress ledger and the gate — completion as a record, not a claim
Source: Study S006, the tito tool and the run of the study. NEW in Study S006.

A module is complete when a record says so, and the
record is earned through a gate. The gate, inside
the complete command: the tests inside the notebook; a
check of the shape of the notebook; the export of the
notebook into the student's package; the tests of
the package as imported; and only then the writing
of the ledger — a small file (.tito/progress.json)
holding the started and completed modules. Every
later step reads the ledger: the completion of the next
module is refused until the one before it stands
in the ledger as completed, and the milestones
unlock from what the ledger holds. The shape is
worth naming: the claim 'I finished' is not taken
from the student; it is the last step of a pipeline
whose earlier steps are checks, and the record is
written by the tool, not by the person it is about.
Run end to end in the study (14.1.2): the gate
passed for module 01, the ledger moved, and the
refusal for module 03 was shown.
# Concept: The update-preservation partition — the student's work stands outside the updates of the course
Source: Study S006, the update command of the tito tool. NEW in Study S006.

A course that updates itself under its students has
to say, ahead of time, what an update may touch and
what it may never touch. The update command carries
the two lists in its code. To be written over: the module
sources, the tool, the tests, the milestones, the
data sets, and a short list of files. Preserved: the
folder of the student's notebooks, the environment,
and the .tito folder with its ledger — and, inside
the package, the core the student's exports built,
while the own door file of the package still updates.
Before any update runs, the tool copies the kept
heart — the progress files, the notebooks, the built
core — into a snapshot folder stamped with the time.
The newest release is found over a checked
connection; if it cannot be reached, the command
says so and changes nothing. The partition is an
invariant the tooling keeps, not a promise the
student is asked to trust: the lists are the code.
# Concept: The autograd tape — operations recorded, then walked back
Source: Study S006, module 06 and the fields module 01 lays down for it. NEW in Study S006.

The backward pass needs to know what happened in
the forward pass, in the order it happened. The
engine of module 06 keeps that as a tape of recorded
operations: while a tracking switch stands on, and
an input of an operation asks for gradients, the
operation is written down — its kind, its inputs,
its output — as a node of a graph. The backward call
then walks the graph in the order opposite to the
making: a sorting of the nodes so that each is
visited after all that used it, one pass, the
gradients of shared inputs summed as they meet. At
the end of the walk the whole tape is let go in one
pass. Module 01 already carries the shape of this
machine before it exists: the fields for the
gradient and for the node stand in its tensor from
the start, and its backward raises an error that
says, in effect, not yet. The course states its own
cuts against the grown systems plainly: one object
per call in place of a carried context; the gradient
filled on every visited tensor, not only the leaves;
the tape freed whole, not node by node.
# Concept: Answers in the open — a course that does not rest on hiding its solutions
Source: Study S006, the book, the start command, and the site. NEW in Study S006.

The answers to every exercise of this course are
public: the book that goes with the modules prints
the full working code of each one, and the default
start command hands out the solved notebook. What
is graded, then, cannot be the having of the answer.
The course moves the weight to two other places: the
tests that run against the student's own exported
package (the answer copied is still the student's to
keep running), and the milestones, which pass only
when the landmark runs on the student's code. The
exercise tier — the start that clears the core
regions — stands ready for the classroom the course
is pointed at (its full release is set for spring
2027), and the tool says in its own words that the
preview ships the solutions first. A course can be
built this way when its measure is the building and
the re-making, not the keeping of secrets; whether
the measure holds from outside the project is a
question the design leaves open (Q38).
# Concept: Measure, then change — the order of the optimization modules
Source: Study S006, modules 14-19 and their order in the map. NEW in Study S006.

The last third of the course is one discipline in
six parts, and the order is the discipline: profiling
first (14) — find where the time and the memory go;
then the changes that shrink the work — quantization
(15) and compression (16); then the changes that
speed the work — acceleration (17) and memoization
(18); and benchmarking (19) over all of it, the
measure that says whether a change earned its place.
No change is taught before the measure that would
judge it, and the capstone runs the student's whole
framework against the grown ones on shared ground.
The pattern is older than the course: a change
without a measure is a guess, and a measure taken
after the fact can be bent to fit the change. Put
the measure first and the change has to answer to
something it did not choose.
# Concept: The Arnold number and the strong Arnold conjecture
Source: Paper 008, §§1a-1c. NEW in Paper 008; cluster E grows from
the making of manifolds to the counting of what their maps must
leave standing.

For a closed symplectic manifold M (1.3.22), the ARNOLD NUMBER
Arn(M) is the least number of fixed points (1.3.118) a Hamiltonian
diffeomorphism of M can have — the fewest points such a map can
be made to leave standing. Crit(M) is the least number of
critical points (1.3.119) a smooth function on M can have — the
fewest resting points of a slope. The STRONG ARNOLD CONJECTURE
(Arnold, 1965 and 1978) said Arn(M) is never under Crit(M): a map
made by a flow can be no more still, at its fewest, than a
function. The weaker forms stand proved: count the fixed points
by homology instead, and the bound holds for every closed
symplectic manifold, over the fractions and over the whole
numbers. The strong form fell in 2026, twice before this paper:
The map of Jiao on S^2 x S^2 with two fixed points (the paper's ref
29), and the OpenAI map on the quadric threefold with three (ref
36) — both under Crit. Paper 008 carries the fall onto the flag
varieties (1.2.102): whole families with Arn(M) under Crit(M) by
any margin named in advance, and one family at near half of
Crit(M). What Arn(M) is for a manifold in general the paper
calls, in its own words, completely open. This scroll therefore
holds a claim and the record of its fall in one place — and the
standing it takes is the say-so of its sources, for the model
held the conjecture in no scroll of its own before this paper
arrived: there was nothing here to step down. The record
of that near-miss stands at 15.1.5.
# Concept: Clean maps and the perturbation bound — Arn(M) at most Crit of the fixed locus
Source: Paper 008, §2a (Definition 2.1, Proposition 2.2). NEW in
Paper 008.

A Hamiltonian diffeomorphism is CLEAN when its graph meets the
diagonal cleanly — in plain terms, when its fixed point set is
a submanifold, and at each fixed point the directions the map
holds still are exactly the directions of that submanifold (the
fixed locus rests Morse-Bott, 1.3.119). A map of finite order
is clean without being asked. THE BOUND: if a clean map has
fixed locus N, then Arn(M) is at most Crit(N). A small turn of
the map, made in the model of Pozniak of a neighborhood of the
diagonal, trades the standing submanifold for its own fewest
resting points: the turned map is Hamiltonian, and it has
exactly Crit(N) fixed points. The price is named in the same
breath: the new fixed points may be degenerate — gathered on the
locus, not spread through M — and the strong conjecture of
1.2.98 is a claim about exactly that case. This one bound is
the engine of the whole paper: every count in its table is the
bound spent on a well-chosen clean map (the choosing is
1.2.100 and 1.2.101).
# Concept: The Arnold number of a group — clean group actions as a source of maps with few fixed points
Source: Paper 008, §2a (Definition 2.5) and §§2c-2d. NEW in
Paper 008.

For a group G (1.3.10) whose members are all clean Hamiltonian
diffeomorphisms, Arn(G) is the least of Crit(Fix(g)) over the
members g. By the bound of 1.2.99, Arn(M) is at most Arn(G):
to make a map with few fixed points, it is enough to search a
good group. A complex flag variety (1.2.102) comes with such a
group for free — the largest compact part of its own symmetry
group acts by Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms, and acts cleanly,
its members keeping every distance of the metric the variety
carries. The Arnold problem becomes a search over a group: name
the fixed locus of a member, count its resting points, and
find the member that makes the count least (the search itself
is 1.2.101). What the group cannot give is a floor: Arn(G)
bounds Arn(M) from above alone, and the floors in the paper's
table come from another tool entire (1.2.103). Where the floor
and the search meet — the quadrics — the Arnold number is
known whole.
# Concept: Stretching films — the move that lowers Crit(Fix(g))
Source: Paper 008, §§2c-2d (the proofs of Propositions 2.7 and
2.12). NEW in Paper 008.

The members of the group of 1.2.100 are named by the flats of directions that share an eigenvalue (1.3.121): blocks the map turns by an angle, blocks
it holds (+1), blocks it flips (-1). For a member with a turning
block, the fixed locus falls into many pieces — smaller
Grassmannians, one for each way the block can meet a fixed
subspace — and each piece carries its own resting points.
STRETCHING A FILM is the move that turns the angle of one block all
the way to a hold or a flip: the new fixed locus is one piece
where the old was many — the old pieces stand inside it as the
fixed locus of a circle of maps acting on it, a film stretched
between them — and the count does not rise. In the type C
count of the paper, the difference is a count of sets of three drawn
from the block plus a count of pairs drawn beside it, each at
least zero; the type B count runs by the same shape, through
the moment map of a circle acting on the stretched locus. So
the search runs downhill by turning blocks, and stops only
when every block is a hold or a flip; the least count then sits
at the balanced split — half hold, half flip — and that split
writes the numbers of the paper's table. The authors name the
hope that the move walks further than group actions: that the
fixed pieces of any clean map might be joined by films, step
by step (carried as Q43).
# Concept: Flag varieties, and Crit(M) = dim + 1
Source: Paper 008, §§1c and 2b-2e (result 2.6, Theorem 1.1). NEW
in Paper 008.

A flag variety is the space of all flags of one shape (1.3.120)
— chains of subspaces in a space, each inside the next — and
the Grassmannians, spaces of all subspaces of one fixed size,
are its one-step case. The paper works three families: the
Lagrangian Grassmannian LG(n,2n), all Lagrangian subspaces of a
2n-space; the isotropic Grassmannians OG(n-k,2n+1) of type B;
and, inside the second family, the quadrics Q^n — so the
genuine quadric of Paper 005 (S^2 x S^2, 1.2.34) is Q^2, a
member of this family. The result numbered 2.6 in the paper: on any complex flag variety,
Crit(M) = dim_C M + 1 — a function can be made with one resting
point per complex dimension plus one, and with no fewer.
Against that count the paper's table stands: for LG(n,2n),
Arn(M) at most n(n+2)/4 + 1 (n even) or (n+1)^2/4 + 1 (n odd)
— near half of Crit; for OG(n-k,2n+1) with k over 0, Arn(M) at
most Crit(M) - (n-k) — under by a margin that grows without
bound, so Arn(M) under Crit(M) - N for any N named in advance;
for the quadrics, Arn(Q^n) = n whole, for every n at least 2
but n = 4 (the floors are 1.2.103). In the type B family the
least member is a single flip: the map that sends the last
direction to its negative and holds all the rest, whose fixed
locus is the same kind of Grassmannian one size down.
# Concept: The floor that stands — lower bounds from the minimal Chern number
Source: Paper 008, §§1b-1c (Remark 1.2) and §2e. NEW in
Paper 008.

Against the falls of 1.2.98 stands what still binds. For the
families of this paper, the best floor the authors know comes
from the MINIMAL CHERN NUMBER of M: the least positive degree
of a sphere in M, read off its quantum cohomology ring (1.3.14)
— worked out for these Grassmannians by Tamvakis. By a theorem
of Schwarz, on a positively monotone manifold the count made
by the quantum cup-length gives a floor of fixed points for
every Hamiltonian diffeomorphism, and for the flag families
here that floor is the minimal Chern number itself. On the
quadrics the floor is n, and the search of 1.2.100 meets it
exactly: Arn(Q^n) = n, known whole — the one place in the
paper where the Arnold number is a number and not a gap.
Everywhere else a gap stands: for LG(n,2n) the floor is n + 1
while the best map known stands near half of Crit, and the
true number lies somewhere in the wide field between (Q41).
The shape of the whole paper is this pair: a search that
lowers the ceiling, a floor that does not move, and a family
where the two touch.
# The straight-segment problem — gravity from a line, not a point

A particle with no mass of its own moves in the pull of a fixed straight
segment S of length 2L, carrying a mass spread along it by
any positive density σ — a rule for mass per length,
nowhere zero and nowhere infinite on the segment. The pull
at a point q is the sum over the segment, and the potential
U(q) is that sum weighted by the density and turned
downhill (1.3.125). One body, drawn out to a line: the
simplest heavy thing that is not a point.

Two facts shape everything the paper proves. First, the
potential at q is set by two numbers only — the distances
r1 and r2 from q to the two ends of the segment (Remark 2.1 of
the paper). Second, the field is unchanged by turning q
around the line of the segment: the pull has a symmetry, and the
symmetry keeps one quantity fixed along every road — the
angular momentum about the axis of the segment (1.3.126). The
field is not central: it does not point at one center, and
no road is forced into a plane by the shape of the field
alone.

Why a segment: a long thin body — an asteroid drawn out —
is priced in the cited line of work by this model, cheaper
than a full stone-by-stone reckoning and more true to the body than a
point. The paper's question is not how the particle moves
in detail, but where its road can end. As taken in by:
Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# The logarithmic wall — the pull of a segment deepens only like a log

Hold the distance from q to the segment, δ — the least
distance from q to any point of S. Lemma 3.2 of the paper:
the size of the potential is held between two logs of the
same shape,

  k · log(1 + 2L/δ) ≤ |U(q)| ≤ 2K · log(1 + 2L/δ),

where k and K are set by the least and the greatest values
the density takes on the segment. As δ shrinks to zero the
potential falls without bound — the segment is a true
singularity of the field — but it falls like a log: slower
than any power, far slower than the 1/r fall toward a point
mass.

The proof is a sandwich: the same bounds are first shown
for a segment of constant density, where the potential
can be written down whole, and an arbitrary density is then
priced between its own least and greatest values — the
shape of the density washes out, and only its reach
survives. Every later result of the paper spends this one:
the singularity is strong enough to forbid standing clear
(1.2.106), and weak enough to lose its race against a
turning particle (1.2.107). As taken in by: Paper 009
(Chapter 16).
# Every singularity is an approach

The flow of the segment problem (1.3.124) runs on the
states outside the segment. A road of the
flow can fail to run forever in either direction of time,
and the failure at a finite time is what the paper calls a
singularity of the road. Theorem 3.3: at every such finite
end, the distance to the segment falls to zero —
δ(q(t)) → 0. There is no breakdown at a distance: no
blow-up of speed, no wild turn, nothing that ends the road
while the particle stands clear of the body.

The proof runs by contradiction on the continuation of
roads: if the distance stayed above some floor, the
potential and the pull would stay bounded on the whole neighborhood
of the road (the log wall of 1.2.105 is finite at
every positive distance), the speed would stay bounded with
them, and the road could be carried past its supposed end.
So the only door out of the flow is the body itself. The
claim holds for every positive density; the earlier form of
it, in the thesis this paper builds on, held for densities
growing in a straight line along the segment, and the step
to all densities is the log wall doing the work. As taken
in by: Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# The angular-momentum barrier — every approach lies in a plane

A road that comes at the segment (1.2.106) with any turning
at all cannot arrive. Theorem 4.1: if δ(q(t)) → 0 at a
finite end, then the angular momentum about the axis
of the segment is zero — ℓ = 0. Read in the coordinates the symmetry
gives (along the axis, out from it, around it), the energy
holds a term ℓ²/2ρ², where ρ is the distance from the axis.
Near the segment that term grows like 1/ρ²; the potential
can only pay for growth like a log (1.2.105). Energy is
kept, so the books do not balance unless ℓ is exactly zero:
the turning term would outrun everything the field can
lend.

With ℓ = 0 the angle around the axis never moves, and the
whole road lies in one fixed plane through the segment —
the invariant plane. In the work this paper builds on, the
plane was a starting cut: the motion was studied inside a
plane from the first line, and only for even densities.
Here the plane is a finding, not a frame: any road, from
any start in space, that ends at the body lies in a plane at its
end, for any positive density. Roads with ℓ not zero are
not shown gentle — they may run forever — they are only
shown never to arrive. As taken in by: Paper 009
(Chapter 16).
# Approach is collision — the endings, classified

In the plane (1.2.107), approach becomes arrival. Theorem
4.4: if a road in the plane has δ(q(t)) → 0 at a finite end, then
q(t) itself comes to one point q* of the segment as the
end nears — the particle does not skim along the segment
forever; the road has finite length, and a road of finite
length has a limit. The pull near the body points across
the axis with a strength bounded below, and that strength,
integrated, is what buys the finite length: each crossing
of the axis costs the road a fixed coin of its remaining
length, so only a finite length of road is left.

Corollary 4.5 closes the book of endings: every singularity
of the flow, read forward, is a collision — the particle
meets the body at a point of it; read backward, the same
event is an ejection — a road that begins at the body, the
time-turn of a collision. Together with 1.2.106 there is no
third ending: a road of this flow either runs for all time,
or ends at the body, arriving or leaving. As taken in by:
Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# The angular cone — a test that promises collision, with a clock

For a point q* of the segment, the paper builds a cone in
two halves (its Definition 5.1). The triangle T(q*) is the
set of points from which the segment is held under the same
spread of sight-lines as from q* itself: every line from
the point to the segment falls inside the wedge the two
ends cut. At each point x of the segment, the cone PWC(x) holds
all pulls the segment can aim at a particle placed at
x — every sum of the unit vectors from x toward the
points of the segment, each weighted by a number that is
not negative. The
angular cone C(q*) pairs them: a state (q, p) stands in the
cone when q stands in the triangle and the direction of p
is one the segment itself could be pulling toward, read
from q*. The same condition reads the same on velocity, on
momentum, on acceleration.

Three theorems make the cone the paper's working tool.
Lemma 5.2: the cone holds what enters it — under the flow,
the sight-point of the momentum walks toward the average sight-point of the segment,
weighted by its pull, and never leaves the
reach of the segment, and the position stays in the triangle;
the cone is a trap with no exit but the body. Theorem 5.3:
a state in the cone with inward momentum (p_ρ0 < 0)
collides in finite time, with a clock anyone can read off
the start:

  ω+ ≤ −ρ0 / p_ρ0,

the straight-line time the inward fall of the start would take;
a remark of the paper extends the promise to starts with
p_ρ0 = 0, by first letting the flow run a moment. Theorem
5.5: the trap is also a portrait — the last arc of every
collision with an inner point of the segment satisfies the
cone condition: coming in, the direction of the momentum comes
to lie along the segment, and the cone is where all such endings
are found. A sharper clock (the paper's §5.1) bends the
straight bound down along a curve; it is proved under one
added condition on the acceleration, which the paper shows
holds for an constant density, and leaves open for the
rest (Q47). As taken in by: Paper 009 (Chapter 16).
# Off-policy evaluation — pricing a rule you did not run

A logging rule ran in the world and left a record: for
each round, a context (what was seen), the action the
logging rule took, and the reward that came back. A
second rule — the target — never ran. Off-policy
evaluation prices it from the record alone: the value of
the target rule is the average reward its choices would
have earned on the contexts the record holds (1.3.134).

Three families do the pricing. The DIRECT way fits a
reward model on the record and averages the fit under
the target rule. The WEIGHTED way weighs again each logged
reward by the importance weight (1.3.135) — how much
more, or less, the target rule would have chosen that
action than the logging rule did. The DOUBLY ROBUST way
does both at once: the fit prices most of each round,
and the weighted leftover — the part of the reward the
fit got wrong — corrects it; the score of that way is
the doubly robust score, and its two halves cover each
other's failures when one of them is right.

The paper's frame for the whole cluster: fit one reward
model per action, by gradient descent (1.3.136). Two
risks then stand apart. The target risk prices the fit
the way the target rule would meet it — the prediction
error, weighed by the own choices of the target rule. The
logged risk prices error on the record by its square,
weighed by the importance weights; the two differ by a term that
does not depend on the fit at all. And the price of the
pricing: the gap between the fitted model's value and
the true value is bounded by the target risk — the value
error is no larger than the square root of the
prediction error under the target rule. So pricing a
rule comes down to pricing a fit, under the own weighing of the rule itself.
Everything else in this cluster is about
where that pricing can honestly be done. As taken in
by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The held-out flaw — judged on less, chosen for all

The standard machine for judging a fitted model: split
the record, fit on one part, grade on the part held
back, then fit again on the whole record and ship. The
machine has a flaw that stays hidden while samples are
large against dimensions, and stands bare when the two
grow together (1.3.137): a model's risk depends on the
ratio of sample to dimension it was fitted at. The judge
therefore prices a model fitted at one ratio — nine parts in
ten of the record, for ten-fold splitting — and the
ship sails with a model fitted at another. The two risks
are different numbers, and their order over candidate
models can differ: a ranking that is right for the
smaller-sample models can be wrong for the full-data
ones, and the model fitted again then ships the worse
model, with the blessing of the judge.

The paper proves an instance with nowhere to hide: two
candidate stopping steps, rewards with no noise at
all, the nets of least width — ten-fold splitting ranks its fold
models rightly, its criterion lands above the starting
model's risk, and it chooses the start; the full-data
risk of the first step stands below the start, and the
loss for choosing wrong is a fixed 39/800 of risk that
does not shrink as the dimension grows. Smaller holdout runs
do not save the machine in the paper's runs: hold back a
share that itself shrinks (the square root of the
record, or its two-thirds power) and the judge is still
pricing a model fitted on less, and the
weaker for it — in the runs, worse than both the corrected
criterion and ten-fold splitting at every size. The flaw
is not noise in the grade. It is a mismatch of the thing
graded and the thing shipped, and no care in the grading
repairs it, because the grade is of another model. As
taken in by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The own-observation effect — a model graded on its own homework

Grade a fitted model on the same record that fitted it,
and the grade is bent. Each round of the record helped
shape the model; when the model is then asked to price
that very round, part of what it says is the own echo of that round
— its reward is already inside the prediction that
prices it. In the doubly robust score the bend has an
exact address: the sample average of the score splits into
the true value, a term that averages the noise of the score,
and a third term — the empirical-process term — that
carries the bend: how far the average of the fitted score on
its own fitting record stands from its average on fresh
rounds.

The classical theory makes that term vanish with two
demands: the fitted scores must settle inside one fixed
class of a bounded power to wiggle (a Donsker class), and their
error against the true score must fall to zero. In the
regime where sample and dimension grow together, both
demands fail. The prediction error need not fall at all
— the risk of a fitted network can stand away from zero for
good — and the paper shows the wiggle demand failing
on the simplest ground it can find: a family of straight-
line scores whose errors fall to zero evenly, in mean
square, at the rate of the dimension, while the largest
empirical-process term over the family tends to a
standing constant instead of zero. Small errors, spread
evenly, are not enough; the worst case remembers what the
average forgets.

The bend, priced in one instance: rewards constant, one
gradient step, the net of least width — the naive same-record
doubly robust price keeps an error of a fixed size that
does not shrink, and its mechanism is a self-term: each
own prediction carries a share of the own noise of that round,
proportional to the square of the length of its context. The
training leftovers — the grade with no correction at
all — are the effect at its most plain: in the paper's
runs they stand near 1.35 at every dimension while the
true risk falls by a factor of four. A grade that does
not move while the work improves is not a grade of the
work. As taken in by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The Onsager correction — subtracting a model's memory of its own data

Gradient descent walks: each step, every round of the
record pushes the weights, and the weights then read
every round again. The own pushes of a round are inside the
weights that price it — that is the own-observation
effect (1.2.112) at the level of the walk itself. The
Onsager correction removes them, after the fact, from
the own record of the walk: for each round and each step, the
corrected hidden state is the state the walk would have
held at that round if the own pushes of that round had never
landed — computed not by walking again without the round
(that is the leave-one-out fitting again, priced at
one walk per round) but by a recursion over the walk itself. The
recursion carries response coefficients: how much each
state answers to the pushes of each earlier step,
gathered into blocks — each step answers only to steps
before it — with determinant one,
so the correcting system is solved by walking forward,
never by inverting anything whole. The coefficients are
computed from the training walk alone: no fresh data, no
knowledge of the true rewards, no second walk. The cost
is work done after training, not a repeat of it.

The name the line takes from physics, by way of message
passing: the reaction term of Onsager — the part of a field
at a particle, the doing of the particle itself, named so
it can be taken away. Here the particle is a round of
the record, the field is the walk, and the subtraction
is exact in the limit the paper works in: the corrected
predictions stand where leave-one-out predictions stand.
The paper's check of exactly that: fit the nets again
with one round left out, and the distance of the
corrected prediction to that second fit falls — 0.177 down to 0.086 as the
dimension grows from 128 to 512 — while the raw
distance stands near 1.17, unchanged. The
machinery that says what the echo must be is state
evolution (17.1.2): the Gaussian shadow of the walk,
extended by this paper to carry the index of the target rule,
because the importance weight leans on the same context
the walk is reading. As taken in by: Paper 010
(Chapter 17).
# The corrected criterion — stopping the full-data walk by its own lights

Early stopping is a choice of where a walk ends, and
every way of choosing it is a judge. This paper builds
the judge out of the walk itself: per arm and per step,
the corrected criterion — the importance-weighted mean
of the squares of leftovers at the corrected predictions
(1.2.113), the leftovers of rounds priced by predictions
with their own echoes removed. Stop each arm at the
step where its criterion is least.

What the paper proves of this judge: it is uniformly
close — at every step of the walk at once — to the
conditional test risk of the very model it judges: the
risk of the full-data step on fresh rounds, reward noise and
all. That is the property the held-out judge cannot
have (1.2.111): the thing graded and the thing shipped
are the same model here. From closeness at every step,
the choice itself is near: the true risk of the chosen
step stands within a
vanishing term of the best step on
the walk — the selection is near-oracle, though the
oracle it nears is only the best stopping of this walk,
not the best model anywhere.

The same judge is spent two ways. The criterion is
weighed by the target rule: grade the walk by the rule
you mean to run, not by the rule that logged the data —
in the paper's runs, weighing cuts the chosen model's
risk by about 0.031 to 0.033 against the same criterion with
the weighing left off, for every target rule tried. And the judge
travels: inside ten-fold cross-fitting, where a judge of
some kind is called for, for each walk of a fold, corrected
stopping cuts the folds' chosen models' prediction error
by 19 to 42 percent against an inner held-out judge —
the correction paying rent even inside the machine it
replaces. One walk per arm, no second walks: the runs price it
at 5.5 to 8.9 times faster than cross-fitting with the
same judge inside. As taken in by: Paper 010
(Chapter 17).
# The corrected doubly robust estimators — ADM and ADR

The corrected predictions (1.2.113) buy more than a
stopping rule: they buy back the doubly robust price
itself, on the record that fitted the model, with no
split. Two estimators stand on them. ADM — the adjusted
direct price — averages the mean, under the target rule, of the
adjusted predictions: corrected on the own arm of that round,
where the echo lives; raw on the other arms, whose walks
never saw the round. It prices the FITTED model: the
value the model in hand would earn under the target
rule. ADR — the adjusted doubly robust price — adds the
importance-weighted mean leftover at the corrected
predictions, and prices the RULE ITSELF: the true value,
whatever model was fitted.

The theorem is the center of the cluster: both prices are
uniformly consistent over the whole grid of stopping
steps — so they hold for any stopping the data chooses,
including the stopping the corrected criterion
names — and the consistency of ADR does not ask the reward model's error to
vanish. That is the ground the classical doubly robust
theory cannot stand on without splitting: there, a
standing prediction error poisons the same-record price
through the own-observation effect (1.2.112); here, the
correction removes the echo and the price stands. The
two prices together measure the model: their difference
consistently prices the gap between the fitted model's
value and the true value — the record can say how far
the shipped model stands from the truth it serves,
without fresh data.

In the paper's runs the prices behave as proved: the
same-record doubly robust bias at dimension 2048 falls
from 0.0149 to 0.0006 under the correction — over 95
percent of it gone — and the bias intervals of ADR hold zero
at every size tried, in Gaussian and non-Gaussian
contexts alike, and under logging rules far from even.
ADM halves and better the error of pricing the fitted
model across arms (50.7 to 57.4 percent below the
cross-arm direct price at every dimension). What the
theorem does not yet give is the shape of the own wander
of ADR around the truth — that stands open (Q48). As
taken in by: Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# The oracle price of judging — the floor every estimator pays

Ask how well any price of the value of a rule can do, from a
logged record of a fixed size, and the classical theory
answers with a floor: the variance, over the rounds of the record,
rounds, of the doubly robust score at the TRUE reward
model, divided by the size of the record. No estimator
that plays by the rules of the record beats that floor for
long; the score at the truth is the own best reading of the record
summary of itself.

The paper's corollary walks its estimators down to that
floor. On a fresh round, the doubly robust score at the
chosen model has variance no more than the variance
of the floor plus a term priced by the chosen model's own
prediction risk — and that term is trapped between two
fixed multiples of the target risk, the multiples set by
how uneven the logging rule was. Choose a model whose
risk falls, and the variance of the score falls toward the
floor with it: good selection and good pricing are the
same purchase. In the paper's runs where the record grows
past the dimension, the error of ADR, measured in units
of the floor, stands at 1.000 — the floor itself, met:
the corrected same-record price does what the theory
says nothing can do better.

The floor also marks the honest edge of the cluster. What is
proved is that the prices close on their targets; what
is not proved is how the error of ADR is shaped around its
target — the wander of the corrected price, the
how its corrected scores lean on each other, the bell
law it might obey in the regime where sample and dimension grow
together. The classical limit (the record growing past the
dimension without bound) is named by the paper as the
first thing to recover. A floor for the price of judging
is not yet a theory of the judge (Q48). As taken in by:
Paper 010 (Chapter 17).
# Behavior-conditioned symbolic synthesis — a program that reproduces one behavior

Take a program that does a task right — the reference
program, written in a small language made for the field
(1.3.142) — and one answer somebody gave, wrong as it
happens to be. A large language model is shown the
language, the task, the reference program and that one
answer, and proposes small sets of changes to single
lines of the reference; each set is applied, the lines
the answer no longer hangs on are cut away, and the
changed program is run on the test input. It is kept
only if what it makes matches the answer it was gathered
around, to within a small allowance for noise. Fifteen
tries are made for each answer — five each at budgets of
two, four and eight changed lines. The model supplies
the guesses; running the program supplies the gate each
guess must pass. The search space of programs is far too
wide to walk whole; the guessing model walks to the part of it
worth testing, and the test is hard.

What the method returns for one answer is not one program
but a small set of them — every changed program that
reproduces the behavior — each one a witness, not yet the
rule the answer came from (1.2.123). Read together, the
witnesses are the finding: what they share is the shape
of the mistake (1.2.118-1.2.121); read across people and
machines in one such space, they carry the comparison of
1.2.122. Two turns of the standing ideas of this tracker
meet here. The reference program is a skeleton (1.2.57)
of the right rule — the structure of specification as
control, turned around: structure given not to steer a
maker but to read a mind. And the method is the
measurement agenda (1.2.14) at work — behavior read at
the level of the program that could have produced it,
not at the level of the score it earned. As taken in by:
Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# Errors live near the rule — wrong answers crowd around the right program

The first finding of the synthesis of Paper 011 is a
place, not a shape: wrong answers live near the right
answer in the space of programs. Taken together, 49.1
percent of the non-trivial wrong attempts of people on
the Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus are reproduced by a
program a few line changes away from the reference
program of the task — 52.5 percent on the training split,
46.2 on the evaluation split — with the least number of
changes averaging 2.84. The machine settings land in the
same neighborhood: 51.5 to 62.8 percent of their wrong
attempts are captured the same way. Against this stands
the walk no model guides: every single-line change of every
training reference program, more than sixteen million
candidates, reaches only 25.8 percent of the attempts —
about half of what the guided search reaches — and with
only fifteen samples per answer the guided search still
recovers 89 percent of everything that walk
finds. Depth is the reason: answers sit several changes
away, and the count of candidates grows past walking
whole.

So a wrong answer is not noise scattered over the space
of possible rules. It is a near neighbor of the truth,
and it carries news about the neighborhood of the truth: which
lines a solver changed, and which they left standing,
is the raw material every other concept of cluster P is
read from. The half that is not captured stands as the
the edge of the finding (Q53): the changes this method makes can
only replace lines, never add them, so answers that build
on the rule rather than shrink it are the ones it is
built to miss. As taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# The simplicity signature — the wrong rule is a smaller rule

Programs that reproduce human errors are shorter than
the reference programs they are changed from — and by
more than the pull toward the short that the method itself carries. The
method can only shorten or hold a program, never lengthen
it, so the check is run against control programs made by
the same method aimed at the correct answers. On the
training split, error programs run 19.1 percent shorter
than their references, control programs only 5.2 percent
shorter: 13.9 points of the shortening belong to the
errors themselves. On the evaluation split the same shape
stands larger — 24.3 against 2.7. And the shortening
grows with the size of the rule missed: about half a
point more for each line the reference program carries,
so a rule four times as long loses about ten points more
of itself to its errors.

Machines carry the same signature, graded by what they
can spend. Every setting tried shows error programs
shorter than its own controls, and the less a setting
earns, the shorter its errors run: across the five
settings, shortening and accuracy move against each other
hard (the paper's correlation is -0.80), each ten points
of accuracy buying about three points less shortening.
The reading the paper gives the signature, for both
species of solver: search is bounded, and a rule that
costs more to find or to check is traded for a smaller
one that still does much of the work — the simplicity
signature is the price of the search, showing. It is one
of the two signatures the satisficing reading of 1.2.120
stands on. As taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# Partial fits — rules that explain some of the evidence

A program kept for reproducing a wrong answer can also be
run on the examples the solver was shown, and there the
second signature stands. Of the modeled wrong attempts of
people, 20.9 percent are partial fits: programs that make
some of the shown examples right and miss the rest. A
further 1.7 percent fit every shown example and miss only
the test — different rules than the target, but as good
as the target on the evidence given, and wrong only where
the evidence ran out. The rest fit none of what was shown.
Among the partial fits, which examples a program fits is
not scattered. On the training split, 62.5 percent of them
form a shaped set — the first examples shown (41.0
percent), the last ones (17.0), or a block between — and
only the remainder fit a lone middle example or a scattered
set. The evaluation split shows the same shape, weaker
(48.5 percent shaped; 30.1 the first examples).

A reckoning that weighs all the evidence at once has no
order in it: the examples stand or fall together, in any
order. A search that takes the examples in the
order they stand, and stops when the rule in hand fits
enough of them, leaves exactly this shadow. The paper
reads the two signatures together — the simpler rule
(1.2.119), the partly-fitting rule — as satisficing
(1.3.144): a rule taken when it passes a good-enough
mark, not when it has been checked against everything
shown. What the record cannot yet separate is whether
the rule was formed from the early examples alone, or
formed whole and only checked against them (Q51). The
machines show the same kinds in their own proportions
(1.2.122). As taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# The kinds of deviation — a small set of ways to be wrong

Classified by how the kept program differs from the
reference, wrong answers fall into seven kinds and a
remainder. The target computed from the wrong feature —
the thing to be set is read off a simpler or louder
feature than the one the rule turns on — is the largest
kind by far: 45.2 percent of modeled human attempts. A
part of the program missing outright is next (partial
program, 11.9 percent), then the right parts given a
change from the wrong family (10.8). Four kinds each stand under five
percent: the right change worked on too few of the things
(scope restriction); the wrong member of the right family
chosen (operator confusion); the right steps in the wrong
order (sequencing); the right count off by one (boundary
precision). And 22.3 percent the classifier could not
place in any kind. The kinds were named in advance, the
naming done by a language model over the two programs and
their runs, the naming done five times for each program.

The kinds are not labels only: they carry how much of the
rule survives. The small local kinds — off by one, wrong
member — keep nearly the whole program; the missing-part
and wrong-family kinds keep the least, and their programs
are the shortest of the kinds (1.2.119). This is the
working claim of the cluster: a score reads differences
in degree between two attempts, and two attempts with the
same score can be wrong in different kinds; the kinds
read the difference in kind, and the difference in kind is
where the strategy of the solver shows. It is in the
kinds, not the rates, that people and machines are
compared at 1.2.122. As taken in by: Paper 011
(Chapter 18).
# Shared signatures, separate ground — people and machines, failing alike and apart

Put people and machines on the same tasks, in the same
program space, and the signatures of this cluster are
shared. The comparison is made on the 56 tasks where
people and every machine setting both went wrong. The
machines' wrong answers are captured by the synthesis at
the rates of people's or better (51.5 to 62.8 percent of
attempts, by setting). Their error programs run shorter
than their controls at every setting, more so the less
the setting can spend or earn (1.2.119). Their partial
fits grow with capability — 20.2 percent of the modeled
attempts of the weakest setting, 40.0 of the strongest — and
the dominant kind is the same wrong-feature kind as
people's, growing with performance: about 43 percent at
the weakest setting, about 64 at the strongest, against
people's 45.2.

And still the two can be told apart, and they fail on
different ground. A classifier over how the kept programs
differ from their references — which of the small
language's operations were gained and which lost —
separates human from machine errors within a task at 0.65
to 0.69 (the area under the curve the report card of
1.3.38 reads): clearly for the settings that earn less
than people (0.68 to 0.77), barely, and not surely above
chance, for the settings that earn more (0.57 to 0.63).
The signal is spread over many weak marks, not a few
operations. And task by task, success agrees only weakly
between people and each machine setting (0.30 to 0.34),
while the machine settings agree with each other far more
(0.49 to 0.82). One space holds both species of solver;
in it the shapes of failure match, and the ground failed
on does not. The paper's name for the difference:
the search itself leaning another way — inductive
biases, in its words — under signatures that the bounds
of search impose on both. This scroll is where the
revision of 1.2.4 lands: the jaggedness of machines is a
family shape of its own, not a copy of the human one. As
taken in by: Paper 011 (Chapter 18).
# Program aliasing — many programs make one behavior

A kept program is a witness, not the rule the solver
ran. For each human error the synthesis recovers a median
of two distinct programs that make the same wrong answer,
and the count runs to twelve. Distinct programs, one
behavior: that is aliasing, and the paper prices it
rather than waving at it. It built errors by hand —
programs changed in known ways, to known kinds (2,375
made, 148 checked by hand) — and ran the whole pipeline
on them. Run on the known programs themselves, the
classifier names the right kind 85.1 percent of the time:
the ceiling. End to end, the pipeline names it 64.9 to
76.4 percent, as the change budget grows from one to
three. Search failure is nearly none of the loss —
recovery rises from 84.5 to 99.3 percent over the same
budgets. What is lost is lost to aliasing: the search
finds a program that makes the behavior and reads as a
different kind, and the injected program itself is found
again only about two times in three.

So the whole of cluster P is read through a fog, and this
scroll is the price of the fog. Behavior leaves the
program open, as output leaves structure open (1.2.60):
no reading of a mind from its answers can be sharper than
the aliasing of the space it reads in. Two handles stand
open. The witnesses are not yet weighed against each
other — by length, or by what a mind would pay to run
them (Q52). And the fog itself is a property of the small
language the programs are written in: change the kit of
operations and the same behavior draws different
witnesses (1.3.142). As taken in by: Paper 011
(Chapter 18).
# Foundations — the level-zero layer (charter rule 9)
Built 2026-11-06 at Will's direction: everything in research.phext is
written so a reader with NO prior specialized knowledge can follow it.
This section is where the base definitions live, sucked in once,
defined in plain words. Rules of the layer:
- A foundation scroll defines ONE term (or one tight family).
- It may use only everyday words, or terms defined in foundation
  scrolls it points to under 'Builds on'. The chains bottom out at
  ordinary language: number, shape, space, same/different, more/less,
  rule, choice, cost. Those are assumed; nothing else is.
- Paper chapters and concept scrolls do NOT contain these definitions.
  Section 3 of each chapter (its standoff references) lists the
  foundation coordinates its claims need, in reading order. Follow
  the coordinates like hyperlinks.
General primitives: 1.3.2-1.3.8. Mathematics: 1.3.9-1.3.27.
Economics: 1.3.28-1.3.32. Computing & agents: 1.3.33-1.3.36.
Language & measurement: 1.3.37-1.3.39. Machine learning & hardware:
1.3.40-1.3.45. DEFINED WORDS (rule 10): from 1.3.46 on, one scroll per
word this phext coins — each scroll written in Singular words alone,
so the word it defines is the only word in it that is not already
Will's. All other scrolls may use a coined word once its scroll
stands here. Added with Study S001 (2026-11-06): foundations
1.3.73-1.3.78 — types and terms; context and judgment; computation
steps and still forms; paradox; universes; tactics and goals. The
Defined Words register grows by use: words coined after the first
register stand at 1.3.79 on (recursor, impredicative). Added with Studies
S002-S003 (2026-11-06): foundations 1.3.81-1.3.83 — ownership and
borrowing; symbolic execution; a tenant — and the coined word
tenant at 1.3.84. Added with Paper 006
(2026-11-07): foundations 1.3.87-1.3.91 — specification and
test; candidate class and goal set; prior and conditioning;
contract; interface and observability. Added with Paper 007
(2026-11-07): foundations 1.3.92-1.3.99 — attention and the
key-value cache; growth rates; the top-p set; tree; a summary
that stands for a group; the tangent; distillation; roofline —
and the coined words sublinear at 1.3.100 and subquadratic at
1.3.101.
# Foundation: function, variable, input, output
A FUNCTION is a rule that takes an INPUT and produces an OUTPUT:
given the same input, always the same output. A VARIABLE is a named
slot that can hold different values in different situations (x can be
3 today and 7 tomorrow; the rule using x does not change). A PARAMETER
is a variable whose value is chosen once to set up a situation, then
held fixed while other things vary. Nearly every claim in this phext
is, underneath, a claim about some function: what goes in, what comes
out, and how the output changes when the input does.
# Foundation: set and element
A SET is a collection of things, with no order and no repeats: the
set of the three colors on a flag. An ELEMENT is one thing in the set.
Much of mathematics is: pick a set, put structure on it (ways to
combine, compare or measure its elements), then ask what must be true
of everything with that structure. When a definition here says 'a set
with...', it means exactly that recipe.
Builds on: (everyday language only)
# Foundation: 'the same' — equal, isomorphic, homeomorphic, diffeomorphic
'Being the same' in mathematics is always being the same UP TO some allowed way of
reshaping, and arguments are often about which level is meant:
- EQUAL: literally the same object.
- ISOMORPHIC: the same structure — elements can be renamed so every
  rule and relationship matches exactly. (level of the Algebra.)
- HOMEOMORPHIC: the same shape if you may stretch, bend and squash,
  but not tear or glue. A coffee cup and a donut are homeomorphic
  (one hole each). (level of the Topology.)
- DIFFEOMORPHIC: homeomorphic, and moreover smoothly — no corners or
  kinks created or destroyed. The finest of these levels; two spaces
  can be homeomorphic but not diffeomorphic, and 4-dimensional space
  is where that gap gets strange (Paper 005 lives near this gap).
Builds on: set (1.3.3), shape (everyday).
# Foundation: invariant
An INVARIANT is a measurement you can make of an object that does not
change when the object is reshaped in the allowed ways (see 1.3.4).
Invariants are how being the same is tested in practice: if two objects
have different values of an invariant, they are NOT the same at that
level. The trap — it is Paper 005's whole subject — is that equal
invariants do NOT prove being the same: a weak invariant can agree while a
more fine one disagrees. Choosing an invariant is choosing how closely you
are willing to look.
Builds on: 'the same' (1.3.4).
# Foundation: integers, fractions, finite, infinite
INTEGERS (written Z) are the whole numbers: ..., -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, ...
Fractions (written Q) are fractions of integers: 3/4, -7/2, 5 (= 5/1).
Every integer is a rational; most fractions are not integers.
Measuring with Z keeps the fine print (exact counts, leftovers,
what is left over); measuring with Q makes smooth it away, because division is
always possible. A set or group is FINITE if it has a definite count
of elements, INFINITE otherwise. Paper 005's punchline, in these
terms: two manifolds can agree completely under Q-measurement and
differ under Z-measurement — the difference lives in finite leftover
structure (torsion, 1.3.13) that fractions erase.
Builds on: set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: vector and distance
A VECTOR is a list of numbers of fixed length: (3, 1, 4) is a vector
with three entries, called its COORDINATES or dimensions. Vectors let
a complicated thing be pinned down by several measurements at once —
a person's (height, weight, age) is a vector. Once things are vectors,
they live in a SPACE where DISTANCE makes sense: two vectors are
close if their entries are close. A huge amount of this phext works
by turning things (agents, stories, program parts) into vectors and
then asking about distances, clusters and averages of vectors.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: average, spread, percentile, distribution
Given many measurements: the AVERAGE (mean) is their sum divided by
their count. The SPREAD (dispersion, variance) is how far the values
typically sit from the average — same average, very different spreads
is a common and crucial situation. A PERCENTILE places one value
among the rest: the 71st percentile sits above about 71% of the
values. The DISTRIBUTION is the whole picture — which values occur,
how often. Claims like 'group A is more spread out than group B' are
claims about distributions, not about any individual member.
Builds on: (everyday counting).
# Foundation: dimension and manifold
The DIMENSION of a space is how many coordinates you need to say
where a point is, locally: a line is 1, a sheet of paper is 2, the
room you are in is 3. A MANIFOLD is a space that LOOKS like ordinary
flat space of some dimension if you zoom in far enough, even if its
global shape is curved or closed — the surface of the Earth is a
2-dimensional manifold: every small patch looks like a flat map,
though the whole is a sphere. Paper 005 is about 4-dimensional
manifolds: spaces that locally need 4 coordinates. Nobody can picture
one directly; the field works by cutting them into pieces that CAN be
handled (1.3.20) and keeping careful algebraic accounts (1.3.13).
Builds on: vector/coordinates (1.3.7), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: sphere, surface, genus, torus
A SPHERE S^2 is the surface of an ordinary ball: all points at one
fixed distance from a center. (S^1 is a circle, S^0 is two points.)
A SURFACE is a 2-dimensional manifold (1.3.9). Its GENUS is its
number of holes/handles: a sphere has genus 0, a donut (a TORUS) has
genus 1, a three handled shape has genus 3. Sigma_g means 'the surface of genus
g'. Genus is the oldest invariant in the subject: two closed surfaces
with different genera are never the same shape, and genus can be
counted from any picture of the surface.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9), invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: group
A GROUP is a set equipped with one operation that combines two
elements into a third, satisfying three plain requirements: combining
is order-stable (brackets don't matter), there is a do-nothing element
(identity: 'add 0'), and every element has an undoing (inverse: 'add
the negative'). Examples: the integers under addition; the rotations
of a square under 'do one then the other'; the ways to shuffle a deck.
Groups are how mathematics packages 'symmetry' and 'movement'. A
FINITE group has with a finite count many elements; its ORDER is how many. 'Z/n'
is the group of clock arithmetic with n hours: counting wraps around,
so n steps bring you back to the start — that wrapping around is the seed
of torsion (1.3.13).
Builds on: set (1.3.3), integers (1.3.6).
# Foundation: loops, fundamental group, universal cover, contractible
Stand on a space and walk a closed LOOP: start somewhere, wander,
return to the start. Two loops count as 'the same' if one can be slid
continuously into the other without leaving the space. Loops can be
combined (walk one, then the other) and undone (walk it backwards) —
so the loops of a space form a group, its FUNDAMENTAL GROUP, written
pi_1. pi_1 measures the hole of the space-structure as seen by paths: flat
land has trivial pi_1 (every loop slides to a point); the pi_1 of a donut
is Z x Z (loops around the two hole directions). A space is
CONTRACTIBLE if it can be continuously shrunk to a single point —
contractible spaces have trivial pi_1 and trivial homology (1.3.13).
Every space has a UNIVERSAL COVER: a contractible-ish space laid over
it like an opened out map, whose own pi_1 is trivial; the original
space's pi_1 is then visible as the symmetries of the unrolling.
Builds on: group (1.3.11), manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: homology — holes, counted by algebra; torsion
HOMOLOGY is a machine that gives to each space a list of groups
(H_0, H_1, H_2, ...), designed so that H_k detects 'holes of
dimension k': H_0 counts separate pieces; H_1 counts independent
tunnels (loops that can't be filled in); H_2 counts enclosed voids.
On a donut: H_1 = Z^2 (two tunnel directions, each usable any whole
number of times). The small raised mark-style part Z^n ('free' part) counts
unbounded directions. But an H_k can also contain a FINITE piece, like
Z/3: a cycle that is not a boundary, yet THREE copies of it are. That
finite leftover is called TORSION. Torsion is invisible if you only
measure with fractions (over Q it vanishes, since 3 becomes
that can be undone) — which is why integral (Z) homology knows strictly more
than rational (Q) homology. Paper 005's manifolds are told apart by
torsion alone.
Builds on: group (1.3.11), integers vs fractions (1.3.6), loops (1.3.12).
# Foundation: cohomology and ring
COHOMOLOGY is record of the homology keeping twin: instead of cycles in the
space, it tracks measurement-functions on the space (ways to assign
numbers to cycles consistently). For most counting purposes it holds
the same information as homology. Its extra feature is a
MULTIPLICATION: two measurements can be combined into a third, making
the cohomology groups into a RING (a system where you can add and
multiply, like ordinary numbers). 'The cohomology ring of S^2 x S^2'
therefore names a specific little multiplication table — and Paper
005's manifolds reproduce that table exactly under rational
measurement while differing in their integral homology. Same ring,
different torsion: that is the whole trick of a fake quadric.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), group (1.3.11).
# Foundation: Euler characteristic and signature (two summary numbers)
The EULER CHARACTERISTIC, written chi, is a single integer summarizing
the shape of a space, computable from any decomposition into pieces:
chi = (pieces in even positions) - (pieces in odd positions) — for a
surface, chi = 2 - 2 x genus (sphere 2, torus 0, genus-2 surface -2).
It is additive: the chi of a glued space is the sum of the chi values of the
parts. The SIGNATURE, written sigma, applies to 4-dimensional
manifolds: it counts, roughly, the balance between 'positive' and
'negative' directions in how surfaces inside the manifold cross
each other (the intersection form); sigma is
also additive under careful gluing (Novikov the adding rule). chi = 4 and
sigma = 0 are the values of S^2 x S^2 — Paper 005's sums match both,
as they must to fake the quadric.
Builds on: genus (1.3.10), invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: maps between spaces — continuous, embedding, diffeomorphism, orientation
A MAP from one space to another gives each point of the first a
point of the second. A map is CONTINUOUS if nearby points land nearby
(no teleporting, no tearing). An EMBEDDING is a continuous map that
places the first space inside the second without self-intersection —
a knot sitting in ordinary space is an embedded circle. A
DIFFEOMORPHISM is a smooth two-way map (it and its reverse create no
corners or kinks): the notion of exact being the same for smooth manifolds
(1.3.4). An ORIENTATION is a consistent choice of 'which hand it favors'
(clockwise vs counter-clockwise) across a space; gluing two pieces
together requires their orientations to DISAGREE at the seam
(orientation-reversing), the way two halves of a zipper face opposite
ways to close.
Builds on: 'the same' (1.3.4), manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: neighborhood, tubular neighborhood, normal direction
A NEIGHBORHOOD of something inside a space is a small region around
it, included in the space. If a surface sits inside a 4-manifold, a
TUBULAR neighborhood is the region of all points very close to the
surface — it has the shape of the surface made thick slightly in the
directions sticking OUT of it. Those sticking-out directions are the
NORMAL directions (at right angles to the surface, in the only sense
available): at each point of a surface inside a 4-manifold there is a
2-dimensional disk of normal directions, which is why the thick layer
is 'surface x disk' and its boundary is 'surface x circle'. Removing
a tubular neighborhood is the standard way to make a clean hole with a
well-defined boundary, ready for gluing (1.3.23).
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), maps (1.3.16), sphere (1.3.10).
# Foundation: bundle, fiber, section
A BUNDLE is a space built by placing a copy of one space (the FIBER)
over every point of another (the BASE), possibly twisting as you move
around the base. A brush: base = the handle, fiber = each hair of the brush.
Formally the total space maps onto the base, and the points over one
base point are one fiber. A SECTION is a choice of exactly one point
in every fiber, made continuously — a rule for picking a hair of the brush over
each point of the handle. Paper 005 uses this twice: the complement
of a multisection is a bundle over a surface whose fiber is a sphere
with holes; and its torsion class is the DIFFERENCE of two sections
(the two 'pole' sections, over the north of the sphere and south poles).
Builds on: maps (1.3.16), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: covering space and sheets
A COVERING space of a space X is a space laid over X so that every
small patch of X has several exact copies above it (the SHEETS), like
a many floored car park over one floor plan. The number of sheets is
the DEGREE of the cover. A cover is With no merging of sheets if every point of X has
exactly the full number of sheets above it (no merging floors). Going
around a loop in X can cycle the sheets — walk downstairs, and you
may come back on a different floor. That permutation behavior is what
'cyclic' and 'braided' mean in Paper 005: its surface is a p-with sheets
cover of the base surface whose sheets are moved in a cycle in a cycle around
certain loops, braiding the sheets into ONE connected surface instead
of p separate copies.
Builds on: maps (1.3.16), loops (1.3.12).
# Foundation: handle and handlebody decomposition
A HANDLE is a standard building block: take a ball and attach a thick
strap (in dimension terms, a lower-dimensional ball glued along part
of its boundary). A HANDLEBODY DECOMPOSITION describes a manifold as:
start with balls (0-handles), attach straps (1-handles), attach
thicker patches (2-handles), and so on, with a diagram recording each
attachment and a number (the FRAMING) recording how each strap is
twisted as it is glued. The counts (h_0, h_1, h_2, h_3) are the ingredient of the recipe list. Why it matters: a decomposition turns an
abstract space into something computable — homology (1.3.13) can be
read off the recipe by arithmetic (1.3.21), and two pieces can be
glued when their recipes share boundary data.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: chain complex — the arithmetic under homology
A CHAIN COMPLEX is the record keeping that makes homology computable.
From a handle decomposition (1.3.20), write one group per dimension:
C_3 -> C_2 -> C_1 -> C_0, where each C_k lists the k-handles, and
each arrow (the BOUNDARY OPERATOR) records how handles of one level
attach to handles of the next, with integer coefficients. The golden
rule is 'the boundary of a boundary is zero' (arrows compose to 0).
Homology is then pure algebra: H_k = (things the arrow out of C_k
kills) / (things hit by the arrow into C_k) — cycles modulo
boundaries. Torsion (1.3.13) appears when a cycle is hit only after
multiplication: 3 copies of it bound, but it doesn't. Given a
handlebody diagram, a patient person (or a computer) can compute all
of this by integer arithmetic alone.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), group (1.3.11), handlebody (1.3.20).
# Foundation: symplectic — geometry with an area rule; the symplectic sum
A SYMPLECTIC structure on a manifold is an added piece of geometry:
at every point, a consistent rule for measuring the 'area' of tiny
tiny flat shapes in pairs of directions — the mathematical form of the
position/momentum pairing in physics. Spaces carrying it are called
symplectic manifolds; they are always even-dimensional, and the rule
makes area-like quantities behave with no give (you cannot squash a
symplectic ball through a small hole, however you bend it). Most
4-manifolds in Paper 005 are symplectic. The SYMPLECTIC SUM is a
cut-and-paste construction: two symplectic manifolds containing the
same surface (with opposite normal directions, 1.3.17) have the neighborhoods of the surface removed and the resulting boundaries glued —
the splicing is done so the area rules still match. It is the main
known factory for exotic symplectic 4-manifolds.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), neighborhood (1.3.17), boundary (1.3.23).
# Foundation: boundary of a manifold; gluing along a boundary
Some manifolds have an EDGE: points whose neighborhoods look like
half-space instead of full space (the edge of a disk is its rim circle; the edge of a ball is its spherical surface). The edge itself is a manifold
of one dimension less, the BOUNDARY, written dM. Removing something's
tubular neighborhood (1.3.17) creates boundary where none was. Two
manifolds with the same boundary (up to being the same, 1.3.4) can be
GLUED: identify the boundaries by a chosen map, and the result is a
new closed manifold. The choice of gluing map is real data — different
choices can yield different manifolds from identical pieces, which is
why Paper 005 specifies its gluing maps explicitly instead of just
their effect on homology.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), neighborhood (1.3.17), maps (1.3.16).
# Foundation: aspherical; exceptional spheres and being minimal
A manifold is ASPHERICAL if its universal cover (1.3.12) is
contractible — which is the same as saying, it has no higher-homotopy 'bubbles': any
map of a sphere into it can be shrunk to nothing. Its whole topology
is then carried by pi_1. An EXCEPTIONAL SPHERE is an embedded sphere
whose self-intersection number is -1 (roughly: it links itself
negatively once); such a sphere can always be 'blown down' (removed
by a standard surgery), so a symplectic manifold containing none is
called MINIMAL — it is not a blown-up version of something smaller.
Paper 005's one-line consequence: in an aspherical manifold every
embedded sphere has self-intersection 0, never -1, so aspherical
implies minimal.
Builds on: universal cover (1.3.12), embedding (1.3.16), symplectic (1.3.22).
# Foundation: gluing groups — amalgamated product and trees of spaces
When two spaces are glued along a shared part, their fundamental
groups combine in a forced way: the AMALGAMATED PRODUCT takes both
groups and identifies the two copies of the shared group of the part
inside them (like merging two organizations while holding that the
shared department is one department, not two). The combined group
acts on a TREE (the Bass-Serre tree) whose vertices are copies of the
pieces and whose edges are copies of the shared part — a floor plan
of how the glued space's universal cover is assembled from
contractible blocks. Normal forms in such groups say every element
has an essentially unique spelling as an alternating product from the
two sides, which is what lets proofs track elements across the seam.
Builds on: fundamental group (1.3.12), group (1.3.11).
# Foundation: two computing tools — Mayer-Vietoris and Poincare duality
MAYER-VIETORIS is the standard divide-and-conquer for homology: if a
space is two pieces overlapping in a known part, there is a
prescription (an exact sequence) that computes the homology of the
whole from the homology groups of the pieces and the overlap — the
topological version of inclusion-exclusion in counting.
POINCARE DUALITY is a symmetry of closed manifolds: in dimension n,
the k-th homology and the (n-k)-th cohomology determine each other.
Consequence used in Paper 005: if H_1 is finite, duality forces H_3
of a closed 4-manifold to vanish, because H_3 is tied to cohomology in
degree 1, and finite groups contribute nothing there.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), cohomology (1.3.14).
# Foundation: contact manifold and Stein filling (brief)
A CONTACT structure on an odd-dimensional manifold is a field of
planes with maximum twisting — the geometry of wave waves and of
thermodynamics' special curves; a 3-manifold with one is a contact
3-manifold. A STEIN FILLING of it is a particularly well-behaved
4-dimensional manifold whose boundary is that contact manifold — a
filling the way a solid fills a shell. Paper 005 mentions them
because its complements, with one more piece removed, become Stein
filling spaces — including infinitely many that share ALL ordinary homology
yet are two by two different manifolds, told apart only by looking at
homology of their finite covers (1.3.19 run in reverse).
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), boundary (1.3.23), homology (1.3.13).
# Foundation: firm, worker, wage, outside option
A FIRM is an organization that hires inputs (labor, materials,
compute) and sells outputs. A WAGE is the price of the labor of a worker
per unit time. the OUTSIDE of A worker OPTION is the best they could get
if they leave — the wage they could earn elsewhere, or the value of
not working. No firm can hold a worker for less than their outside
option, and a worker whose skills are scarce gets paid above it —
the excess being a rent (1.3.31). Paper 001 treats AI systems rented
by the instance as a kind of worker whose wage is its rental cost.
Builds on: (everyday language; function (1.3.2) for prices as signals)
# Foundation: equilibrium
An EQUILIBRIUM is a situation in which every participant, given what
everyone else is doing, is doing the best they can — so nobody has
reason to change on its own side alone, and the situation rests. A market
equilibrium adds: at the resting prices, the amounts people want to
buy equal the amounts people want to sell (the market CLEARS).
Equilibrium is a consistency condition, not a claim that the outcome
is good, fair, or efficient — though theorems sometimes connect them.
Paper 001's results are equilibrium statements: 'in equilibrium, the
wages must be...' means 'any other wage pattern would give someone a
profitable move'.
Builds on: firm/wage (1.3.28).
# Foundation: mixing and the convex hull
Take points in a space. A MIX (convex combination) is a weighted
average of points: weights are positive and sum to 1, like a recipe
in proportions. The set of ALL mixes of a collection of points is
their CONVEX HULL — the smallest 'straight-edged' region containing
them all; picture a rubber band snapped around pegs on a board. If
the pegs are people's skill profiles (1.3.7), the hull is every
average profile a team drawn from those people can have, because the average of a team is exactly a mix of its members. Whether the best
mix sits strictly inside the hull or at a corner is the whole
question in Paper 001's mosaic argument.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: scarcity, rent, and the cost of a copy
A resource is SCARCE if there is not enough of it for everyone who
wants it at a zero price. RENT is the payment a scarce resource earns
above what is needed to keep it supplied: a uniquely talented singer
earns rent; an ordinary microphone doesn't, because more can be made.
The dividing line is COPY COST: if a thing can be copied at cost r,
no copy can command more than about r for long, because a competitor
can mint another. If it cannot be copied at any price — a particular
trained of the human brain — its earnings are set by bargaining over
scarcity. Paper 001's first great divide: human expertise is
that can not be copied (fixed supply, rents possible); AI expertise is that can be copied
at compute cost (rents capped by the copy cost).
Builds on: firm/wage (1.3.28).
# Foundation: threshold and phase transition
A THRESHOLD is a value of some quantity at which the behavior of a system
changes in kind, not just in degree. A PHASE TRANSITION is the
physical archetype: water heated degree by degree does nothing
qualitative until 100°C, then boils — the same input, a different
regime. Many claims in this phext are threshold claims: below the
threshold, step by step change; above it, a different structure. A
threshold claim is stronger (and more falsifiable) than 'more X leads
to more Y': it says the interesting action is at one point of the
scale, and it invites the question 'which side are we on, measured
how?'
Builds on: function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: program, runtime, state
A PROGRAM is text that specifies computation. A RUNTIME is the
machinery that carries a program out: loading it, giving it memory
and tools, stepping it forward. STATE is everything about a running
system that can change as it runs — the contents of its memory at a
moment. Two systems running the same program with different state
behave differently; two systems in the same state with different
programs do too. Paper 002's entire subject is the layer of program
that surrounds the fixed of a model core and shapes what its state becomes.
Builds on: function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: policy and mechanism
A POLICY is a rule for choosing what to do in each situation — the
strategy an agent follows. A MECHANISM is the fixed scaffolding that
makes policies run and constrains them: who may act, what gets
recorded, what is checked. The distinction that matters here: a policy
can be wise or foolish, but a mechanism is what remains true even when
a policy is foolish or hostile. Institutions, operating systems and
agent runtimes all make this split somewhere; Paper 002's
contribution is drawing the line sharply and making only one side
self-editable.
Builds on: program/runtime (1.3.33).
# Foundation: sandbox, version, rollback, append-only
A SANDBOX is a inside a fence area where a program can act without touching
the real system outside the fence — experiments inside can't break
things outside. A VERSION is a saved snapshot of some state or text,
kept under a name or number. ROLLBACK is returning to a saved version
after a change goes wrong; it is only trustworthy if the snapshot was
kept outside the reach of the thing being rolled back. APPEND-ONLY
means records can be added but never edited or deleted — a ledger in
ink. Append-only histories are how systems prove what happened, and
the foundation of Paper 002's safety story.
Builds on: state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: token, context, cache (how a language model reads)
A TOKEN is the chunk of text a language model reads or writes at one
step — roughly a short word or word-piece. The CONTEXT is the tokens
the model can currently see: its entire working memory, limited to a
fixed number of tokens (the context window). Work the model did
earlier falls out of view unless something writes it down and brings
it back — that 'something' is memory machinery around the model, and
choosing what to keep in view is a policy (1.3.34). A CACHE is stored
earlier computation reused instead of computed again; for models, a high
share of cached input tokens means most of the context was already
processed, so the step is cheap. Costs in agent systems are roughly
proportional to not cached tokens processed.
Builds on: program/state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: corpus, prompt, feature
A CORPUS is a collection of texts (or other items) assembled for
study. A PROMPT is the instruction or opening given to a writer or a
model, to which it responds with a complete piece of work. A FEATURE
is one measured property of an item, stated so different items can be
compared: 'number of side plots', 'does the narrator address the
reader'. Turning an item into a list of features turns it into a
vector (1.3.7) — after which distances, clusters and rarity (1.3.39)
become arithmetic. What a study can see is bounded by its features:
unmeasured properties are invisible to it.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: classifier and its report card (accuracy, F1)
A CLASSIFIER is a machine (or rule) that sorts items into labeled
bins: given the features of an item, it guesses which source or class the
item came from. Its REPORT CARD compares guesses to truth. Accuracy
is the share of all guesses that are right. F1 is a more strict grade
balancing two failure modes per class: missing real members
(recall) and wrongly claiming members (precision); MACRO-F1 averages
the per-class F1 grades so small classes count as much as big ones.
A classifier far above the guess-at-random rate proves the features
contain real information about the classes — it does not, by itself,
prove anything about why.
Builds on: feature (1.3.37), average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: nearest neighbors, distance, rarity
Once items are vectors (1.3.7), each item has NEIGHBORS: the items
closest to it in the space. The k NEAREST NEIGHBORS of an item are
the k closest such items, and the average distance to them measures
how crowded the neighborhood of the item is. RARITY, as Paper 003 uses it,
is exactly that: an item is rare if its nearest neighbors are far
away — its combination of measured properties is uncommon in the
collection. Rarity is always relative to the collection and the
features: add items or change features and the same rarity of the item
changes.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), average (1.3.8), feature (1.3.37).
# Foundation: neural network, weights, training
A NEURAL NETWORK is a function (1.3.2) built from many simple units
whose combined behavior is set by a large list of adjustable numbers,
its WEIGHTS (or parameters). TRAINING is the process of adjusting the
weights — by automated trial, error measurement and correction — so
the outputs of the network match desired outputs on training examples.
Training is expensive and slow; using the trained network (inference)
is comparatively cheap. The weights are the entire of the network learned
knowledge, stored as numbers; two networks with the same design but
different weights are different machines. Several papers here hold
weights fixed and vary everything AROUND them — the interesting
action is deliberately elsewhere.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), vector (1.3.7).
# Foundation: graph — nodes, edges, and program graphs
A GRAPH is a set of NODES (things) connected by EDGES (relationships
between pairs of things): cities and roads, people and friendships,
instructions and dependencies. Degree, paths and neighborhoods are
defined as you'd expect. A GRAPH NEURAL NETWORK (graph network) is a neural
network (1.3.40) whose computation follows the edges of a graph, letting
the representation of each node absorb information from its neighbors. For
programs, the standard graph is the control/data-flow graph: nodes
are instructions and values, edges record 'executes next' and 'feeds
into'. Paper 004 predicts hardware behavior from program graphs.
Builds on: set (1.3.3), neural network (1.3.40).
# Foundation: generalization and domain shift
A model GENERALIZES if it performs well on items it was not trained
on. A DOMAIN is a family of similar items (the problems of one benchmark;
one program; the scans of one hospital). DOMAIN SHIFT is what happens when
the test items come from a different domain than the training items:
the surface changes while the underlying task doesn't, and models
often fail badly. Domain generalization is the discipline of building
models whose performance survives the shift. The core dispute in
Paper 004: whether to survive shift by ERASING domain differences
from the view of the model, or by KEEPING the differences and routing each
new item to specialists that recognize its parts.
Builds on: classifier (1.3.38), neural network (1.3.40).
# Foundation: surrogate model
A SURROGATE is a cheap, fast stand-in for an expensive, slow
measurement: predict the result instead of running the real thing.
Wind-tunnel models are stand ins for flight; a spreadsheet budget is
a surrogate for a year of business. A surrogate is useful when its
errors are small compared to the cost it saves, and when the final
decision still checks the few best candidates the expensive way.
Its quality is judged twice: how close its predictions are (offline),
and how good the decisions made from them turn out (online). Paper
004's stand ins predict chip performance in milliseconds where the
real measurement takes hours.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), classifier report cards (1.3.38).
# Foundation: FPGA, kernel, pragma, latency (the high level synthesis stage, plainly)
An FPGA is a chip whose circuits can be rewired after manufacture —
hardware you can reprogram. HIGH-LEVEL SYNTHESIS (high level synthesis) is a tool that
compiles an ordinary program into such circuits. In this vocabulary a
KERNEL is the program (in a confusing way, the same word means other things
elsewhere; here it is a piece of C++ to be turned into circuitry). A
PRAGMA is an instruction written into the program telling the compiler
how to build the circuit: run these steps in parallel, pipeline
those, tile this loop. The same kernel with different pragma instructions becomes
circuits of wildly different speed and size. LATENCY is how long the
circuit takes (in cycles); resource use is how much chip it occupies.
Finding good pragma instructions by trial requires running high level synthesis each time — hours
per trial — which is why Paper 004 predicts instead (1.3.43).
Builds on: program (1.3.33), surrogate (1.3.43).
# Foundation: regularization and fine-tuning (two training habits)
REGULARIZATION is any deliberate pressure added during training to
keep a model from a degenerate solution: a penalty for being one sided,
for complexity, for being too sure — chosen to protect a property the
raw score doesn't reward. Paper 004 adds a balance pressure for BALANCE: experts
that are never used can't specialize. FINE-TUNING is training an
already-trained model a little more, on a small amount of new data,
to adapt it to a new domain — cheap compared to training from
scratch, and the standard first move under domain shift (1.3.42).
Both are admissions that the headline objective alone doesn't capture
what's wanted.
Builds on: training (1.3.40), domain shift (1.3.42).
# Defined Words — the register (charter rule 10)
Words this phext needs that are not in Will's Singular list live here,
one per scroll, each defined in a scroll written in Singular words
alone (the word being defined, and the forms named in its first line,
are the only words in the scroll that are not already Will's). A word
may be used in the rest of the phext because its scroll stands here.
Names need no scroll: they are addresses, not speech.
# Defined word: homology
Homology is the algebra that counts the holes of a space. It gives a space a list of groups. The first group counts the separate pieces. The next counts the tunnels: loops that can not be filled in. The next counts the closed empty rooms inside. A part of the answer can be finite: a loop that is not the edge of anything, though three copies of it are. That finite part is the part that measuring with fractions can not see.
# Defined word: cohomology
Cohomology is the twin record keeping of the hole counting algebra. In place of cycles in the space, it keeps the ways to give numbers to cycles so the numbers agree. It holds the same news as the hole counting algebra for most counting. It adds one thing: two such ways can be multiplied to make a third, so the groups form a ring, a system where you can add and multiply.
# Defined word: symplectic (also: symplectically)
A space is symplectic when it carries an added rule: at each point, a way to measure the area of tiny flat shapes in pairs of directions, the same pairing of place and push that physics uses. The rule is strict: you can bend the space, but the rule makes it impossible to press a ball of the space through a hole smaller than the rule allows. A symplectic sum joins two such spaces along a shared surface so the rules still match.
# Defined word: aspherical
A space is aspherical when every map of a sphere into it can be shrunk to nothing. The covering space that opens all its loops out flat is then a space that shrinks to a point. All of its shape news is carried by its loops alone.
# Defined word: genus
The genus of a surface is its number of holes, counted as handles. The surface of a ball has genus zero. A donut has genus one. A shape with three handles has genus three. Two closed surfaces with unlike genus are never the same shape.
# Defined word: quadric (also: quadrics)
A quadric, in this phext, is the product of two spheres: each point is a pair, one point from the first sphere and one from the second. It is the standard four dimensional space that the manifolds of Paper 005 copy in every test that uses fractions, and fail to be in fact.
# Defined word: multisection (also: multisections)
A multisection is a surface sitting inside a product space, spread over the base surface as a cover with p sheets. The sheets are joined into one surface when travel around a loop of the base cycles them. The multisections of Paper 005 are cyclic: one loop sends sheet one to sheet two, sheet two to sheet three, and the last back to the first.
# Defined word: handlebody (also: handlebodies)
A handlebody is a space given as a building recipe: balls first, then straps, then thicker patches, each joined along part of its edge, with a number for each strap that tells how it twists as it is joined. The list of counts of each kind is the bill of parts of the recipe. From the recipe alone, the hole counting of the space can be worked out by whole number arithmetic.
# Defined word: gluing (also: gluings)
Gluing is the joining of two spaces along a shared edge or surface, by a chosen map that says which point of one side meets which point of the other. The choice of map is real: two gluings of the same pieces by unlike maps can give unlike spaces.
# Defined word: diffeomorphism (also: diffeomorphisms)
A diffeomorphism is a map between two smooth spaces that goes both ways and makes no corners or kinks in either direction. It is the most strict of the same shape tests used here: spaces joined by one are the same smooth space.
# Defined word: diffeomorphic
Two spaces are diffeomorphic when a map joins them that goes both ways and makes no corners or kinks in either direction: they are the same smooth space, point for point.
# Defined word: homeomorphic
Two spaces are homeomorphic when one can be stretched, bent and squashed into the other with no tearing and no joining, and back again. This is a less strict same shape test than the smooth one: spaces can pass it and still fail the smooth test.
# Defined word: contractible
A space is contractible when it can be shrunk, inside itself, to a single point, the way a ball of clay can be pressed down without being torn. Such a space has no holes of any kind.
# Defined word: hull
The hull of a set of points is the smallest straight edged region that holds them all: the shape a rubber band takes around pegs on a board. Every weighted average of the points lies in the hull, and every point of the hull is such an average.
# Defined word: centroid
The centroid of a set of points, or of a cluster, is its center point: the average of all the points, each given the same weight. Distances to a centroid say how spread out the set is around its own middle.
# Defined word: seam
A seam is the surface or line along which two pieces are joined. When two pieces are joined by a chosen map, the seam is where the map does its work, and how the pieces meet at the seam decides what the joined space is.
# Defined word: framing (also: framings)
A framing is the twist number of a strap in a building recipe: as a handle is glued down, it may be turned some whole number of times, and that number is part of the recipe. Unlike framings of the same recipe give unlike spaces.
# Defined word: gating
Gating is the choosing, for each input, of how much weight each of several expert parts will have in the answer. A gate is the small part that makes the choice, after looking at the input. The weights add to one, so the answer is a weighted average of the experts.
# Defined word: polarization
Polarization, in a mixture of experts, is the collapse of the choosing: the gate sends nearly all weight to the one expert that got ahead first, that expert gets nearly all the training, and the gap grows until the mixture is one expert and dead weight. It is a rich get richer failure, and it must be trained against.
# Defined word: supercooperation
Supercooperation is the state of a system of agents whose working together has crossed the line where growing the group makes each added member worth more, not less. Below the line, groups hit a ceiling on their size. Above it, returns grow with scale, and the ceiling breaks.
# Defined word: substructure (also: substructures)
A substructure is a part of a structure that keeps its own pattern: a block of a program, a turn of a story, a piece of a space. Two large unlike things can share substructures, and what is learned on one can be carried to the other through the shared part.
# Defined word: granularity (also: granularities)
Granularity is the size of the parts you choose to look at: the grain. One program can be read at the grain of single steps, of blocks, or of the whole. A claim at one granularity need not hold at another, and some machines keep a separate expert for each grain.
# Defined word: jagged (also: jaggedness)
A profile is jagged when it is strong in one direction and weak in another, and the pattern of strong and weak is its own. Two jagged profiles can fit together like teeth: the weak place of one is the strong place of the other. That fit is the root of the mosaic effect.
# Defined word: coasean
Coasean names the line of thought from Coase: a firm grows until one more step inside costs as much as buying that step in the market. The ceiling this sets on firm size is the coasean ceiling.
# Defined word: ricardian
Ricardian names the line of thought from Ricardo: a thing that can not be copied earns by its scarcity. Its owner can charge above the cost of keeping it in use, and the excess is a rent that copying would destroy.
# Defined word: amalgamated
An amalgamated product is a group built by joining two groups along a shared part: the two copies of the shared part are made into one, and nothing else is forced. It is how the loop group of a joined space is built from the loop groups of the pieces.
# Foundation: types and terms
A TYPE is a kind label: 'a whole number', 'a true-or-false claim',
'a function from numbers to numbers'. A TERM is a thing that
carries one: the number 3, a proof, a function. One set of building
rules makes both — a term is itself a type when its own label is a
universe (1.3.77). The whole language of terms of MiniLean4 has
five kinds: a universe Sort u; a name; a Pi type (for each input of
one type, a result of a type that may depend on the input); a
lambda (a function built from a bound name); and an application
(one term put to another). Paper chapters add one more kind: a
constant, a name declared once for the whole system.
Builds on: 1.3.2 (function, input, output), 1.3.3 (set).
# Foundation: context and judgment
A CONTEXT is an ordered list of givens: names, each with its type,
where a later type may use the names before it. A JUDGMENT is a
claim made under a context: 'under these givens, this term has
this type'. The typing rules (1.2.42) say which judgment claims hold.
A judgment with an empty context is a claim that stands on its
own, with nothing given.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms).
# Foundation: computation steps and still forms
A term COMPUTES by steps of three kinds: beta (a function applied
to an input steps to its body, with the input put in place of the
bound name — putting in must not let a name be caught by a binder
of the same name inside; the system renames first, and getting
this wrong is where checker bugs hide); delta (a name with a
definition opens up to its definition); iota (a recursor (1.2.44)
applied to a built value runs the case for that builder). A term
to which no step applies is STILL (a normal form). Two terms are
equal by computation when both compute to the same still form,
up to renaming of bound names. A working checker puts a cap on
the steps (its fuel; MiniLean4: 20,000) so a bad term stops with
an error in place of running forever.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms), 1.3.4 ('the same').
# Foundation: paradox
A PARADOX is a build inside a system that makes the system prove
a claim and its opposite — after which the system proves anything
and is worth nothing. The paradox of this study: if one universe
held itself ('Type : Type'), a term can be built (Girard, made
smaller by Hurkens) that proves 'for every proposition P, P' —
the empty claim of 1.2.47 filled. The shape of the trick: a
collection that ranges over all types while being one lets it
hold a copy of its own double power set, a thing no set can do;
the load is carried by well-founded families that are, all of
them, below a family that is itself one of them.
Builds on: 1.3.3 (set), 1.3.77 (universes).
# Foundation: universes (floors of types)
A UNIVERSE is a type whose terms are types: Sort 0 holds the
propositions, Sort 1 holds Sort 0 and the small types (Nat lives
here), Sort 2 holds Sort 1, and so on — floors of a building, each
holding the one below, no floor holding itself (a floor that held
itself is the paradox of 1.3.76). To say which floor a function
type lives on, the imax rule: if the result lives on floor 0, the
function type lives on floor 0 too, whatever its input ranges
over; else it lives on the higher of the two floors.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms).
# Foundation: tactics and goals
A GOAL is a judgment still to be proved: a context, a target type,
and a hole where the proof term will go. A TACTIC is a step that
fills a hole with a term — possibly a term with new holes, which
become new goals. In MiniLean4 the tactics are: intro (the target
is a function type; take its input as a given and prove the rest),
exact (put in a finished term), apply (put in a function and leave
its inputs as goals), change (restate the target as one equal to
it by computation), rfl (close a goal of Eq when its sides compute
the same), induction (run the recursor of a given on the goal),
rw (rewrite the goal by an equation), and qed (send the whole
built term to the kernel). The soundness of the system rests on
qed alone: tactics only propose, the kernel decides.
Builds on: 1.3.74 (context and judgment), 1.3.75 (computation
steps).
# Defined word: recursor (also: recursors)
A recursor is the one function a system like MiniLean4 builds for
each inductive type: it takes what is to be shown or built for
each value of the type, one small case for each way of building a
value, and the value itself, and it walks the value by its
building steps. With a claim as its aim it is induction; with a
value as its aim it is recursion. One shape serves both.
# Defined word: impredicative
A floor of types is impredicative when a type on that floor may
range over the whole floor, itself counted in, and stay on the
floor. In MiniLean4 only floor 0, the propositions, is
impredicative: 'for every proposition P' is itself a proposition.
One such floor is safe; two would let the paradox in, for a type
could then be looked inside as data.
# Foundation: ownership and borrowing
In Rust, every value has exactly one OWNER. A value may be
BORROWED in two ways: shared (many readers at once, no writer) or
mutable (one borrow at a time, and while that borrow lives no
other road to the value exists — not even its owner). When a
borrow ends, the road returns to the owner. A checker in the Rust
compiler (the borrow checker) refuses any program that breaks
these rules, before the program runs. The rules are what Aeneas
(Study S002) stands on: because a mutable borrow is the only road
to its value, the value can stand in for the borrow (1.2.49).
Builds on: 1.3.33 (program, runtime, state).
# Foundation: symbolic execution — running on stand-ins
To run a program SYMBOLICALLY is to run it with stand-ins in
place of its values: where the program would hold the number 3,
it holds 'some whole number, call it n', and where it would
branch, the running follows both arms with the condition of each
written down. The result is not one run but the shape of all
runs. Checking a program this way can find what no set of test
runs reaches; carrying a program into another language this way
is how Aeneas works (1.2.52).
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms), 1.3.2 (function).
# Foundation: a tenant
In a system shared by many customers, a TENANT is one customer's
sealed region: their rows, their files, their names — present in
the same store as those of every other tenant, and kept apart by
the rules of the system. That tenant A can not read the rows of
tenant B is the kind of claim the framework of Study S003 proves
of its kernel (1.2.53): the seal is a theorem, not a hope about
careful code.
Builds on: 1.3.33 (program, runtime, state).
# Defined word: tenant (also: tenants)
A tenant is one customer of a system that many customers share,
counted as a sealed region of the system: the rows, files and
names of the tenant are in the same store as those of every other
tenant, and the rules of the system keep them apart. To say a
system is safe for its tenants is to say no tenant can reach into
the region of another.
# Foundation: state machine
A STATE MACHINE is a thing whose behavior depends on which of a
set of named states it is in. It starts in one of the states.
When an input arrives — a signal, a request, a step of work —
rules say what it does and which state it moves to next. The
same input does different things in different states: a door
that is locked stays shut when pushed, and a door that is open
swings. What the thing does is never fixed by the input alone;
it is fixed by the input together with the state it is in.

A state machine is EXPLICIT when its states and its rules are
named outright, where they can be read and checked. It is
IMPLICIT when no such naming exists but the machine is present
anyway, carried in how the thing is built: a kept value that
picks which behavior runs, several kept marks that are read in
combination, or the place a running program has reached in its
flow — each of these is a state that was never given a name.
Making an implicit machine explicit means finding the states
it already has and writing the rules down. Charter rule 13
(1.1.1) sends this tracker looking for them.
Builds on: state (1.3.33), computation steps and still forms (1.3.75).
# Defined word: refuted
A claim stands refuted when a receipt speaks against it: a
recorded clash, a check that failed, a counter-case. The word
names the ground on which a claim stands False in this phext
(charter rule 14, at 1.1.1): the claim is kept, with the receipt
that speaks against it, and is never quietly dropped.
# Foundation: specification and test
A SPECIFICATION is a written statement of what would count as
acceptable: what a thing must do, or be, for the one asking to
take it. A TEST is a specification cut down to one case that
can be checked with no room between pass and fail: give this
input, expect that output. A set of tests is a specification
made of cases, and it is silent about every case it does not
name. A specification can also take other shapes — rules over
whole classes of cases, or statements about the inside of a
thing (1.3.90, 1.3.91) — and the shapes differ in what they can
say and in what they leave open.
Builds on: function, variable, input, output (1.3.2).
# Foundation: candidate class and goal set
Name all the programs an agent could in principle produce: that
whole set is the field of CANDIDATES. Inside it sits the GOAL
SET: the programs the one asking would accept. When part of the
answer is fixed in advance — this shape of data, these parts,
these boundaries — only the programs sharing the fixed part
remain in play; that smaller set is a CANDIDATE CLASS. Questions
of the form 'how much more must be said to land inside the goal
set?' are always asked inside one candidate class, and their
answers can change completely from one class to the next.
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3), program (1.3.33).
# Foundation: prior and conditioning
A PRIOR is a spread of belief over a set of candidates before
new evidence: each candidate carries a share, the shares add
to one, and a bigger share means more expected. CONDITIONING
is what evidence does to the spread: cross out every candidate
the evidence rules out, and re-share what is left among the
survivors in the same proportions as before. The spread after
the evidence is the POSTERIOR. Conditioning only ever removes
candidates; it cannot move belief back to a crossed-out
candidate, and among the survivors it never changes who stood
ahead of whom.
Builds on: set and element (1.3.3), average, spread, distribution (1.3.8).
# Foundation: contract — promises at a boundary
A CONTRACT, at the boundary of one part of a program, states
what the part asks and what it promises. What it asks: what
must hold when the part is called — the inputs it can take,
the state it expects to find. What it promises: what will hold
when it returns — the result it gives, the state it leaves
behind. A contract is LOCAL when it binds one part. An
INVARIANT (1.3.5) is a promise of the wider kind, binding many
parts at once. A part that keeps its contract can be relied on
by those who call it and never look inside it, and in the best cases
whether a contract is kept can be checked by machine.
Builds on: function, variable, input, output (1.3.2),
invariant (1.3.5), program, runtime, state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: interface and observability
The INTERFACE of a part is the named surface where the rest of
the world touches it: the values it takes in, the values it
gives back, and the middle values it lets others name. A value
that lives inside a running program but is named at no
interface is HIDDEN STATE. A computation is OBSERVABLE at a
point when the values there can be read from outside — by a
test, say — before later steps cover them up. What no interface
names cannot be tested directly; it shows itself only by what
it does to the final output, if anything it does survives that
far.
Builds on: program, runtime, state (1.3.33),
function, variable, input, output (1.3.2).
# Foundation: attention — how a model weighs what it has read
When a language model writes its next token, it weighs the tokens
already written: a few count for much, most for almost nothing.
That weighing is attention. From each earlier token the model has
made two things: a KEY, which says what the token can be found by,
and a VALUE, which is what the token hands over when it is used.
The token now being written makes a QUERY — a question in the same
shape as a key. The model scores the query against every key, turns
the scores into shares that add to one, and takes the share-weighted
average of the values as the contribution of the past to the
next token.
In writing one token at a time, earlier keys and values do not
change, so they are kept — the KEY-VALUE CACHE — and each step adds
only its own. The cache is the cost: every new token is scored
against every key in it, so the work of one step grows in a straight
line with the length, and the work of the whole text grows with the
square (1.3.93).
Builds on: token, context, cache (1.3.36), average, spread,
distribution (1.3.8), function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: growth rates — straight line, square, and power laws
Costs grow, and the shape of the growth decides what is possible at
large sizes. A cost grows in a STRAIGHT LINE when doubling the size
doubles the cost; it grows with the SQUARE when doubling the size
makes the cost four times as large. Between and beyond those steps
sit the POWER LAWS: the cost grows as the size raised to a power,
its EXPONENT — exponent 1 is the straight line, exponent 2 the
square, and an exponent under 1 is growth slower than the straight
line: the bigger the size, the smaller the share of it the cost
takes. Growth slower than the straight line is called sublinear
(1.3.100); growth slower than the square, subquadratic (1.3.101).
Builds on: integers, finite, infinite (1.3.6), function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: the top-p set — the few keys that carry the mass
The shares of attention (1.3.92) form a spread over the keys: most
keys hold almost none of the mass. Order the keys by share, largest
first, and take keys until the shares taken add to a chosen part p
of the whole — 90 percent, say. The keys taken are the TOP-P SET
for that query: the few that carry the mass. What is left together
holds 1 minus p of the mass, so dropping it moves the final average
only a little. Every sparse-attention method makes this trade; they
differ in how they find the set without scoring every key.
Builds on: attention, keys, values, cache (1.3.92), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: tree — groups inside groups
A tree is a way of holding a set in groups inside groups. The set
is split into chunks; the chunks are grouped into larger chunks;
and so on up to one group that holds all. Each group is a NODE; the
groups directly inside it are its CHILDREN; the group that directly
holds it is its PARENT; the smallest pieces are the LEAVES; the one
top group is the ROOT. A node sits at a LEVEL — how many rounds of
grouping up from the leaves it sits — and the number of levels is the tree's
DEPTH. The use of the shape: start at the top, and go down only
into the groups that look worth opening.
Builds on: set (1.3.3), graph (1.3.41).
# Foundation: a summary that stands for a group
A summary of a group is a small stand-in, computed from the group's
members, used in their place for a first look. A summary is judged
by one test: the first look it gives should match the look the
members themselves would give. A good summary lets the reader skip
the members that do not matter and open only the groups that do. A
summary of summaries is a summary one level up (1.3.95). A summary
is not the group: what it leaves out, it cannot give back.
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: the tangent — a straight line that touches a curve
Take one chunk of keys and let the query move. The total pull of the chunk
pull on the query — the sum of its keys' scores, logged — traces a
curve: smooth, and rounded the same way everywhere, never sharply bent.
A TANGENT is a straight line that touches such a curve at one
chosen query and hugs it near that query. A handful of tangents,
each touching at a different chosen query, can stand in for the
whole curve: at a new query, read the highest of the lines as the
the height of the curve there. Near one of its touching points a line is
nearly exact; far from all of them it only bounds. Lines are cheap
to keep and quick to read — that is their use (1.2.68).
Builds on: function (1.3.2), attention, keys, values, cache (1.3.92).
# Foundation: distillation — teaching a part to copy a whole
Distillation is teaching one small part to behave as a larger,
already-trained whole behaves, without training the whole again.
The student is shown the teacher's outputs and changed until its
own outputs match them; the size of the mismatch between two
spreads of shares is measured by a gap (the KL gap), and training
drives the gap down. Only the student is trained — the teacher is
frozen — so distillation adds a small trained part to a finished
system in a short run, at the price that the part keeps only what
the matching forced it to keep.
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8),
neural network, weights, training (1.3.40).
# Foundation: roofline — a reckoning of speed from two ceilings
A roofline estimate reckons how long a piece of computing takes
from two ceilings: how fast the machine can do arithmetic, and how
fast it can move numbers between its memory and its arithmetic.
The time is at least the larger of the work divided by the first
ceiling and the numbers moved divided by the second — plus, on
real machines, the cost of starting many small pieces of work,
which arrive in waves and waste the edges of each wave. A roofline
number is a reckoning, not a measurement: it says what the time
would be if the machine behaved exactly as the ceilings say. Where
the picture the reckoning holds of the work is wrong, the number is wrong
with it, however careful the arithmetic.
Builds on: FPGA, kernel, pragma — latency named (1.3.44),
function (1.3.2).
Defined word: sublinear
sublinear, slower-than-the-line (word coined by this phext,
Paper 007, 2026-11-07): of a growth, slower than straight-line
growth — double the size, and the amount grows by less than double;
the bigger the size, the smaller the share of it the amount takes.
See 1.3.93.
Defined word: subquadratic
subquadratic (word coined by this phext, Paper 007, 2026-11-07):
of a growth, slower than the square of the size — double the size,
and the amount grows by less than four times. Attention that costs
less than the square of the context length is subquadratic
attention. See 1.3.93.
# Foundation: memory and address
A computer's MEMORY is a long row of places, each holding
one BYTE, a small piece of data. Each place has a number,
its ADDRESS, and to read or write a place is to name its
address. Places are used in runs: a WORD is a run of bytes
taken together — 4 bytes in the world of Chapter 12 — and
sizes are counted in whole words, so a thing of 5 bytes takes
8 bytes of room and a thing of 10 takes 12. A REGION is a run
of places set aside for one kind of use. A SEGMENTATION
VIOLATION is the system's stop when a program tries to write
a place it may only read, or to run from a place that holds
no code: the run ends there.
Builds on: state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: register and pointer
A REGISTER is one of the few named places inside the
processor itself, each holding one word, read and changed
faster than any place in memory. Three registers rule
Chapter 12: the STACK POINTER (SP), holding the address of
the top of the stack; the FRAME POINTER (FP), holding the
address of a fixed point inside the current frame; and the
INSTRUCTION POINTER (IP), holding the address of the next
instruction to run. A POINTER is an address kept as data —
in a register, or in a place in memory — so that what it
points to can be found again. A saved pointer is still data:
it can be written like any other word; and when the machine
later trusts it as an address, the data becomes direction.
Builds on: memory and address (1.3.102).
# Foundation: the stack
A STACK is a run of memory used in one strict order: what is
put on last is taken off first — last in, first out. Two
moves rule it: PUSH puts a word on top, and POP takes the
top word off. On the machines of Chapter 12 the stack grows
DOWN, toward lower addresses, and its bottom rests at a
fixed address. Programs use the stack to hold the run
itself: each live call owns one FRAME, its run of words on
the stack — the parameters of the call, its local variables, the saved frame pointer of its caller, and the return address
(1.2.74). A call pushes a frame; a return pops it. The stack
is a state machine (1.3.85) whose state is the chain of live
frames.
Builds on: memory and address (1.3.102), register and
pointer (1.3.103), state machine (1.3.85).
# Foundation: buffer and bounds
A BUFFER is a run of memory set aside to hold data of one
kind — most often characters, one after another: a string.
Every buffer has BOUNDS: the first place that is its own,
and the first place past it that is not. A copy into a buffer
is CHECKED when it counts what it writes against the bounds
and stops at the end; it is UNCHECKED when it writes until
the source runs out, wherever that may end. What lies past
a of the buffer end is other data, in the order memory laid it
down; an unchecked copy does not know where the buffer ends,
and neither does the data it carries.
Builds on: memory and address (1.3.102).
Defined word: shellcode
shellcode (a word of the field, taken into this phext by
Study S004, 2026-11-08): a short run of code bytes made to
be carried into a running program as data — set down inside
a buffer, or in the world the system sets around the
program — and then run by turning the program's flow of
execution to the place where the bytes sit. The name is for
what it most often does: start a shell, a program that takes
the orders a person types and runs them.
See 1.2.78.
# Foundation: design token — a named value, held once
A design token is a name given to one value of look — a
color, a space, a corner round, a shadow — so that the
parts read the name and never hold the value themselves.
Change the value in the one place and every part that
reads the name changes with it. A theme (1.2.84) is a
set of design tokens with their values set.
Builds on: variable (1.3.2).
# Foundation: custom property and layer — values a page carries, and the order of sheets
A custom property is a named value set on a page or on
part of it, read by the sheets that style it, and open
to being set again lower down — the page carries the
value, the sheets only name it. A layer is a named rank
among sheets: when two sheets speak to the same thing,
the sheet in the later layer wins. Together they are
how a look can be laid over a system without touching
its parts: set the values again, in a later layer.
Builds on: design token (1.3.107).
# Foundation: module graph — who reads whom
The module graph of a set of code is the web of its
parts and their readings of each other: each part a
point, each 'reads the other to build itself' a line
between the two. To copy a part whole you must walk its
graph and take every part it stands on; to know what a
change touches, you read the graph the other way.
Builds on: graph (1.3.41).
# Foundation: rule checker — a tool that reads code against stated rules
A rule checker is a program whose input is a program:
it holds stated rules — shapes the code must take, or
must not take — and reports every place that breaks
one. The running of such a checker over code is called
lint, and one rule of it is a lint rule (1.3.112). A
rule is only as strong as its tests and its running; a
rule written in a book but not in the tool is advice,
not a check.
Builds on: program (1.3.33).
Defined word: swizzle (also: swizzled)
swizzle (a word of the field, taken into this phext by
Study S005, 2026-11-09): to take a part out of a kit as
source — the part and the inner pieces it needs, copied
into your own project to own and change — while its
calls on the public parts of the kit stay calls on
the kit.
See 1.2.85.
Defined word: lint (also: linting)
lint (a word of the field, taken into this phext by
Study S005, 2026-11-09): the running of a rule checker
(1.3.110) over code, and the tool that does it; a lint
rule is one stated rule the checker holds.
See 1.2.86.
# Foundation: tensor — a number field of many axes
A tensor is a field of numbers laid out on axes: one
axis makes a line of numbers (a vector, 1.3.7), two
axes make a table, three or more make fields no page
can draw, still counted by the length of each axis.
The word names the promise of the shape: every place in
the field is reached by one index per axis, and the
field can be read in an order the axes set down —
the order in memory is part of what a tensor is, and
two tensors with the same numbers in a different
order of memory are different things to a machine
that reads them fast. The work of machine learning
is, at the bottom, work on tensors: the inputs, the
weights (1.3.40), and every field between them.
Builds on: vector and distance (1.3.7).
# Foundation: gradient and autograd — the slope of the loss, found by walking the work back
A gradient is the slope of one number against
another: how much the end number moves when a number
inside the work moves a little. Training (1.3.40)
is steered by the gradient of the loss — the score
of how wrong it is — against every weight: each weight is
nudged against its slope, a small step at a time.
Finding the slopes by hand for a large network is
beyond anyone; autograd (the machine finding of
gradients) finds them by rule. While the work runs
forward, each step is written down; then the steps
are walked back in the opposite order, and the slope
of the end is handed from each step to the steps
that fed it, by the known slope of each kind of
step. The whole of it rests on one fact of the
calculus: the slope of a chain is the product of
the slopes of its links.
Builds on: neural network, weights, training (1.3.40).
# Foundation: training loop — the four moves, made until the loss falls
A training loop is the round a learning machine
turns while it learns, the same four moves in the
same order: (1) the forward pass — the inputs run
through the network and a guess comes out; (2) the
loss — the guess is scored against the known answer,
one number for how wrong it is; (3) the backward
pass — the gradient of the loss is found (1.3.114);
(4) the step — every weight is nudged a little
against its gradient, by a rule for the size of the
nudge. Then the round turns again, over more data,
until the loss stops falling or the data runs out.
Everything a framework exists to do is one of these
four moves or the keeping of them.
Builds on: gradient and autograd (1.3.114); neural
network, weights, training (1.3.40).
# Foundation: kernel, in the machine-learning sense — the small hot piece of work
In machine learning, a kernel is a small, named
piece of number work that runs over tensors — a sum
over a sliding window, a product of two fields, a
turning of scores into shares — small enough to
hold in the head, hot enough to be where the time
goes. Kernels are the units the systems work of the
field is made of: they are counted, timed, joined
together so the memory is touched once instead of
three times, and written again for each kind of
machine that will run them.
This word is not the kernel of 1.3.44: there, a
kernel is the pattern a high-level synthesis tool
works from. The two fields took the same word for
the small thing at the center of the work; the
tracker keeps both, and this scroll marks the
difference.
Builds on: kernel in the synthesis sense (1.3.44);
tensor (1.3.113).
Defined word: immediacy
immediacy (word coined by this phext, charter rule 17,
2026-11-09): the rule that the steps of a taking-in follow
one another with no gap between them — pick, download, read,
take in, write the journal — all in one pass, none of them
left for a later day.
# Foundation: fixed point, and a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism
A FIXED POINT of a map is a point the map sends to itself: it
stands where it stood. A map that can be run both ways,
smoothly, is a diffeomorphism (1.3.16). A HAMILTONIAN
DIFFEOMORPHISM is a diffeomorphism made by a flow: lay a
height function over a symplectic manifold (1.3.22); at each
point, the slope of the height, turned by the area rule,
points a way; let every point ride its way for one whole unit
of time. The map the points land under is a
Hamiltonian diffeomorphism. Such a map can be turned a little
— its flow changed a little — and stay one; the small turns
of 1.2.99 ride on this.
Builds on: maps between spaces (1.3.16); symplectic (1.3.22).
# Foundation: critical point of a function — where the slope rests
A CRITICAL POINT of a smooth function on a manifold is a
point where the slope of the function is zero in every
direction: a top, a bottom, or a pass where the ground rests
before falling either way. Every smooth function on a closed
manifold has some; Crit(M) names the least number any smooth
function on M can be made to have — the fewest resting points
of a slope drawn over M. A critical point is not degenerate
when the slope grows away from it at a nonzero rate in every
direction. A whole submanifold of critical points, the slope
resting along it and growing across it, is a Morse-Bott
locus — the shape the fixed locus of 1.2.99 takes.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: flag, Grassmannian — spaces whose points are subspaces
A SUBSPACE of a space is a smaller space sitting inside it,
flat in the same way. A FLAG is a chain of subspaces, each
inside the next, growing by steps toward the whole. A
GRASSMANNIAN is the space of all subspaces of one fixed size
inside a given space: its points are subspaces, and it is
itself a manifold — a flag of one step. A FLAG VARIETY is
the space of all flags of one fixed shape of chain. A
subspace is LAGRANGIAN when it has half the size of the
whole and the area rule (1.3.22) reads zero on every pair of
its directions; the space of all Lagrangian subspaces is the
Lagrangian Grassmannian.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9); symplectic
(1.3.22).
# Foundation: eigenvalue — the directions a map does not turn
A linear map turns most directions into new ones. A
direction it does not turn at all is special: every point
of that line is sent to a point of the same line, stretched,
held, or flipped by one factor — the EIGENVALUE of the
direction. A map that turns a plane by an angle has no
such direction in that plane; the turn itself, written as
a pair on the unit circle, is what stands in their place.
The whole flat of directions that share one eigenvalue
stands together as one block of the search at 1.2.101:
the block the map holds, the block it flips, and the
blocks it turns.
Builds on: vector and distance (1.3.7).
Defined word: subspace — a space that sits inside a
bigger space, flat in the same way: add two of its points, or
stretch one of its points by a number, and you stay inside it.
A flag (1.3.120) is a chain of subspaces, each inside the next.
Defined word: perturbation — a small turn of a map, or of
the flow that makes the map, made to trade a hard count for an
easy one; the turned map stays a map of its own kind. The bound
of 1.2.99 is spent by a perturbation, and the word names the
spending wherever this phext needs it.
# Foundation: flow, trajectory, and the end of a road
A flow is a rule that carries every starting state of a
system to the state it holds at each later time — the whole
motion of the system, read as one map of time. A trajectory
(one road of the flow) is the path of a single start: the
states it passes, in order, for as long as the rule can
carry it. The maximal interval of a road is the longest run
of time the road exists on; its ends, when they fall at a
finite time, are where the road stops — not by choice, but
because the rule cannot be carried past them inside the
states the system allows. Such a finite end is called a
singularity of the road: the motion breaks down there, and
the map is silent past it unless the road is continued by
some further device. Whether the roads of a flow can end at
finite time at all, and what the state is doing as an end
nears, are questions about the flow — for the segment
problem they are answered whole at 1.2.106.
# Foundation: gravitational potential — the field as a height
A heavy body pulls a particle toward itself, and the pull
is summed from every part of the body: each part pulls
along the line to it, with a strength that falls like the
square of the distance, weighted by the mass of the part. The
potential U(q) holds that sum in one number at each
point q — minus the mass-weighted sum of 1 over the
distance to each part, times the fixed pull constant G. The
pull itself is the slope of the potential, read downhill: the
particle hastens toward lower U. Energy splits along the
same seam: the moving part, half the square of the momentum, plus
U; the sum is kept along every road of the flow, and the
keeping rules the whole of mechanics. A
place where U falls without bound is a singularity of the
field: roads can gain any speed there, and the flow may end
(1.3.124). For a point mass, U falls like 1 over the
distance; for a segment, only like a log (1.2.105).
# Foundation: angular momentum — the kept turn
A particle moving around an axis carries a turning
quantity: how much of its motion goes around, weighted by
its distance from the axis. In coordinates fitted to the
axis — along it, out from it, around it — the quantity is
ℓ = p_φ, the momentum of the around-coordinate, and the
energy holds it in a term ℓ²/2ρ²: the farther out the turn,
the less it costs; the nearer the axis, the more, without bound
as ρ falls to zero. A field that is unchanged by turns
about the axis keeps ℓ fixed along every road of its flow:
turn-symmetry is what is kept — the same bargain symmetry
always strikes, a quantity kept, for a field that does not change
under the turn. The term ℓ²/2ρ² is called the angular-momentum
barrier: a road with ℓ not zero
must pay it to approach the axis, and whether the field can
lend enough to pay is a race between the growth of the barrier
and the depth of the potential — for the segment, the race is
run at 1.2.107.
# Foundation: cone — all the rays through a set
From a point, look at a set: the rays from the point
through every point of the set sweep a cone — every direction
from that point to the set, stretched as far forward as
it goes. A cone is closed under stretching its vectors and
under adding them with weights that are not negative: it holds
directions, not lengths. The cone over a segment, from a
point off its line, is a flat wedge; from a point on the
line, it falls flat to the line itself. A triangle is the
filled shape: a point together with a segment, and every
point between. The cones of this phext do their work where
where the possible directions of a field are gathered into one set and
a road is asked whether its directions stay inside — the
angular cone of the segment problem (1.2.109) is two cones
at once: where the particle may stand, and which ways its
momentum may point.
# Defined word: lemma
A lemma is a small claim, shown true on the way to a
theorem and spent there: strong enough to stand under the
larger claim, small enough to be held whole in the hand. A
paper's theorems are where its roads end; its lemmas are
the steps cut so the roads can be walked. The word belongs
to the field itself, old in mathematics, and it is coined here
because the stream will meet it in paper after paper — a
tracker that reads mathematics needs a word for the steps
as much as for the theorems. First spent at Paper 009
(Chapter 16), whose whole weight stands on one: the log
sandwich, its step 3.2.
# Foundation: chance and probability — a rule for what comes
Some things are not settled in advance: a coin in the
air, the next context a system will meet. PROBABILITY is
the measure of how unsettled a thing is: a number
between 0
and 1 given to each way things could come out — 0 for
never, 1 for certain, and the numbers over all the ways
adding to 1. A DISTRIBUTION is the whole rule at once:
every way things could come out, each with its number.
To say a draw FOLLOWS a distribution is to say the draw
is one pull from that rule — which way comes up is not
known beforehand, but the pull is governed by the rule,
and many pulls show the shape of the rule. The average and
the spread of 1.3.8 are properties of a set of numbers
already in hand; a distribution is the rule standing
behind numbers not yet drawn.
Builds on: average, spread, distribution (1.3.8).
# Foundation: a sample, and independence — draws that do not lean on each other
A SAMPLE is a set of draws from one distribution, kept
as the evidence about the rule behind them. Two draws
are INDEPENDENT when neither leans on the other: knowing
how the first came out changes nothing about the chances
of the second — not its rule, not its tilt. A sample
whose draws are independent and all from the same
distribution is the standard evidence of this phext's
statistics cluster: each draw is a fresh pull from the
same rule. The seam the whole cluster turns on is the
difference between a FRESH draw — independent of
everything already in hand — and a draw already inside
the evidence, leaning on the rest and leaned on by it:
a model fitted on a sample is not independent of that
sample, and judging it there is judging it on ground
that leans. Splitting a sample is the classical repair:
fit on one part, and the other part stands fresh against
the fit — at the price of fitting on less.
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129).
# Foundation: expectation — the average a distribution promises
The EXPECTATION of a quantity under a distribution is
its promised average: each value the quantity can take,
weighed by the chance of that value, and the weighed
values summed. It is the number the plain average of
many independent draws closes on — the average of 1.3.8
is what you count from draws in hand; the expectation is
what the rule promises before the draws are made. Nearly
every claim in the statistics cluster is a claim about
an expectation: a value is an expectation of reward, a
risk is an expectation of loss, and an estimator is
judged by how far its naming stands from the expectation
it names.
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129); a sample
(1.3.130); average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: risk — the loss a rule expects
A LOSS is a number that says how bad an outcome is: the square
of the gap between a guess and the truth is the loss
this phext's statistics cluster spends most. The RISK of
a guessing model is the expectation of its loss — the average
badness the rule promises over fresh draws. TEST RISK is
the same promise read on draws the model never saw
while being made: the risk of the shipped thing, in the
world. A risk always belongs to a model and a
distribution together — change whose draws, or which
weighing of them, and the risk is another number: the
same fitted model carries one risk under the rule that
logged the data and another under the rule it will
serve.
Builds on: expectation (1.3.131).
# Foundation: estimator and consistency — naming a number you cannot see
An ESTIMATOR is a rule that reads a sample and names a
number, meant to stand for a truth the sample does not
show directly — a value, a risk, a parameter of the rule
behind the draws. The own error of an estimator is the gap
between the number it names and the truth, and the gap
is itself unsettled: another sample would name another
number. An estimator is CONSISTENT for its truth when,
as the sample grows, the number it names closes on the
truth — the chance of a gap wider than any fixed width
falls to zero. Consistency UNIFORM over a set of
candidates is the stronger claim the statistics cluster
leans on: the closing happens at every candidate at
once, so the claim still holds when the data itself
picks which candidate gets named. An estimator can be
consistent and still wander on any one sample; the
wander has its own measure, the spread of the naming
over samples — its variance (1.3.8).
Builds on: a sample (1.3.130); expectation (1.3.131).
# Foundation: context, action, reward — the bandit table, and its two rules
The setting the statistics cluster prices: rounds. Each
round, a CONTEXT arrives — what can be seen before
choosing: a user, a patient, a state of play. A rule
chooses an ACTION from a fixed set. A REWARD comes back
— the payoff of that action at that context — and only
of that action: the rewards of the actions not chosen
are never seen. The game is named for the slot machines
of an old picture — many levers, each with a hidden payoff — and
the word bandit stands in the archive for it. Two rules
stand at the table. The LOGGING rule is the one that
played: it made the record, choosing each action with
known chances that may lean on the context or not. The
TARGET rule is the one being priced: the rule whose
choices the record is asked to judge. POSITIVITY is the
ground rule of the table: every action keeps a chance under
the logging rule bounded away from zero — an action the
logging rule never takes leaves no trace in the record,
and no price for a target that takes it can be read from
that record.
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129); a sample
(1.3.130).
# Foundation: the importance weight — pricing a record again by the ratio of two rules
A record made by one rule can be priced again for another by
weighing each round: the IMPORTANCE WEIGHT of a round is
the chance the target rule gives the action that was
taken, at the context of that round, divided by the chance
the logging rule gave it. A round the target rule would
have chosen twice as often counts double; a round it
would never have chosen counts for nothing. Averaging
the weighed rewards prices the target rule — that is the
whole of the weighted way at 1.2.110: the record stands
unchanged, and the weighing turns it into evidence about
a rule that never ran. The price of the trick is
how uneven the weights run: where the target rule leans
far from the
logging rule, a few rounds carry enormous weights, and
the price wanders with them — the spread of the weighed
average is set by how far apart the two rules stand.
Builds on: the bandit table (1.3.134); expectation
(1.3.131).
# Foundation: gradient descent — walking downhill by the slope, one step at a time
A fitted model is a rule with settings — its weights —
and a loss that prices the settings on the data in hand.
GRADIENT DESCENT is the plain way of bettering the
settings: stand at the current weights, read the slope
of the loss there — the direction of its rise, in every
setting at once — and step the other way, by a
step size chosen in advance. Then read the slope again.
The positions of the walk, step by step, are its ITERATES;
the whole list of them is the TRAJECTORY. Early stopping
is the choice of which iterate to ship, and it is a real
choice: the loss on the fitting data falls as the walk
goes, while the risk on fresh data (1.3.132) can turn
and rise — the walk passes the best model it will ever
hold and walks on into worse ones. Which step a walk is
judged at, and by what judge, is the whole question of
the statistics cluster.
Builds on: function (1.3.2); risk (1.3.132).
# Foundation: growing together — when two sizes rise in step
Two ways a body of data can grow. In the classical way,
the sample grows while the thing measured stays the
size it was — more draws of the same few numbers — and
every average comes to rest: errors wash out, and the old
theory's promises hold. In the way the statistics
cluster works, the sample and the dimension grow
TOGETHER: each draw carries more numbers as the count of
draws rises, the ratio of the two holding steady — the
proportional regime. There, the washing-out fails in a
specific way: the many numbers of each draw carry a standing
amount of noise that no growth of the sample removes,
because the noise grows with the draw. Results proved
for the first way — grades that settle, terms that
vanish — cannot be carried into the second way without
being proved again there, and several of them are false
there. The ratio itself becomes a property of every
claim: a risk, a ranking, a variance, each is a function
of the ratio, not a number.
Builds on: a sample (1.3.130); dimension (1.3.9).
# Foundation: the bell law — the Gaussian distribution
One distribution does more work than any other in this
phext's statistics: the BELL LAW (the Gaussian, or
normal, distribution). Its shape is the bell: most draws
near the middle, fewer as you walk away, the falling-off
set by one spread. It is fixed by two numbers — its
middle and its spread — and it has a property no other
law has in the same strength: sums and mixtures of its
draws stay inside the law, so a quantity built from many
small unsettled parts is priced by the bell almost
whatever the parts were. A context drawn from the bell
law in many dimensions is a list of numbers, each
bell-drawn on its own, none leaning on the others: the
round cloud with no features of its own, the statistics
theorems are proved on — and that the cloud is round is
itself load-bearing, which is why the edge of the cluster
stands where it does (Q49).
Builds on: chance and probability (1.3.129); vector
(1.3.7).
# Foundation: an index — many numbers read through a few directions
A context is a list of many numbers; a rule that reads
all of them at once is hard to hold and harder to judge.
An INDEX reads the list through one direction: one fixed
list of weights, the context dotted against it — each
number times its weight, summed — giving ONE number that
stands for the whole list along that direction. A reward
that depends on the context only through one index is a
single-index reward: the list matters only as its shadow
on one line, and the payoff is a fixed curve of that
shadow. A few directions at once give a multi-index
reading — the target rule of the statistics cluster
leans on the context through a few indices, so its
choices are governed by a small shadow of a large list.
The reading loses the rest of the list on purpose: what
stands orthogonal to the directions is invisible to the
rule, and proofs about the rule need only track the
shadow — until the own directions of the walk grow out of the
shadow, which is where the work of the statistics cluster
begins.
Builds on: vector and distance (1.3.7); function (1.3.2).
Defined word: criterion — the measure a judge reads
off a thing at each of its steps: where the measure
stands least, there the judge stops. A criterion is
spent by being read, never by being fitted. First
spent at Paper 010 (Chapter 17), which builds a
corrected criterion that prices a whole walk from the
walk itself.
# Foundation: induction — a rule guessed from examples

Some tasks give the rule; others give only examples of
the rule at work, and ask for the rule itself. INDUCTION
is the second kind of task: from a few cases where input
and output stand side by side, find the rule that turns
each input into its output — and that would turn new
inputs the same way. The examples never name one rule
alone: however many stand shown, a rule that fits them
all and a rule that fits some of them and more besides
can part ways on the next case. Guessing the rule is
therefore a search with a taste for some rules over
others, not a reading-off. Program induction is induction
where the rule found is a program: the guess is a piece
of code, and it can be checked by running it on the
examples that stand shown.
Builds on: program, runtime, state (1.3.33); function,
variable, input, output (1.3.2).
# Foundation: a small language made for one field

A language for writing programs in general carries tools
for every field at once. A SMALL LANGUAGE FOR ONE FIELD
— a domain-specific language — carries only the tools of
one field: a fixed kit of operations, the primitives,
out of which every program in the language is built, and
nothing else. The kit is the field, written as code:
change the kit and the same words build different things.
Programs in such a language can be counted, compared and
searched in ways a general language does not allow,
because the whole space of what can be said is the reach of the kit. The Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus is read in
this tracker through one such kit: 160 operations over
grids and the things in them, the programs written so
each step names its result once and is called by that
name after — the normal form the field calls A-normal.
Builds on: program, runtime, state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: pooling — groups lending each other strength

When a number is wanted for each of many groups — how
often each kind of error is made, say — each group can be
read alone; and then a group with few cases speaks as
loudly as a group with many. POOLING reads the groups
together: one reckoning holds every group's number and a
shared shape over them all, so each group's number is
pulled a little toward the shape of the whole — most
where its own cases are few, least where they are many.
No group rules the reading and no group is thrown away:
the strength of the many makes the few steady. What comes out
is the middle kind of estimate of 1.3.133 — not one
group's count, not that of the whole crowd, but each group's
count made steady by the crowd.
Builds on: a sample, and independence (1.3.130);
expectation (1.3.131); estimator and consistency
(1.3.133).
Defined word: satisficing — taking the rule that
is good enough: the first rule that fits enough
of what stands shown, and not paying to search for the
rule that fits all of it. The word is Simon's, for
choice under a bounded search. First spent at Paper 011
(Chapter 18), where people and machines alike are found
to take rules that fit some of the shown examples, and
stop.
# The epistemic registry — what this phext holds, and how surely

Every claim-bearing scroll of this phext carries exactly one standing, and
this section is the record of that standing, scroll by scroll. The standing
says how the model itself holds the claim — not whether the claim is so in
the world.

TRUE — settled. Either true because we set it so by definition (our
foundations, our defined words, our own measures), or backed by a checking
receipt we can point to: a test run, a count we made, a source fact checked
when it was taken in.

FALSE — found against, and kept. A claim a refutation receipt speaks
against: a recorded clash, a check that failed, a counter-case. False
claims are knowledge. They are never quietly dropped; they are retired
with their receipt and a pointer left behind.

UNKNOWN — written down, not settled. The common standing of a claim a paper
makes: that the paper makes it is a fact of record, but the claim itself is
Unknown until a receipt lands. Open questions, Emise's own reasoned links
(marked [synthesis]), and weighed estimates (the Research Oracle ledger)
are Unknown too.

FICTION — made on purpose, and held as made. Worked examples with invented
beings, scenes built to show a shape. Fiction is never set out as fact,
and it never steps straight to True.

No standing stands bare. Each entry carries a BASIS — the ground of the
standing — and a pointer to its evidence. The basis binds the standing:
by definition (stipulation) and by receipt (verified) may stand True; by
refutation receipt (refuted) stands False; by the say-so of a source
(attributed), by being an open question (open), by being Emise's reasoned
link (synthesis), and by being a weighed estimate (estimate) stand
Unknown; by being made for show (construction) stands Fiction. An entry
whose standing breaks its basis row is a defect by the meaning of the
words — that is what accurate means here, and it is the thing the Lean
check proves.

What the Lean check proves, and what it does not. A small Lean model of
this registry — the standing of each scroll, the bases, the rules that
bind them — is machine-checked: that every scroll of the phext stands in
the registry exactly once, that no standing breaks its basis, that a
standing only ever changes along the allowed steps and only with the
receipt the step names, and that reshaping the phext (merging, splitting,
retiring with a pointer) cannot break any of that. And nothing is pruned
by reshaping: merging, splitting, and retiring move claims and leave pointers,
and a retired claim stays reachable, because all knowledge has a purpose
— for we might be wrong. The check proves the keeping of the record
cannot rot. It does not prove that a receipt-backed True is true in the
world — that rests on the receipts themselves: the kernel checks, the
counts, the test runs, each open to being checked again.

