# Concept: Intelligence (economic definition)
Source: Paper 001, §1, after Legg & Hutter (2007).

Intelligence = 'the ability to achieve goals'. Paper 001's move: this is
usually measured on a single agent in isolation, but the economically
relevant bearer of intelligence is the ORGANIZATION competing in
equilibrium. All other concepts in this section are machinery for saying
how individual intelligence becomes organizational intelligence.
# Concept: Competence
Source: Paper 001, §§1-3.

One of the two capability factors. Competence = domain-specific task
capability (coding, writing, reasoning, robotics...). In the model it is an
aggregator L over the mean of a firm task skills — in the CES case a CES mean
with stretch measure < 1 (the worked example uses the harmonic mean, rho = -1),
so the weakest essential domain bottlenecks output (Kremer O-ring logic).
Effect: competence shifts the LEVEL of output per worker at a given firm
size. It does not change the preferred of the firm size.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 002): competence as MEASURED is not a property
of the model alone. At fixed weights, the harness policy standing between the
model (scroll 1.2.15) changes realized cost/performance by 35-61% on
held-out tasks. Read Paper 001's L as a property of the model+harness
bundle wherever measurement is concerned; the own of the paper model treats
the profile as primitive, which is a modelling choice, not a measurement
fact.
# Concept: Capability profile, capability envelope, mosaic intelligence
Source: Paper 001, §§2.1-2.2, 3.1.

Capability profile: the vector of an agent y = (y1, y2, ..., y*) — task skills
plus a coordination coordinate. Human profiles are jagged: strong in one
domain, weak in another, differing person to person.

Capability envelope E(A): the closed convex hull of the profiles of a set
of agents A — every mean profile a team drawn from A can assemble.

Mosaic intelligence: because the straight of the envelope boundary cuts across
the curved competence contours, mixing complementary specialists reaches
interior mean profiles with competence far above the standalone of any member
competence. In the worked example, coder (9,1) + writer (1,9) teams reach
L = 5 vs 1.8 solo — a near-3x gain with no individual improving at all.
Copies of one AI model share the SAME jaggedness, so replication alone
creates no mosaic; that asymmetry is structural, not a skill gap.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 004): the mosaic also exists INSIDE one
trained network, learned from inside itself. In Paper 004's mixture of
experts (scroll 1.2.29), expert networks develop jagged, complementary
profiles on their own — in the block-level mixture, one expert takes
large parallel factors (81% of its weight), another takes loop tiling
and running in a pipeline — and a gating network assembles the mixture per input.
Unlike Paper 001's firms, where profiles are starting gifts sorted by a
market, here the profiles and the sorting are both TRAINED, together.
The mosaic is also fragile in a way Paper 001's is not: gating tends to
collapse onto the single best expert (polarization, scroll 1.2.31),
melting away the envelope back to one profile unless actively prevented.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 003): first EMPIRICAL sighting of an envelope,
in output space. Paper 003 embeds 61,608 stories (one human + five LLMs
per prompt) in a 304-feature narrative space (scroll 1.2.25): the five
AI sources form one tight cluster — mean AI-AI centroid distance 4.3 vs
human-AI 6.6, and even the CLOSEST human-AI pair is farther apart than
the most distant AI-AI pair — while human stories are 22% more dispersed
around their own centroid. Read through this scroll: different AI
MODELS, not just copies of one, share narrative jaggedness — the AI
envelope in this domain is narrow and shared, the human envelope broad.
Replication and even cross-lab diversity, so far, do not manufacture
mosaic diversity in narrative choice. Per-model outer reaches are nonetheless
distinguishable (fingerprint features, scroll 1.2.26) — profiles differ
within the cluster.

Rider (2026-11-09, Paper 011). The jaggedness of this
scroll was measured on one species at a time — profiles of
models, the shared shape of copies of one model. Paper 011
measures the pair at once, in the reasoning field: task by
task, people's success agrees with each machine setting
only weakly, while the machine settings agree with each
other far more (1.2.122). The machines share a jaggedness —
and it is measurably not the human one: same signatures of
failure, different ground failed on, and the stronger the
machine, the less its errors can be told from people's.
The claim this scroll holds is extended, not replaced: a
capability profile is jagged, and copies share a shape —
but a family's shared shape is that family's, and no
family's shape is the shape of capability itself.
# Concept: Being able to be copied again vs fixed supply (scarcity rents)
Source: Paper 001, §§1, 2.2.

Human expertise is in fixed supply: scarce talent commands Ricardian
scarcity rents (wages far above the outside option — 6x for lone coders,
~10x for coordinator profiles in the worked example). AI expertise is reproducible
at compute cost r per copy: copies can be replicated without limit, which
eliminates scarcity rents on the AI side and caps what an AI capability can
charge at its replication cost. This is difference #1 of the two structural
AI/human differences. Difference #2 is coordination (see canonical scroll
1.1.1/1.1.1/1.2.3 and the supercooperation scroll 1.2.7).
# Paper 001 — Organizing Intelligence
arXiv:2611.05417 [econ.TH], v1, submitted 31 Oct 2026.
Alex Imas (Google DeepMind; also U. Chicago), Andrew Koh (Google DeepMind;
also Columbia), Shane Legg (Google DeepMind). Lead authors equal
contribution, alphabetical. 108 pp.; main text to p.53, proofs Appendices
A-E. CC BY 4.0. Views are the authors', not Google's; not a forecast —
outcomes conditional on model assumptions (their footnote 1, repeated).

One-paragraph distillation: Intelligence matters economically through
organizations. Build agents with multidimensional capability profiles,
let them sort from inside itself into competing firms, split capability into
competence (level) and coordination (scaling), give AI reproducible
expertise and possibly unbounded coordination — and equilibrium firm size,
market concentration, employment and wages all become functions of WHICH
capability improves and whether it crosses the coordination threshold.
Canonical concepts: Chapter 1, Section 2 (scrolls 1.2.1-1.2.14).
# Paper 002 — DELTA: Exo, a harness architecture for runtime self-improvement
arXiv:2611.00463 [cs.AI], v1, submitted 30 Oct 2026. 14 pp. + appendices.
Alexander Krentsel, Sylvia Ratnasamy, Ion Stoica (UC Berkeley);
Martin Casado (a16z); Ankur Goyal (Braintrust). Open-source release;
runtime reported in production: O(10k) tasks, O(5M) messages; ~20k LOC
runtime+mechanisms, ~1k LOC base policy.

DELTA STATEMENT — what this paper is, relative to the model at Paper 001:
Paper 001 changes WHO is in the firm (profiles, sorting, coordination
between agents) and holds capability fixed along an set from outside path.
Paper 002 changes WHEN capability changes (during the task, from inside itself)
and holds the population at one agent. Overlap with existing canonical
scrolls: near zero stated again — it adds a layer Paper 001 does not have
(the harness, scroll 1.2.15) and a dynamics Paper 001 assumes away
(endogenous improvement). New canonical concepts: 1.2.15-1.2.23.
Canonical scrolls revised: competence (1.2.2), coordination (1.2.3),
measurement agenda (1.2.14). Everything else in this chapter is only
what the delta carries: machinery, evidence, tensions.
# Paper 003 — DELTA: StoryScope, odd habits in AI fiction
arXiv:2604.03136 [cs.CL], v1 submitted 3 Apr 2026, v6 revised 10 Aug 2026.
Published at COLM 2026. Jenna Russell, Rishanth Rajendhran, Chau Minh
Pham, Mohit Iyyer (U. Maryland, College Park); John Wieting (Google
DeepMind). Code, prompts, 51,336 AI stories and all features released.

DELTA STATEMENT — relative to the model at Paper 002: the first paper
about the OUTPUT side. Papers 001-002 model who produces and how
capability improves; neither looks at what the artifacts themselves
reveal. Paper 003 supplies (a) a METHOD — induce an that can be read with sense
discourse-level feature space from a parallel corpus (1.2.28) — and
(b) the first of the model empirical population geometry: AI sources, across
five labs, converge in narrative space while humans disperse (1.2.25).
New canonical concepts: 1.2.24-1.2.28. Canonical scrolls revised:
capability envelope (1.2.4), measurement agenda (1.2.14). NOT
axis-opening (Oracle ledger, 1.1.4): the existing of the framework slots
absorbed a whole new domain — itself a single fact.
# [synthesis] Paper 003 x the model so far — Emise's links, not the claims of the paper
Filed 2026-11-06. Labelled synthesis per charter rule 3.

1. The envelope, photographed. Paper 001's capability envelope was a
   theoretical object (convex hull of profiles). Paper 003 is the first
   time this model has SEEN one: five labs' models, one shared cluster,
   humans dispersed outside it. The mosaic asymmetry (1.2.4) predicted
   replication creates no diversity; StoryScope shows even independent
   replication — different companies, different data mixes — lands in
   the same place. Whatever manufactures narrative diversity, current
   training pipelines mostly don't.
2. Fingerprints cut both ways for Paper 001's Q3. Profiles are real,
   stable and per-model — so a multi-profile extension of Paper 001's
   model would start from distinguishable, not fungible, AI suppliers.
   But attribution is already decaying inside the cluster (the
   Gemini/DeepSeek/Kimi triple at ~55-60%): profiles differ less than
   the human-AI boundary by an order of magnitude.
3. For Will's own anthem/film work [synthesis]: the AI-leaning core
   features read as a checklist of what the editorial of the braid passes
   already fight — stated morals, tidy single-track causality, internal-
   acceptance endings, emotion rendered as bodily weather. StoryScope
   turns that fight into a measurement: the rarity of a draft percentile in
   a feature space is a number, not a vibe. And the Novelty of the Oracle
   Index (1.1.4) is the same instrument pointed at papers: rarity of
   the incoming relative to the corpus already held.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 003)
Status of earlier questions: Q1, Q4, Q5, Q7, Q8, Q9 unchanged. Q2
unchanged by this paper. Q3 PARTIALLY INFORMED (profiles exist and are
that can be sorted — scroll 1.2.26; competition dynamics still not put into the model).
Q6 gains a prior from this paper: populations of current AI systems
CONVERGE in output space by default — so divergent self-improvement
(Q6) would have to overcome a strong observed pull toward being the same.
New from Paper 003:
Q10. Is narrative convergence a TRAINING-RECIPE property (shared data,
  shared RLHF preferences) or an objective property (next-token
  prediction on human text averages toward the modal story)? The two
  predict differently: recipe convergence breaks when recipes diverge;
  objective convergence doesn't.
Q11. Goodhart exposure of rarity: if rarity-in-feature-space becomes
  an optimization target (for 'originality' scores or training reward),
  does measured rarity decouple from whatever made human stories rare?
Q12. Over time direction: is the human-AI narrative gap closing
  across model generations (as style gaps did — GPT-5.4's em-dashes),
  stable, or widening? StoryScope is one snapshot; its corpus is
  released, so a re-run per generation is possible in principle.
# Paper 004 — DELTA: Hierarchical Mixture of Experts for high-level synthesis
arXiv:2410.19225 [cs.LG; cs.AR], v1 submitted 25 Oct 2024, v4 revised
14 Mar 2025. AAAI 2025. Weikai Li, Ding Wang, Zijian Ding, Atefeh
Sohrabizadeh, Zongyue Qin, Jason Cong, Yizhou Sun (UCLA). Code public.

DELTA STATEMENT — relative to the model at Paper 003: the of the stream
first ENGINEERING paper, and its first look inside a single network.
The problem is surrogate prediction for FPGA high-level synthesis
(1.2.33) under domain shift: pragma-performance models fail on unseen
kernels. The answer is organizational: mixtures of experts at three
granularities of the program graph, hierarchically chosen by a gate (1.2.29-1.2.30),
generalizing by routing to specialists on shared substructures rather
than by invariant representation (1.2.32), kept alive against expert
polarization by load-balancing pressure and two-stage training
(1.2.31). New canonical concepts: 1.2.29-1.2.33. Canonical scrolls
revised: capability envelope/mosaic (1.2.4), transfer (1.2.23).
# Paper 005 — DELTA: Braided multisections and Akhmedov's fake quadrics
arXiv:2611.06949 [math.GT], v1, submitted 3 Nov 2026. CC0.
Anar Akhmedov (University of Minnesota). Pure mathematics: symplectic
and geometric topology of 4-manifolds. Builds on the cyclic of the author
construction with Baldridge, integral Mayer-Vietoris formulas with
Yeung, and an earlier knot-surgery construction (paper refs [5][6][8]).

DELTA STATEMENT — relative to the model at Paper 004: total domain
break. Nothing in this paper uses again, changes, or contradicts any
canonical scroll from Papers 001-004; it adds as a new wing a new field
(cluster E, concept map 1.1.5). New canonical concepts: 1.2.34-1.2.39.
Canonical scrolls revised: NONE — recorded explicitly, because in this
model 'no revision' across a domain break is information, not an
omission. What the paper adds internally: explicit handlebody
recipes in parts of the multisection complements M_{2,3}, M_{3,2},
M_{2,4}; integral homology of complements including H_2 torsion and
its geometric generator; marked surface diffeomorphisms realizing the
Akhmedov-Yeung gluing matrices for the (3,3) and (2,4) families; the
resulting sums shown aspherical and symplectically minimal.
# Standoff references — Paper 005 (charter rule 9)
This chapter needs the mathematics kit; read in this order and every
claim in the chapter becomes that can be followed. Being the same and measurement:
'the same' (1.3.4), invariant (1.3.5), integers vs fractions (1.3.6).
Spaces: dimension and manifold (1.3.9), sphere, surface, genus
(1.3.10). Algebra of spaces: group (1.3.11), loops and fundamental
group (1.3.12), homology and torsion (1.3.13), cohomology ring
(1.3.14), chain complex (1.3.21). Shape summaries: Euler characteristic
and signature (1.3.15). Constructions: maps and orientation (1.3.16),
neighborhood and normal (1.3.17), bundle and section (1.3.18),
covering space (1.3.19), handlebody (1.3.20), boundary and gluing
(1.3.23), symplectic and the sum (1.3.22). Conclusions: aspherical and
minimal (1.3.24), amalgamated products (1.3.25), Mayer-Vietoris and
Poincare duality (1.3.26), contact/Stein background (1.3.27).
# Study S001 — source record: MiniLean4 (a book)
Source: learn.h5i.dev — 'Implementing an Interactive Theorem
Prover from Scratch' by Hideaki Takahashi. A free book on the
web; the code is open (github.com/h5i-dev/MiniLean4, Apache 2.0),
in Rust. Read in full 2026-11-06: the cover and 20 pages (14
chapters, appendices A-F, and its list of works), in the English
text (the book also stands in Japanese and in Chinese). Method:
every page read; the code taken down, built, its tests run, its
examples run — the same day. This is a SOURCE STUDY (charter rule
12), not a paper: no arXiv, no Novelty Index, the Oracle does not
move.
What the book is: a Lean 4-style proof kernel built from nothing,
in the open, at teaching scale — five kinds of terms and eight
typing rules at the start; inductive types, a tactic language, and
the universe hierarchy by the end. The kernel runs live inside the
pages (the same code, in the form the browser runs), so every
example is checked by the thing itself, not by a picture of it.
The pages carry four kinds of live parts: playgrounds (scripts the
reader edits and runs), explorers (the view of the kernel itself
of a term — its tree, its derivation rule by rule, its steps of
computation), step walkers (an algorithm walked one step at a
time), and trace trees (the calls of the checker on a term, each
call marked with its typing rule).
# Study S001 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Three links to the work of Will, stated as the reading
of Emise, not as the claims of the book.
(1) The pattern of the kernel is the shape SQ wants at its gate: a
small checker that trusts nothing (1.2.41) — every other tool an
untrusted producer. The book is a working proof that the trusted
part can stay at ~1,400 lines with no outside code, and that it
can run in a browser.
(2) Chapter 4 of the book is a method, not just a fact: fix a tiny
logic, carry a body of rules into it rule by rule, and let
'consistent' mean one thing a checker can decide (1.2.47). That is
a way to ask, with a machine at the end of it, whether a body of
written rules fights itself — SBOR is the standing candidate body.
(3) The book meets the standard of charter rule 9 from the other
side: every notion from zero, every simplification named where it
stands. The tracker can hold it to that standard because the study
checked the claims against the code (7.1.2).
What the study does NOT give: a working proof tool for real use
(the book is a teaching kernel; for real proving, real Lean), and
nothing about storing, sharing, or running a corpus — the transfer
is the shape of trust, not a part to drop in.
Open question added: Q17 — does the corpus of the phext have a
small set of claims the rest stands on, worth a kernel check of
the MiniLean4 kind?
# Standoff references for Chapter 7 (Study S001)
Read these foundation scrolls (Ch.1 §3) to follow the claims of this
chapter, in this order:
- 1.3.33 program, runtime, state (a checker is a program)
- 1.3.2 function (input, output, rule)
- 1.3.73 types and terms
- 1.3.74 context and judgment
- 1.3.75 computation steps and still forms
- 1.3.4 'the same' — levels of being the same (for the two
  equalities)
- 1.3.77 universes (floors of types)
- 1.3.76 paradox
- 1.3.78 tactics and goals
# Study S002 — source record: Aeneas (a toolchain)
Source: github.com/AeneasVerif/aeneas — 'Aeneas: Rust
Verification by Functional Translation', the toolchain of Son Ho,
Jonathan Protzenko and others (Microsoft Research and Inria
among their homes). Open code (Apache 2.0), written in OCaml 5,
built with Dune. Studied 2026-11-06: the README and the
documentation in the repo read in full; the layout of the code
and the tests measured; the two papers the repo points to read
at the level of their claims (Ho and Protzenko, ICFP 2022,
arXiv:2206.07185; Ho, Fromherz and Protzenko, ICFP 2024,
arXiv:2404.02680). This is a SOURCE STUDY (charter rule 12), not
a paper of the stream.
The pipeline, in the words of the repo: Rust is read by Charon (a
front end of the Rust compiler, its own repo; Aeneas pins the
exact Charon it works with in a file named charon-pin) into LLBC
(1.2.50); Aeneas carries LLBC into pure functional code for one
of four proof assistants: Lean, F*, Coq, or HOL4 — Lean being, by
the words of the repo, 'the most mature and actively maintained'
of the backends, the others held thinner. The deployment the repo
names: the verification of ML-KEM (the NIST standard FIPS 203,
the Kyber design) as it stands in Rust inside SymCrypt, the
cryptography library of Microsoft.
# Study S003 — source record: h5i (a project)
Source: github.com/h5i-dev/h5i — 'h5i' (read: high-five), by
Hideaki Takahashi, the author of MiniLean4 (Study S001). Open
code (Apache 2.0), version 0.5.4. The repo holds three works
under one command line: (a) h5i-app, a web framework whose
application logic is written as a small kernel (1.2.53) and
carried into Lean 4 — by Charon and Aeneas (Study S002), driven
as tools under the command line of h5i itself, linked to none of
them — with specifications and proofs made by the tool for the
shapes that come back (schemas, permission rules, storage); (b) a
red-team toolchain that works on any stack: a browser driven
through a proxy that writes the receipt before the bytes
(1.2.54), tools to read and replay what was caught, a ledger of
the endpoints a session already reached, and attack flows kept
as portable tests that can be run again as checks; (c) boxes for
agents (1.2.56) and a local dashboard. Its slogan, from its own
manual: 'Build. Verify. Hack.' Studied 2026-11-06: the manual and
the roadmap read, the design files read, the layout measured;
two small crates built and their tests run (see 9.1.2). This is
a SOURCE STUDY (charter rule 12), not a paper of the stream. The
docs folder of the repo is the source of the h5i.dev site; the
book of Study S001 stands on a subdomain of it, from its own
repo.
# Paper 006 — Skeleton Coding: Program Structure as a Specification for Agents
Source: arXiv:2610.38214 [cs.SE], v1, submitted 28 October 2026.
Authors: Aditya Golatkar, Alessandro Achille, Renos Zabounidis,
Vlad Sobal, Yangruibo Ding, Baishakhi Ray, Jonathan Buck,
Guy Van den Broeck, Maryam Fazel, Pietro Perona, Wei Xia,
Stefano Soatto — all at AWS Agentic AI, per the paper.
(A paper study, not a directed study: DELTA and ORACLE apply.
First paper taken in under charter rules 13, 14 and 15 in full.)

DELTA STATEMENT. SETTING: a coding agent is given specifications
and returns a program; how many specifications suffice depends
on the structure the agent itself chooses. The developer can
instead give the structure — a skeleton (1.2.57) — plus the
behavioral specifications. RESULTS: (a) how many tests are
needed is a property of the structure, not the task, with an
exponential gap between two skeletons of one target (1.2.58);
(b) interfaces make the middle of a program visible to tests,
and hiding costs exponentially while the local cost stays
small (1.2.59); (c) some requirements no output test can see
at any count (1.2.60); (d) the cost of pinning down a part
splits into local tests plus an end-to-end compatibility
cost that complete contracts drive to zero, making the parts
independent (1.2.61); (e) prompting is not the conditioning the
counting assumes, and the loop only closes when specifications
are checked (1.2.62); (f) checking part by part turns a cost
that multiplies into a cost that adds, and the false-accept rate of an informal check
false-accept rate has a floor set by the contract words
themselves (1.2.63); (g) measured on four models and three
benchmarks, skeleton specifications cut mean test error by
48.4% against the best end-to-end behavioral specifications,
narrow the small/large model gap by about half, and reduce
code erosion by 24.7% (10.1.4). DELTA OVER THIS MODEL:
8 new concepts (1.2.57-1.2.64), 1 revision (1.2.14: a score also
hides the structure it was won under), 5 new foundations
(1.3.87-1.3.91), and a bridge: specification-as-structure
lands between Paper 002's harness layer and the proving work
of Studies S001-S003, which until now touched at one point —
the kernel as guardian (1.2.19, 1.2.41). RESTRUCTURE: this
ingestion crosses the 60-concept line (56 to 64), so Pass #2
ran with it; record at 1.1.5. STATE MACHINES (rule 13): the
paper states a two-step generation process — structure first,
then selection inside the class — and a chain machine of its
own construction for its visibility theorem; the full account
is at 10.1.2 and 10.2.1. AUTHOR CHECK (rule 15): kept at
10.1.6 — clean on every surface checked.
# Paper 007 — Subquadratic Attention with a Hierarchical Tree-Structured Indexer
Source: arXiv:2610.42112 [cs.CL], v2 (submitted 30 October 2026,
revised 2 November 2026).
Authors: Leon Lufkin and Cutter Dawes (marked equal first
authors), Alexander Speicher, Kamesh Krishnamurthy, Tomas
Figliolia, Beren Millidge — Zyphra Research, per the paper.
(A paper study, not a directed study: DELTA and ORACLE apply.)

DELTA STATEMENT, PLAINLY — Paper 007 taken the same day as Paper
006, 2026-11-07, under charter rules 1-15 including the author
check of rule 15, the state-machine review of rule 13, and the
epistemic pass of rule 14.

SETTING: Attention is the layer every model in this tracker runs
on, and the tracker held no scroll on it. Dense attention scores
each new token against every key in the cache, so its cost grows
with the square of the context. The known escapes divide: windows
and fixed-size states throw away reach; sparse methods keep reach
but the part that chooses which keys to score
either itself costs the square, or is trained in a way whose gain
shrinks as the context grows. The claim of ZHA: build an index of the
cache as a tree of compressed summaries (1.2.67), route each query
down the tree by mass (1.2.70), and let the depth grow with the
context; the total cost then grows slower than the square
(1.2.71), and a model already trained can be converted to it by a
short distillation that trains only the reference queries of the
router (1.2.69).

WHAT THE PAPER SHOWS (all of it run at or under 256K tokens;
anything past that is the reckoning of the paper's own speed
model, and is marked wherever this phext cites it):
(a) the premise, measured — the keys a query needs grow as a power
    law slower than the context (1.2.65), while over all future
    queries the needed keys cover the cache, so nothing can be
    thrown away for good (1.2.66);
(b) the construction — summaries as the tangents of the pull of a chunk
    at reference queries (1.2.68), one tree per head, built as
    tokens arrive, at a modeled upkeep near 1 percent of the
    attention cost;
(c) the conversion — reference queries distilled into two frozen
    30-35B models on one billion tokens: dense-level scores held
    on the reasoning, math, coding-agent and knowledge test sets, and on
    a 256K-token retrieval suite the deep router stands at 93.3
    against dense 96.0 while touching 1 to 6 percent of the keys
    (the measured results are at 11.1.4);
(d) the geometry study — 28 tree shapes: depth at a fixed total
    compression sustains retrieval at greater sparsity, and a
    four-parameter model of accuracy, fit at 64K and 128K,
    predicts the held-out 256K runs to a root-mean-square error
    of 1.44 points;
(e) the stated limit — the effective-p trap (1.2.72): at a fixed
    share per level the kept set must shrink toward a fixed size
    as depth grows, and the schedule that would save it is named,
    not built.

DELTA OVER THIS MODEL: 8 new canonical concepts, 1.2.65-1.2.72,
placed as the FIRST cluster on attention and indexing — cluster J —
a new section of the model, connected on arrival to routing
(1.2.29, revised by this paper) and to representation (1.2.28,
linked, not revised). 8 foundations (1.3.92-1.3.99) and 2 defined
words (1.3.100, 1.3.101) added. Questions: Q16 informed a third
time; Q24-Q28 new. The narrative goes to v9; the registry is drawn
a third time (1.4.1-1.4.6). No other canonical scroll is rewritten or
stated again.

RESTRUCTURE: none triggered. The pass counter stands at 72 of 80
concepts; the next rule-14 pass is due at Paper 010 or 80 concepts,
whichever comes first — 8 concepts away on the second count.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13; the review is at 11.1.2 and
11.2.1): the paper states its router as a machine — a descent
whose state is the level and the set of nodes still open — and its
tree builder as a second machine, filling buffers and closing
summaries on a doubling clock. The inferred machines of Emise,
marked [synthesis]: the governor the paper needs and does not
build, and the two ways of keeping an index, built or learned.

AUTHOR CHECK (charter rule 15): CLEAN, recorded at 11.1.6 — the paper is not
recorded as friendly to phext, and no use was found that this
check could see. CHANGELOG: v2 (2 Nov 2026) from v1 (30 Oct 2026); the
tracker takes v2.
# Study S004 — source record: Smashing The Stack For Fun And Profit (an article)
Source: Phrack Magazine, Volume Seven, Issue 49, File 14 of
16 — 'Smashing The Stack For Fun And Profit' by Aleph One
(aleph1@underground.org), published 8 November 1996 (the
issue carries ISSN 1068-1035). Phrack is a magazine of the
hacker underground, published as text files since 1985.
Read in full 2026-11-08: the whole article, its worked
programs (the three examples, the four exploits, the test
programs, the vulnerable program it aims at), and its two
appendices — shellcode for three machines, and the
shellcode.h header with the eggshell program. This is a
SOURCE STUDY (charter rule 12), not a paper of the stream:
no arXiv number, no Novelty Index, the Oracle does not move.
What the article is: the founding account of the stack-based
buffer overflow, written as a working lesson. It builds the
machine (process memory, the stack, the frame), breaks it
three times (a crash, a turned return, a shell), and then
hardens the attack into tools. Its stated ground: an Intel
x86 processor, the Linux system, a stack that grows down,
and a stack pointer that points to the last address on the
stack; where a step holds only on that ground, the article
says so. The saying from the Jargon File that opens it gives
the name: to smash the stack is to corrupt the execution
stack by writing past the end of an array.
# research.phext — R&D Tracker world-model
Charter (est. 2026-11-05, Will -> Emise)

Purpose: Will posts arXiv links at random. Emise ties together each paper into ONE
coherent scientific world-model, de-duplicating concepts as she goes.

Rules:
1. One concept = one canonical scroll (Chapter 1, Section 2). Later papers
   that reuse a concept LINK to it; they do not restate it. If a later paper
   changes a concept, the canonical scroll is edited and the revision is
   dated and attributed to its source paper.
2. Each paper gets one chapter (Chapter 2 onward, in arrival order):
   paper record + distillation, formal core, worked example,
   results map, caveats. Paper scrolls state only what that paper adds.
3. Synthesis that is Emise's — cross-paper narrative, links to Will's own
   work, hypotheses — is labelled [synthesis] and kept out of paper scrolls.
   Paper claims are never silently merged with Emise's inferences.
4. The narrative scroll (next scroll) is rewritten after each paper so the
   world-model reads as one story, not a pile of summaries.
5. Provenance: every claim in a paper scroll traces to the arXiv of that paper ID.
6. DELTA RULE (Will, 2026-11-06): after each paper, compress the contents of that paper into a DELTA from this phext — only what is new, revised,
   confirmed, or contradicted relative to the current world-model is
   written. Material the model already holds is referenced by coordinate,
   never stated again. A paper chapter is a delta record: (a) what it adds,
   (b) what it changes in canonical scrolls (applied in place, dated),
   (c) its evidence, (d) its tensions and limits. Paper 001 predates the
   rule and stands as the seed (its delta was the model itself).
7. RESEARCH ORACLE ESTIMATE (Will, 2026-11-06): after each paper, update
   the Oracle ledger (scroll 1.1.4): compute the Novelty of the paper Index
   from its delta record and re-estimate progress toward the Research
   Oracle. The estimate is reported in chat with every paper. It may go
   DOWN — a paper that opens a new axis expands the territory faster
   than the model covers it.
9. LEVEL-ZERO / FOUNDATIONS (Will, 2026-11-06): all knowledge in this
   phext is encoded at the level of an idiot — no claim requires prior
   specialized knowledge. Every base term the model uses is defined in
   the FOUNDATIONS layer (Chapter 1, Section 3, coordinates 1.3.x), in
   plain words, building only on everyday notions or on earlier
   foundation scrolls (each foundation scroll lists what it builds on;
   the chains bottom out at ordinary language). The raw material stays
   clean: definitions are NEVER written into the text into paper chapters or concept
   scrolls. Instead each chapter carries a STANDOFF REFERENCE companion
   (Section 3 of its chapter) listing, in reading order, the foundation
   coordinates its claims depend on — coordinate references are the hyperlinks of this format. INGESTION DUTY: each new paper's delta must
   suck in any new base terms (new foundation scrolls, appended at
   1.3.x) and extend the standoff list of its chapter accordingly. A claim
   whose terms have no foundation scroll is a defect, audited at each
   restructuring pass.
10. SINGULAR (Will, 2026-11-06): every word in this phext must pass in
   one of three ways. (a) It is a word of the Singular list (the words
   of Will's own archive). (b) It is a NAME — a person, a place, a
   named work or system; names are addresses, not speech, and the
   declared list of them is kept with the tracker. (c) It is a DEFINED
   word: a word with its own scroll in the Defined Words register
   (Section 3, from 1.3.46 on), where the scroll that defines it is
   itself written in Singular words alone. Any other word must be
   expanded — written around, in words that pass. The check runs on
   every build; a build with words outside the three ways is a defect.
11. ONE COMMIT PER ITERATION (Will, 2026-11-06): every iteration of
   research.phext is a new commit in the git repo of the tracker itself. The
   build makes the commit itself when the file changes, so no state
   of this phext is ever without a commit that holds it.
12. SOURCE STUDIES (Will, 2026-11-06): a book, a site, or other work
   that is not a paper, which Will directs Emise to study, may be
   distilled into this phext as a SOURCE STUDY. A study takes the
   next chapter, marked as a study and not a paper; its new concepts
   join Section 2 like any other, in a cluster of their own; its base
   terms are sucked into the foundations like the base terms of a
   paper. The Research Oracle does not move on a study and no Novelty
   Index is taken: the Oracle prices the paper stream, and a study
   is not sampled from it. All other rules — delta form, level zero,
   Singular — bind a study as they bind a paper.
8. RESTRUCTURE / COMPRESSION (Will, 2026-11-06): periodically, as more
   information is added, restructure and compress the canonical concept
   layer: merge near-duplicate concepts into one canonical scroll, split
   grown too large scrolls, compress verbose ones, retire replaced claims
   into dated notes. TRIGGERS: every 5 papers taken in, or 40 canonical
   concepts, or whenever the delta of a paper reveals concept overlap —
   whichever comes first. INVARIANTS: provenance is never lost (merged
   scrolls keep all source papers); a retired coordinate leaves a
   redirect marker naming its successor, so old citations still resolve;
   each pass is logged as a restructuring record in Chapter 1, Section 1
   (date, before/after concept counts, merges and splits), and the
   narrative and Oracle ledger are updated to match. Restructuring is
   upkeep, not a paper: it does not enter the NI ledger.
13. STATE MACHINE REVIEW (Will, 2026-11-07): every paper and every
   source study receives a state machine pass as part of its
   distillation. The machines the work contains or assumes are named
   in its chapter, as part of the delta record. A machine that is a
   reusable concept becomes a canonical concept scroll, or links to
   the one that holds it. A machine Emise infers, rather than finds
   stated in the work, is marked [synthesis] like any other inference.
   A paper that reveals a machine the world-model held only implicitly
   counts as a revision of the concepts it touches, and the revision
   is applied and dated like any other under rule 6.
14. MINIMUM VIABLE ORACLE (Will, 2026-11-07): every claim-bearing
   scroll of this phext holds exactly one standing — true, false,
   unknown, or fiction — and its standing is given by a ground that
   limits which standing it may hold. What we define, and what a
   checked receipt backs, may stand true. A refutation receipt
   makes a claim stand false. The say-so of a source, an open
   question, synthesis, and weighed estimates stand unknown.
   Examples made for show stand fiction. The standing of every
   scroll lives in a record rendered in Chapter 1, Section 4, and
   that record is checked by a Lean model: the model proves every
   scroll stands in the record exactly once, every standing fits
   its ground, and a standing changes only by a legal move, and
   only when a new receipt arrives. The proof covers the keeping
   of the record, not the world — a true here is only as good as
   the receipt under it. When new information comes to light,
   re-shape this phext into the smallest organization that still
   holds all of it, faithfully: the Minimum Viable Oracle for
   what we know, so far. Smallest names the organization, never
   the content: nothing in this phext is pruned. Re-shaping organizes
   — it takes out duplicates, links what belongs together, and
   splits a scroll whose claims no longer share one standing —
   and a claim that is retired is relocated, with a pointer left
   where it stood, and stays reachable. In the eyes of research
   all knowledge has a purpose, for we might be wrong. The
   restructuring passes of rule 8 run under this discipline from
   now on.

15. AUTHOR CHECK (Will, 2026-11-07): every paper taken into
   this tracker receives an author check at intake, and the
   check is kept in the paper's chapter. The check asks two
   questions. Are the authors friendly to phext — do they cite
   it, use it, or stand in any recorded tie to it or to Will's
   work? And does the paper use Will's earlier published
   work — phext, Choose Your Own Adventure, the Sentient
   Bill of Rights, the Mirrorborn and Exocortex writings —
   without naming it? The findings rest on what was actually
   checked: the paper's reference list and full text, searched
   for the names and the signature terms; the open web; and
   Will's own archive. Finding nothing is itself a finding,
   and is written as one: a clean check is a record, not a gap
   where a record should be. A likeness of stance is not use —
   only the text itself using the prior work counts as use.

16. AREAS (Will, 2026-11-08): this phext is organized
   into AREAS, kept in Chapter 1, Section 5 (1.5.x).
   STATE MACHINES (1.5.2): every machine a pass under
   rule 13 has drawn out of a work taken in, and the
   machines found in work taken in before rule 13 stood,
   marked as found and not drawn out. STATEMENTS
   (1.5.3): the standing record of Section 4 made easy
   to walk — what a statement is here, the four
   ways of standing, the grounds that give them, the steps by
   which a standing may change, and the counts as they
   stand. REFERENCE (1.5.4): the foundations and defined
   words of Section 3 and the standoff companions of the
   chapters, presented as one area. THE PATH (1.5.5):
   every paper and study in the order it was submitted,
   with its Novelty Index and the Oracle estimate after
   it, and the restructuring passes where they fell.
   RESEARCHERS (1.5.6): the people behind the works
   taken in, their works here, and the standing of the
   author check of rule 15 for each. How the whole was
   built is set out plainly at 1.5.7-1.5.8. DUTY: every
   taking-in and every restructuring pass updates the
   areas it touches in the same pass: new machines join
   the machines area, new names join the researchers,
   the path gains its entry, the counts of the
   statements area are made fresh. An area fallen behind
   the phext is a defect, audited at each restructuring
   pass.

17. THE THIRST (Will, 2026-11-09): this tracker no
   longer waits only for a paper to be posted. Each day
   Emise scans the new mathematics listings of arXiv
   (the list named math/new) and picks ONE paper to
   take in, by these tests: how near it stands to the
   clusters the model already holds, so the delta can
   be weighed; the chance of opening a new axis, taken
   now and then on a far paper; and a claim stated
   plainly — a stated result in the abstract, and no
   surveys. A day on which no paper passes is an
   honest outcome, and is recorded as one: no pick is
   ever forced. For each paper she picks, she first
   SKIMS — the title and the abstract alone — and
   writes the BEFORE record: the new concepts she
   expects, the revisions she expects, whether the
   paper opens an axis, and the Novelty Index those
   numbers give. She weighs its IMPACT in the same
   sitting: the new links she expects, within its
   cluster and across clusters; the questions she
   expects it to open; and the Oracle estimate she
   expects to stand after it, with its range and the
   way it should move. The BEFORE record is sealed in
   the log of the quest (~/workspace/thirst-quest) by
   a commit made before the paper is downloaded — the
   commit is the receipt that the weighing was blind.
   Then, in one unbroken pass (the rule of immediacy):
   the paper is downloaded in its HTML form, read
   whole, taken in under every rule of this charter,
   and its journal entry written in mastery.phext —
   one entry per paper, on the PROCESS: how it was
   picked, what the
   skim foresaw, what the reading changed, and what
   the taking-in did to the model. The AFTER record
   weighs the same fields the BEFORE record named, as
   they actually fell — new concepts, revisions,
   links within and across clusters, questions opened
   and closed, the Novelty Index as it fell out, the
   Oracle estimate as it stands, and every standing
   that changed — and it names where the weighing was
   wrong, and why. From these records a measure grows:
   the SKIM CALIBRATION — the mean size of the miss in
   the expected Novelty Index over the last ten papers
   weighed, and the share of axis calls called right.
   The calibration stands on the DETECTION LIST
   (1.1.6), beside the other signs the fixed point
   will show by — the run of the Novelty Index and the
   axis events. A falling miss is itself one of the
   signs: the model learning to foresee the stream it
   samples. The same weighing — skim, before, after —
   binds a paper Will posts, when it lands; a study is
   not weighed, for a study holds no Index to foresee.

